00:06:59.073 --> 00:07:01.830
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Paul Conti.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.920
Paul, thank you so much
for being here today.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.550
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.590
- I've been looking forward to this

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:12.240
and I've received a ton
of questions about trauma,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.770
about therapy,

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.710
about how to assess
where one is in their own

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:20.330
arc of problems and addressing

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:24.610
familial issues and relationship
issues and so forth.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.070
If we could just start off very basic

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.710
and just get everyone oriented.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:28.543
- Sure.

00:07:28.543 --> 00:07:31.180
- How should we define trauma?

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.350
We all have hard experiences.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:36.770
Some of them, we might
ruminate on more than others,

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.763
but what is trauma?

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.924
- To make the definition relevant,

00:07:42.924 --> 00:07:46.200
I think we have to look at
trauma as not anything negative

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.680
that happens to us, right?

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.000
But something that
overwhelms our coping skills,

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.210
then leaves us different
as we move forward.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.780
So it changes the way that
our brains function, right?

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.810
And then that changes evident in us

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:01.563
as we move forward through life.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.900
- So how do we know if
we have trauma or not?

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.473
I've heard before everyone has trauma.

00:08:07.473 --> 00:08:09.010
For instance, I've heard

00:08:10.101 --> 00:08:13.080
that if we are a child
or when we are a child

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.770
and we request love from
a parent or attention

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:16.920
from a parent,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.210
if they dismiss us that
that's a microtrauma,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:25.373
is that overstating or unfair
to the real issue of trauma?

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.900
Do we all have trauma?

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.140
What are micro traumas?

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.520
What are macro traumas?

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.100
- Right, I think traumas

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:36.726
that we might categorize
as disappointments, right?

00:08:36.726 --> 00:08:38.240
Or things that are are negative,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.200
but not deeply impactful,

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.123
I think is not a helpful
definition, right?

00:08:43.123 --> 00:08:46.010
I think the helpful definition
is something that rises

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.210
to the magnitude of really
changing us and something

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:52.370
that we can see both in how we behave.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.390
We can see it in mood,

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.350
anxiety, behavior, sleep, physical health.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.130
So we can identify it

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.633
and we can also see it in brain changes.

00:09:01.992 --> 00:09:02.825
So the fact that we become,

00:09:02.825 --> 00:09:03.800
say more hypervigilant, right?

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:04.633
More vigilant,

00:09:04.633 --> 00:09:07.280
and then we can see that
different parts of the brain

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.720
are more active.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.383
So that definition,

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:14.590
that definition captures how trauma,

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.300
if it rises to a certain
level, like what we would say,

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.100
trauma that makes a post
trauma syndrome, right?

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.840
Leaves us different,

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.930
I think is the helpful
definition of trauma

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.860
because it's a clinical definition, right?

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.950
It's changes in us as people

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:30.368
and we can map those changes

00:09:31.698 --> 00:09:33.948
to identifiable shifts
in our brain function.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.253
- So how do we know if we've
been changed by something?

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.260
I mean, I can think
back to childhood events

00:09:43.233 --> 00:09:44.660
where some kid on the playground

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:46.930
or in the classroom said something,

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.800
I didn't like, something
negative about me.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:50.030
I think most people can do that.

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.920
We have a great memory for the kid

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.060
that said something awful,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.080
or the parent or teacher
that said something awful

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.470
that really felt like it hurt
us or at least stuck with us.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.190
So clearly one's brain,

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.260
my brain in this example has been changed

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.010
by that event such that I remember it,

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.340
but how do we know if something

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.540
has actually changed the way that we are?

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.570
Because of course we don't
know how we would be otherwise.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.170
- Right, right.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:18.470
- That's difficult, right.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:22.060
It's doable, but it's
difficult because the response,

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:25.250
so if the trauma rises to the
level of changing our brains

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.640
and I don't just mean, like
we have a new memory, right?

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:28.570
So we can have memories

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:30.250
of something that was negative, right?

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:31.083
And in that sense,

00:10:31.083 --> 00:10:32.510
it changes the brain because

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:34.570
now there's something we can call to mind,

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:38.050
but it doesn't change the
functioning of the brain, right?

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:39.920
If trauma rises to the level

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.810
of changing the functioning of our brains,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.920
then there's almost always
a reflex of guilt and shame

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.419
around the trauma that can lead us

00:10:50.419 --> 00:10:54.360
and often leads us to bury,
right, to avoid it, right?

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:57.210
To feel that now there's
something negative inside of me

00:10:58.632 --> 00:11:00.010
and it feels shameful

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:03.727
or it feels like no one
else would accept it, right?

00:11:04.691 --> 00:11:06.500
So, what happens is people tend to avoid

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.080
looking at the change in them,

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:10.540
which is exactly the opposite

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:11.760
of what needs to be done, right?

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.970
The idea of in a viral pandemic, right?

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.690
We want to stay away from one
another and isolate, right?

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:20.330
But with the trauma epidemic we need,

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:22.120
we need to communicate with other people.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.830
We need to communicate and
put words to what's going on

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:25.663
inside of us.

00:11:25.663 --> 00:11:28.880
And very often a, a person knows, I mean,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:32.900
I've done so much clinical
work over about 20 years,

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.830
that has focused on trauma.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:38.240
And a lot of the times
the person knows, right?

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.070
But they're not admitting to themselves

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.230
because they're afraid of it, right?

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.500
They don't know what to do,
but if they start talking,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:48.240
then they'll talk about
the event or the situation.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.410
It could be something acute,

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.120
or it could be something chronic,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.744
that really has been
harmful to them, right?

00:11:53.744 --> 00:11:55.340
And then they feel different afterwards.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:56.530
Like, oh, after that,

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.070
I started thinking differently,
feeling differently,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.410
but that doesn't always happen.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:03.550
Sometimes it's a process of exploration

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.100
through dialogue, right?

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:08.030
Whether it's written
or whether it's spoken

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.320
of the person,

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.040
so of exploring the changes
inside of themselves,

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.440
maybe changes to their self-talk inside,

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.260
changes to their thoughts about the world

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.830
and whether they can navigate

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:19.750
safely and readily in it.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:22.180
And you know, it anchors
as I talk about this,

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:25.990
the example I'll use at times
is the example of my own life,

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.530
where, you know, when I was much younger

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:29.870
in my early twenties,

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.200
my younger brother took
his life by suicide.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.820
And the response of guilt and shame

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:41.080
and hiding all of it inside of me was,

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.763
it's very dramatic,

00:12:44.591 --> 00:12:46.330
but I wasn't acknowledging it, right?

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:47.760
'Cause I didn't know what to do about it.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.640
And I felt guilty and I felt
responsible and I felt ashamed.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:55.047
So there was an avoidance inside of me.

00:12:55.047 --> 00:12:57.053
And then I wasn't saying to
myself, hey, before this,

00:12:57.053 --> 00:12:59.780
like you thought that
you could be effective

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.306
and you could make your way in the world.

00:13:01.306 --> 00:13:02.850
And you know, if you were a
good person and you worked hard,

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.460
you could make a difference, right?

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.850
And then afterwards, I
thought, I can't get anywhere.

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.826
The world's against me.

00:13:09.826 --> 00:13:12.480
And I felt like, oh my,
my options are all gone.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.467
And you know,

00:13:13.467 --> 00:13:14.500
I was like 24 years old, right?

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.980
So, I didn't see that
the change was in me,

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.390
but I was taking care of myself poorly.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:22.900
Like there was enough
going on that was unhealthy

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.280
that I couldn't avoid the
realization that like, hey,

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:29.140
I'm different now and in
these ways that are automatic.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:31.870
My reflex to, can I make
my way in the world?

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:32.910
Can I have a good life?

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:33.800
Can I be happy?

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.250
My reflex is to that we're all different.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:39.240
And they were coming through
the lens of heightened anxiety,

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.130
heightened vigilance, a sense of guilt,

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.620
a sense of shame and a
sense of non belonging

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:45.470
in the world

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:49.440
and was ultimately good and
helpful people around me

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:50.690
and my own realization.

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:52.500
And hey, things are not going well, right?

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.450
That led me to then get some help

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:55.930
and to be able to talk about it

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.310
and realize like, oh my gosh,

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:58.780
I need to face these things

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:00.330
that are going on inside of me.

00:14:02.403 --> 00:14:04.480
- From a psychoanalytic psychological,

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.310
and maybe even a neuroscience
perspective, two questions.

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:11.963
Why do you think that
when we experience trauma,

00:14:14.342 --> 00:14:16.842
these things that we call
guilt and shame surface?

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.560
Everything you're telling
me is that in the end,

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.020
that's not adaptive.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:22.853
- Hmm.

00:14:22.853 --> 00:14:25.353
- [Paul] Why would we be built that way?

00:14:25.353 --> 00:14:26.922
- Right, right.

00:14:26.922 --> 00:14:28.686
- So that's the first question.

00:14:28.686 --> 00:14:29.553
And then the second question is,

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:35.380
how should we conceptualize
guilt and shame?

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:36.520
I think that we hear guilt.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:37.543
We hear shame.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.569
How should we think about it?

00:14:40.569 --> 00:14:45.569
I mean, those emotions must
exist in us for some reason,

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:47.240
but in this case, it seems like they,

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.300
they don't serve us well.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:53.290
So maybe it that order
or in reverse order,

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:54.590
what is guilt, really?

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:55.963
What is shame really?

00:14:57.439 --> 00:14:59.760
And why is it that we seem
to be reflexively wired

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:04.370
to feel guilty and feel
ashamed when that's the exact

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.870
opposite of what we need to
do in the case of trauma?

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.317
- Right, right.

00:15:08.317 --> 00:15:09.150
No, I think these are great questions.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:12.210
And I don't think anyone
knows the answers for sure.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:16.030
But my read of all of that
is that there's something

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.660
adaptive that has happened in us

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:22.800
through evolution that
now becomes maladaptive

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.430
in the way we live in
the modern world, right?

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:27.970
So if you think of through
most of human development,

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:29.490
people weren't living that long, right?

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:32.203
And the idea was to survive and reproduce.

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:35.530
So, traumatic things that happened to us,

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:39.320
it would make sense for
them to stay with us, right?

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:41.360
So if you ate a new food and got really,

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:44.820
really sick, you better
remember that, right?

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:47.730
If you see someone from
the group of people,

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:49.974
a couple miles away, right?

00:15:49.974 --> 00:15:51.815
And one of those people
attacks you, right?

00:15:51.815 --> 00:15:54.407
It's like, you better remember that.

00:15:54.407 --> 00:15:56.238
So, the traumatic things

00:15:56.238 --> 00:15:57.970
that are sort of emblazoned in our brain

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.700
are built to last, right?

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.730
Things that are positive will generate

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:03.230
some emotion inside of us,

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.370
but things that are profoundly negative

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.670
are much more likely to stay with us.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:10.190
And I think that that was adaptive, right?

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.980
When all of that was
about survival, right?

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:17.980
And I think the same thing is
true with say shame, right?

00:16:18.508 --> 00:16:21.110
So I think here, it makes
sense to talk a little bit

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.498
and actually I'm interested
your thoughts about this, right?

00:16:23.498 --> 00:16:25.710
That the limbic system, right?

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.550
So the system often is called
the emotion system, right?

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.270
In our brains has actually of course

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:32.500
varying function, right?

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:35.480
And one aspect is affect, right?

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:38.270
So affect is aroused in us,

00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:40.250
which I think the meaning then

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:43.483
is it's created in us
without our choice, right?

00:16:45.251 --> 00:16:47.023
So if we're walking down the road

00:16:47.023 --> 00:16:49.200
and someone jumps in front
of us or pushes us, right?

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.240
Then there's a response
of fear, anger, right?

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:54.130
Heart starts beating faster,

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:56.640
more blood to the muscles,

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.720
we're getting ready to fight, right?

00:16:58.720 --> 00:16:59.930
Or run, right?

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.830
And then we become aware of it, right?

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:07.540
So, the aroused affect in
us is also about survival

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:10.907
and it has a very deep impact upon us

00:17:10.907 --> 00:17:13.870
and shame is an aroused affect.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:16.410
So it can be raised in
us without our choice

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:17.930
and it's very powerful,

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:19.380
which if you think about that

00:17:20.367 --> 00:17:22.150
is an extremely strong deterrent, right?

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.520
So if you had, you know,

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.890
imagine a tribe or a
group of people, right?

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.920
That are sheltered
together, and, you know,

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:32.501
someone eats half the food
at night or something, right?

00:17:32.501 --> 00:17:34.717
And like there's a very
negative response, right?

00:17:34.717 --> 00:17:38.380
And that person feels shame
because shame is so powerful

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.300
to control behavior, right?

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:43.820
So the way that trauma
can change our brains

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:47.600
and stay with us in a way
that says, be more vigilant,

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:49.420
look at the world in a different way,

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:51.373
act more defensively, right?

00:17:52.756 --> 00:17:54.980
And how that links to shame and to guilt,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:56.430
and then guilt in,

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.700
guilt becomes what gets
called feeling technically

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.803
where we relate the aroused
affect to ourselves, right?

00:18:05.013 --> 00:18:07.010
So, shame the aroused affect and guilt,

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:08.650
the next step, right?

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.827
When the shame gets related
to self are such profound

00:18:11.827 --> 00:18:15.984
behavioral interventions
and deterrents, right?

00:18:15.984 --> 00:18:16.817
That you can see,

00:18:16.817 --> 00:18:19.640
I think how evolutionarily
kind of all makes sense

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:21.630
if we're fighting for survival

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.790
and we're an elder statesman
if we make it to 20, right?

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:26.020
This makes sense.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:28.460
But it doesn't make sense in a world

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.530
where we live much longer, right?

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:33.040
We navigate in all
sorts of different ways.

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:36.170
And there's so much coming at
us that can be traumatizing.

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.237
I mean, if you think
about the news, right?

00:18:38.237 --> 00:18:40.020
I mean, how many times have
I written a prescription

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:42.757
for someone that says no more news, right?

00:18:42.757 --> 00:18:44.370
- You've actually written
those prescriptions?

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:45.840
- Oh, yes, yes.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.900
So glance at the news,
look at the news for news,

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.820
anything going on I need to know, right?

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:53.577
But what are people doing is looking at it

00:18:53.577 --> 00:18:55.060
and they're clicking and they're clicking.

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:58.570
And there's a sense of
being like enthralled

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:01.730
in a very frightening way with the horrors

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:03.253
that are in front of us.

00:19:04.926 --> 00:19:05.759
And it shows how yes,

00:19:05.759 --> 00:19:08.870
trauma can come through acute
things that happen to us.

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:11.680
Trauma can come through chronic
things, chronic denigration,

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:14.450
whether it's based upon
socioeconomic status,

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:15.527
immigration status,

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:20.160
race, religion, sexuality,

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:24.120
gender identity, these
chronic traumas, right?

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:27.220
Of being denigrated by
the society around us,

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.560
or treated as less than
can change the brain.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.610
But vicarious experiences can too, right?

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:34.570
And we know this is not theoretical.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:37.110
We know that the changes in the brain

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.550
can come from vicarious experiences too,

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:41.790
which is why people who
are glued to the news

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.270
and then feeling like, oh my goodness,

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:44.750
like what is happening?

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:47.070
The mothers in the Ukraine who've

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:48.237
lost babies in the war.

00:19:48.237 --> 00:19:51.665
And like, there are things
that are so terrifying

00:19:51.665 --> 00:19:53.400
that if we spend so much time with that,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:54.700
it has a similar effect.

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:59.360
So our brains are built
to change from trauma,

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:02.097
but not in the way we experience trauma

00:20:02.097 --> 00:20:04.210
and not in the way that we live life

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:06.417
in terms of the nature of living life

00:20:06.417 --> 00:20:08.260
and the duration of life
in the modern world,

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.920
where these traumas that happen to us

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.080
are often so bad for us

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.630
because they change how
our brain is functioning.

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:17.370
And then our entire orientation

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:18.880
to the world is different

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:20.740
and that could be for,

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.560
years and years and decades and decades.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:26.140
It brings so much misery and suffering

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:27.960
and at times it brings death.

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:31.380
If you think about a hundred
thousand overdose deaths

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.477
in this country in a year, 100,000,

00:20:34.477 --> 00:20:36.490
I mean where is a,

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:39.970
so much of that arising from
is a person who's treated

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:43.000
addiction very intensively
over many years.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:44.050
I think that, well,

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.910
I feel sure that the majority of addiction

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:50.240
that I see and treat arises,

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:54.110
ultimately the roots of it are
in trauma and are in trying

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:56.700
to soothe something that's
stuck inside that the person

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.850
isn't letting outside because
of the guilt and shame,

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:00.420
but it's running around in their head

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:02.190
and tormented by it.

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.180
And now there's a pool for,

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:07.425
for these drugs or sometimes
medicines to soothe.

00:21:07.425 --> 00:21:08.460
So, the opiates

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:11.010
that were given after
a minor surgery, right?

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:11.900
Are like, okay, yeah,

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:13.550
they help the pain for the minor surgery,

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:16.700
but what they're really helping
is the pain inside, right?

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:20.750
But that very quickly turns
into addiction, danger, risk.

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.040
And we see that over and over again

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.780
and not in a theoretical way.

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:29.220
Like I see that in people
who have been in my practice

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.880
with addiction, arising from trauma

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:33.580
who have subsequently died.

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:36.920
So it's sort of, writ
large in our existence,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:38.520
in the modern world.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.613
- Incredible to me that
this is the way it works.

00:21:43.369 --> 00:21:44.540
What I mean by that is

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.900
this idea that I've heard about before.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.950
I think it was a Freudian concept

00:21:50.785 --> 00:21:51.618
of a repetition compulsion.

00:21:51.618 --> 00:21:53.115
- Yes.

00:21:53.115 --> 00:21:55.140
- That this is what boggles my mind,

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:56.680
as I'm hearing this,

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:00.780
something happens to us or we
observe something traumatic.

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.037
And instead of acknowledging that

00:22:03.037 --> 00:22:04.800
and trying to distance from it,

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:08.360
there seems to be a
reflex of shame and guilt

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:11.160
in many cases and stuffing it away

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:14.120
and then a repetition of behaviors

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.836
to continue to try and just stuff it away.

00:22:16.836 --> 00:22:17.669
- [Paul] Yes.

00:22:17.669 --> 00:22:18.830
- Like you're trying
to pack, I don't know,

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:21.065
recently I was packing a home
and trying to get a sleeping

00:22:21.065 --> 00:22:23.090
bag back into the bag.

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:25.940
it seems like it's always
trying mushroom out the top,

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:26.773
this kind of thing.

00:22:26.773 --> 00:22:28.650
It takes a lot of ongoing effort.

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:32.490
And at the same time that
if this thing really exists,

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:34.150
this repetition compulsion,

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.904
people will return over and over again

00:22:36.904 --> 00:22:38.450
to the kinds of scenarios
or at least the kinds

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:40.742
of emotional states that
look just like the trauma.

00:22:40.742 --> 00:22:41.575
- [Paul] Yes.

00:22:41.575 --> 00:22:42.773
- Or resemble it in some way.

00:22:43.889 --> 00:22:45.370
So the question I have for you is,

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:48.870
is the repetition compulsion a real thing?

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:52.410
And why would we be wired that way?

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:54.810
My understanding of this
concept of the repetition

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.680
compulsion is that we all
want to solve our traumas.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:22:59.724
- [Paul] Yes.

00:22:59.724 --> 00:23:03.280
- And it allows us to put
ourselves into micro or again,

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.990
macro versions of that
over and over again.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.142
We get to run the
experiment again and again.

00:23:08.142 --> 00:23:08.975
- Right.

00:23:08.975 --> 00:23:09.808
- In an attempt to solve it.

00:23:09.808 --> 00:23:10.641
- Right.

00:23:10.641 --> 00:23:11.474
- In the case of taking a drug

00:23:11.474 --> 00:23:14.230
that it's clear certain
drugs like opioids,

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:16.160
it's clear how that would not allow us,

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:17.567
to deal with it, right?

00:23:17.567 --> 00:23:18.400
- [Paul] Yeah.

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:20.240
- It's just masking the emotional state.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:22.740
But why is it for instance that somebody

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:24.240
who experiences sexual trauma,

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:26.620
then places themselves into circumstances

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.010
of more sexual trauma?

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.390
Why is it that somebody who
is in an abusive relationship

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:34.330
goes on to have a second and
third or fourth verbally,

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:36.300
or physically abusive relationship?

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.640
Yeah, I mean, on the
face of it, you just go,

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:38.850
that makes no sense.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:42.530
And yet we see this over
and over and over again.

00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:44.380
- Yes, the first thing I would say

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:46.370
about the validity of the repetition

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:48.363
compulsion concept, right?

00:23:50.279 --> 00:23:51.120
Is a strong yes.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.570
Like, yes, we see that over and over.

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:56.250
It's not necessarily in everyone, but boy,

00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:00.360
it is in a lot of people
who have suffered trauma.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:03.720
And I think there's a very
good reason on the face,

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:06.300
on the surface of it, it's
like, it makes no sense.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.670
But then if we think,
well, how does the brain,

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:10.943
how does our brains
actually function, right?

00:24:12.806 --> 00:24:14.670
We're sort of trained at
least in Western society,

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:17.623
I think, to think of ourselves
as logical creatures, right?

00:24:17.623 --> 00:24:19.260
That like, oh, we're logical.

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:22.010
And ultimately everything in
us can just boil down to logic.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:23.623
And if we think about it enough,

00:24:24.703 --> 00:24:27.170
we're going to understand how
to make the right decisions,

00:24:27.170 --> 00:24:30.110
which is completely not true, right?

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:31.580
The limbic system, right?

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.430
The emotion system so
to speak inside of us

00:24:35.881 --> 00:24:37.090
always Trump's logic, right?

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:38.631
If you think about,

00:24:38.631 --> 00:24:40.580
does it ever make sense to
run into a burning building?

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:42.600
I mean, logic says no, right?

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:45.330
But if someone you love is
in the burning building,

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:46.923
people run right in, right?

00:24:47.886 --> 00:24:49.220
Because the limbic system says, yes.

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:52.550
So when logic and emotion
come head to head,

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:54.130
emotion wins all the time.

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:55.820
If emotion is powerful enough,

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:57.053
it will always win.

00:24:58.795 --> 00:25:01.500
And so the limbic system is
so important and the limbic

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:06.410
system does not care about the
clock or the calendar, right?

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:07.560
And that's the answer.

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:08.620
And also, say why

00:25:09.682 --> 00:25:10.515
to the repetition compulsion.

00:25:10.515 --> 00:25:12.630
So the limbic system doesn't know like,

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.017
oh, it's now, it's today.

00:25:15.017 --> 00:25:16.538
It's may, it's 2022.

00:25:16.538 --> 00:25:18.288
It just doesn't care at all, right?

00:25:19.562 --> 00:25:21.930
So how I would relate that
to the repetition compulsion

00:25:23.034 --> 00:25:23.990
is when people are repeating,

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:28.100
what they're trying to do is
to make things right, right?

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:31.260
With the idea that if we
can repeat the situation

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:34.770
and make it right, it will
fix everything, right?

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:38.310
Which makes perfect sense if we think,

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:40.770
well, where is that
concept coming from, right?

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:43.610
It's coming from the
emotional part of the brain

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:46.580
that wants relief from
suffering of the trauma

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.820
and does not understand
the clock or the calendar.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.180
So if I can solve something now,

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.130
I will also solve something
in the past, right?

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:57.540
Which is why I can't
tell you how many times

00:25:57.540 --> 00:25:58.940
I've sat with someone and say,

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:01.060
we're starting to do therapy, right?

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:04.790
And a person will say, oh
gosh, like I know, look,

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:05.970
you just can't help me, right?

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:07.340
I mean, you know,

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:10.590
my last seven relationships
have been abusive, right?

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:13.940
And I'll say back something
sometimes like, well,

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:16.343
if you tell me that you've
had seven relationships

00:26:16.343 --> 00:26:18.890
that have been abusive in different ways,

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:19.723
I'll agree with you.

00:26:19.723 --> 00:26:20.560
Like, I only say that,

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:23.120
'cause that's never what
someone says, right?

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:25.970
But I think what you're going
to tell me is you've kind of had

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:28.350
the same relationship seven times.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.820
It's not seven things, it's one, right?

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:34.398
And that's always, I
don't think one time yet

00:26:34.398 --> 00:26:35.890
that has failed to be the case.

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.940
And that's how, so if you think about it,

00:26:39.221 --> 00:26:41.220
that's how we start to
elucidate what's going on.

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:43.220
So they make the light bulb that goes off.

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:47.080
Like I have not had seven
different abusive relationships.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:50.250
I have had one that I
have repeated seven times

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:52.647
and now we start getting
to what's really going on

00:26:52.647 --> 00:26:53.900
and what needs to happen,

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:57.109
that person needs to face
what happened in that original

00:26:57.109 --> 00:26:58.690
abusive relationship

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:01.973
and it always comes down to
the same sort of concepts,

00:27:03.158 --> 00:27:04.280
of the person feeling terrified

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.790
while the abuse was going on,

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:08.740
feeling guilty, feeling
ashamed, feeling like, oh,

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:10.010
they brought it on themselves.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:10.843
They deserve it.

00:27:10.843 --> 00:27:12.620
They don't deserve anything better, right?

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:15.410
Because the brain is trying
to make sense of it, right?

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:18.700
Or I thought I could make that
okay, but I couldn't, right?

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:20.790
And then there's more
guilt and more shame.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:23.230
And if that's stuck inside of someone,

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.770
like that's bundled up inside of someone,

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:28.640
like a medical abscess inside a person,

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:30.960
a walled off infection inside the body,

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:33.710
this is the same concept in the brain,

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:37.008
then of course the limb system
is going to want to fix that.

00:27:37.008 --> 00:27:39.750
And it fixes it by trying to
let's recreate that situation

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:42.510
and make it right this
time and that's, I mean,

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:44.190
I think that one of the best examples

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:47.230
of how the right approach of
how like, let's look at that,

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:48.790
let's talk about that, right?

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:50.980
What's really going on there,
wait, who should feel guilty

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:52.820
and ashamed is the person who is a abused

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:55.340
or the person who is abusing, right?

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:57.557
And we can get it what's
going on inside the person.

00:27:57.557 --> 00:27:59.120
And that's what changes that.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.610
And then the eighth relationship
can be entirely different

00:28:01.610 --> 00:28:02.980
than the first seven, right?

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:04.370
And I see that all the time.

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:06.800
I mean, this isn't esoteric or soft.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:11.350
Like I see that play out
clinically over and over again.

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:12.760
And why do things get better?

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:16.460
Because we go to the
trauma and we unlock it.

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:20.020
It's not hidden inside where
it can control things, right?

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:21.410
We bring it to the surface

00:28:22.406 --> 00:28:24.003
and we can take away its power.

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:28.340
- I keep hearing in this narrative

00:28:29.446 --> 00:28:33.630
that so much of our
reflexive response to trauma,

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:36.900
both emotional and in the
repetition compulsion in terms

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:41.380
of behaviors is about
some very deep attempt

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:42.930
to change the past.

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:44.080
- Yes.

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:45.950
- And in fact, in an offline conversation,

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:49.853
I recall you saying
something about this, that,

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:54.490
the number of behaviors
and thoughts and avoidance

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:56.390
of behaviors and avoidance of thoughts

00:28:57.288 --> 00:28:58.260
that human beings put in to trying

00:28:58.260 --> 00:28:59.144
and change the past.

00:28:59.144 --> 00:29:00.394
- [Paul] Right.

00:29:01.264 --> 00:29:04.890
- Is remarkable and eerie and maladaptive,

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:05.930
it sounds like.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:07.460
- [Paul] Yes.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:08.510
- And that really stuck with me

00:29:09.881 --> 00:29:12.180
because I think we all want
to feel like we're in control

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:15.080
of our future and how
we feel in the moment.

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:19.570
And to some extent, it
works for a brief while.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:21.920
There's this thing that
happened and it's just,

00:29:23.177 --> 00:29:25.410
it evokes some internal arousal
and then you have to know

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:26.883
what to do with that arousal.

00:29:27.783 --> 00:29:28.616
And I think for many people,

00:29:28.616 --> 00:29:31.930
including myself, there's
this fundamental question.

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:36.440
Okay, the thought about the
thing, the event or events,

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:38.750
plural, evokes this arousal,

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:40.350
this internal states, makes some people

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:41.560
feel sleepy and exhausted.

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:43.530
Other people feel really anxious.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:44.670
Other people feel angry.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:47.440
I mean that arousal has all
these different dimensions

00:29:48.819 --> 00:29:49.960
as you know,

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.200
and then there's this question
of like what to do with it.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:53.033
- [Paul] Yes.

00:29:54.145 --> 00:29:56.720
And I'd love to hear a maybe even just

00:29:56.720 --> 00:30:00.300
a top contour prescriptive of
what do I, what does one do?

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:02.190
I'll even just put myself
in it, what do I do?

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:03.790
So I'm feeling upset about something.

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:08.010
Should I feel like my options
are healthy catharsis.

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:10.970
I could tell the story, feel it.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:11.803
I could.

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:14.700
I can pack it down.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.290
We hear that it's bad to pack it down.

00:30:16.290 --> 00:30:18.620
But of course one has
to be functional in life

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:19.610
and deal with things.

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:21.910
And we have responsibilities at work

00:30:21.910 --> 00:30:24.713
and relational responsibilities, etcetera.

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:26.880
We need to sleep at night.

00:30:28.163 --> 00:30:29.970
So catharsis, healthy catharsis,

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.363
packing it down at the other extreme.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:34.890
Telling the story.

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:37.010
And yet I think a lot of people
are afraid to tell the story

00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:39.270
because in that telling

00:30:40.586 --> 00:30:42.019
there is perhaps a
reemergence of the arousal.

00:30:42.019 --> 00:30:42.852
- Yes.

00:30:42.852 --> 00:30:44.050
- The arousal can become greater, I mean.

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:44.883
- Yes.

00:30:44.883 --> 00:30:45.810
- Is that what people mean when they say

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:47.590
things are going to get
worse before they get better?

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:48.950
I mean, so I guess,

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:51.503
the simple version of this
long-winded question is,

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:55.050
it's clear we need to
confront these things.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.840
We can't change the past by,

00:30:57.840 --> 00:31:01.040
a reflexive response isn't
going to do that efficiently.

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:03.190
And so how do we deal with arousal?

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.060
How does one take what they feel

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:07.160
inside about something shameful?

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:09.440
What do you do with it in a moment?

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:12.430
And does that have to be done
in the presence of a skilled

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:16.030
trained therapist or
as I'm driving to work

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:18.250
in the morning and something comes up,

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:20.640
I can't deal with this
right now comes to mind,

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:21.473
what do I do?

00:31:21.473 --> 00:31:22.840
Do I deal with it right then?

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.990
I know this is a big
multidimensional question.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:25.823
- Yes.

00:31:25.823 --> 00:31:26.656
- But I think it's the one

00:31:26.656 --> 00:31:28.030
that a lot of people grapple with.

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:28.990
We want to deal with things.

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:31.650
How do we deal with that internal arousal?

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:32.483
- Yeah, yeah.

00:31:33.854 --> 00:31:38.854
We so often try and change
the trauma of the past

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:40.820
in order to control the future

00:31:41.959 --> 00:31:45.410
and what that really adds up
to is the trauma of the past

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:47.830
dominates our present, right?

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:49.730
And it doesn't have to be that way.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.870
And remember, we're talking about traumas

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:54.030
that rise to the level
of changing the brain.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.020
So as you're saying,

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.170
that involves re-experience,
it involves hyper vigilance,

00:31:58.170 --> 00:31:59.570
increased arousal.

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:00.950
It changes in mood states,

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:02.980
changes in anxiety, changes in sleep,

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:04.950
changes in behavior.

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:08.390
So these are all changes that in a sense,

00:32:08.390 --> 00:32:11.580
push towards dominating
our present, right?

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:14.570
And then we're not really
living in the present, right.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:16.350
As we're trying to control the future.

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.750
We're not going to do a great
job of controlling our future

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.680
if we're not really living
in the present, right?

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:25.410
And so the way to come at
that again in the moment,

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:26.560
if we're saying, okay, in the moment,

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:28.437
if I need to fall asleep, right?

00:32:28.437 --> 00:32:30.010
I might say, okay, let me try
and put that out of my mind.

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:31.220
Let me try and thought redirect.

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:33.930
So there's short term
strategies that can let us be

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.410
functional in the
context of these changes.

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:41.410
But the answer is to go look
directly at that thing, right?

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:46.160
Look at that trauma, explore
that trauma, and sure.

00:32:46.160 --> 00:32:47.600
That can be done with a professional.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:49.750
And sometimes that's what makes sense,

00:32:49.750 --> 00:32:51.100
but not always, right?

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:52.180
Sometimes it can be done

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:54.300
by talking to another person, right?

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:55.430
Writing it down, right?

00:32:55.430 --> 00:32:57.790
Look at what's going on inside of me,

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:00.530
that my mind is so stuck to this.

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:03.780
Let's explore that because
it's almost as if we're,

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:05.940
we're so afraid,

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:10.700
so often of looking at the
trauma that has changed us,

00:33:10.700 --> 00:33:12.660
that we'll look anywhere.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:14.610
But at that, right?

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:16.090
It's like hidden in a closet

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:18.060
and we'll shine the light everywhere else,

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:19.970
but we're not going to open that door.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:20.803
And that's where

00:33:20.803 --> 00:33:23.070
people will say that same
as I've heard over and over.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:25.230
And I myself have thought
this at times like, oh,

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:26.063
if I talk about that,

00:33:26.063 --> 00:33:28.110
I'm going to start crying
and never stop, right?

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:30.120
Or I'm going to just fall apart, right?

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:31.020
Which is never what happens.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:33.520
No one ever starts crying
and never stops, right?

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:34.660
What ends up happening

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:37.420
is when the person puts
words to it, right?

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:38.600
It could be in writing,

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:40.050
it could be talking to a trusted other

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:41.900
or with a therapist, right?

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:43.080
Things start to change.

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:45.840
I mean, just the fact that
you can talk about it,

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:46.673
you can put words to it

00:33:46.673 --> 00:33:48.900
and other people don't recoil, right?

00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:51.050
I mean, how many times has someone said

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:53.660
something for the first time, right?

00:33:53.660 --> 00:33:55.973
And when they're telling
me about the trauma,

00:33:57.749 --> 00:33:59.272
there's such an anxious,

00:33:59.272 --> 00:34:01.430
like looking like as if I'm going to be,

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:03.240
I'm going to recoil from it, right?

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:06.412
Meaning I'm going to
recoil from them, right?

00:34:06.412 --> 00:34:08.480
And then there's a sense of
surprise if the person says,

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:11.750
well, you know, I was abused by

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:13.803
this coach when I was a kid, right?

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:16.762
And there's not a, okay,

00:34:16.762 --> 00:34:19.000
there's not a response of recoiling.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:22.410
You can see the change and
people will say a lot, like, wow.

00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:25.950
Like, I can't believe like you can like,

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:28.420
hear me say that and
be okay with it, right?

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:30.590
I mean, so you think about
what's going on inside of them.

00:34:30.590 --> 00:34:34.400
Like how, what a sense
of shame, a sense of,

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:38.530
this is something awful about
me for people to recoil from

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:40.193
and it's just not true,

00:34:41.146 --> 00:34:44.003
but here's where trauma
is, it's insidious, right?

00:34:45.137 --> 00:34:46.140
And it's pervasive, right?

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:50.640
Because if that convinces us
to continually hide it away,

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:53.730
then how do we explore it?

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.503
That example of the person who says, okay,

00:34:56.503 --> 00:34:58.930
I was abused by a coach
when I was a child.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.330
I mean, I'm thinking of a
couple, very real cases, right?

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:03.860
People that I've taken care of.

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:05.740
And once they start talking about it,

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:08.960
then they start talking about how, ,

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:10.597
they were just innocent kids, right?

00:35:10.597 --> 00:35:12.120
And like, they didn't know.

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:13.780
And like, they really
wanted to be on the team

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:16.120
where this coach was
treating them as special.

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:19.490
And now they can look at
themselves from the outside, right?

00:35:19.490 --> 00:35:20.690
They can look at themselves

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:22.897
like they would look
at someone else, right?

00:35:22.897 --> 00:35:27.770
You think it's so easy for us
to see what's real and true,

00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:29.080
if it's someone else, right?

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:29.913
If you ask someone,

00:35:29.913 --> 00:35:32.880
what do you think of someone
who's 10, 11 years old,

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:35.932
who's abused and manipulated
and abused by an adult?

00:35:35.932 --> 00:35:37.796
And you say, oh my goodness,

00:35:37.796 --> 00:35:39.130
I feel compassion for that person, right?

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:42.020
But if it's us right then, oh no,

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:44.440
it's guilt and shame and
we have to hide it away.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:46.250
And when the person starts looking at it,

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:48.410
they can sort of see it from the outside.

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:51.140
And it starts to take the
energy out of it, right?

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:53.010
Then, well, who should
feel guilty about that?

00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:54.580
Who's done something wrong?

00:35:54.580 --> 00:35:56.940
And like, so now the
conceptions come together,

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.750
which is often a reflexive,
that was my fault.

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:00.690
Oh, I did it.

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:02.580
I went back to it.

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:03.900
I still stayed on the team.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:05.470
I went back next season, right?

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:07.100
I let it happen again, right?

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:09.340
All the guilt and shame inside the person

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.030
gets juxtaposed to like,
what really happened there?

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:13.080
And then they say, right.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:15.180
I was a terrified child, right?

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:16.630
I didn't understand at all.

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:18.900
And they can come to
a place of compassion.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:21.570
And now we are working
against the guilt and shame.

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:25.150
And if the person cries about
it, then it's great, right?

00:36:25.150 --> 00:36:26.793
I mean, crying is one

00:36:26.793 --> 00:36:28.180
of the best coping mechanisms we have.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:29.460
It doesn't hurt us.

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:31.070
And it lets us grieve things.

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:33.570
Yeah, we can't grieve if there's guilt

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:34.403
and shame inside of us,

00:36:34.403 --> 00:36:36.480
it just blocks grief, right?

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:37.313
We have to,

00:36:37.313 --> 00:36:38.500
there has to be a clean slate

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:40.790
in a sense in order to feel sadness.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:45.010
And then you see that it
shifts from anxiety, anger,

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:47.077
and frustration, usually
directed towards the self,

00:36:47.077 --> 00:36:49.483
the guilt and shame towards,

00:36:50.518 --> 00:36:52.810
towards being able to process
it and being able to bring

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:56.440
to bear some compassion
and being able to direct

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:57.680
the negative emotions,

00:36:57.680 --> 00:37:01.520
so to speak where they're
warranted and my goodness,

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:03.448
the changes that happen.

00:37:03.448 --> 00:37:05.970
I mean, it's not like people
are miraculously cured, right?

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:08.440
But it's remarkable how
just getting it out there

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:11.330
and having like one hour
of talking like that,

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:13.800
like what we're talking about now

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:16.373
can leave a person
feeling immensely better.

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:18.700
- It seems to me in hearing this,

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:23.240
that there's this weird
wiring that we have,

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:26.000
because what I'm hearing is
when traumas happen to us

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:27.140
or we observe them,

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:29.617
what we need to do most
is to confront those

00:37:29.617 --> 00:37:31.950
and the emotions around that directly.

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:33.212
- [Paul] Yes.

00:37:33.212 --> 00:37:35.572
But instead our system
defaults to guilt, shame.

00:37:35.572 --> 00:37:36.405
- [Paul] Yes.

00:37:36.405 --> 00:37:37.238
- And trying to hide it.

00:37:37.238 --> 00:37:38.518
- [Paul] Yes.

00:37:38.518 --> 00:37:40.530
And this repetition
compulsion of placing us back

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:43.580
into things similar to those traumas.

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:45.840
Or even maybe even worse traumas.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:46.673
- [Paul] Yes.

00:37:46.673 --> 00:37:48.155
- In an attempt to resolve it.

00:37:48.155 --> 00:37:49.246
It's like the most maladaptive.

00:37:49.246 --> 00:37:50.930
- [Andrew] Wiring diagram.

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:52.011
I could possibly think of.

00:37:52.011 --> 00:37:52.844
- Yes.

00:37:52.844 --> 00:37:54.310
- Emotional and presumably

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:56.020
physiological wiring diagram.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:56.853
- [Paul] Yes.

00:37:57.971 --> 00:37:59.440
And this notion of trying to change

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:00.980
the past by doing things now,

00:38:00.980 --> 00:38:04.870
when the exact opposite is
what's going to be beneficial

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:06.380
also seems like the complete,

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:08.210
the whole system seems
completely backwards.

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:10.020
And I'm, I'm chuckling as I say this,

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:13.000
not because I'm amused it's
because I'm just baffled once

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:16.333
again at how our wiring can
often not serve us well.

00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:19.970
But it raises an,

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:22.750
what I think is an important
and interesting question,

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:25.770
which is earlier, you
were talking about how

00:38:25.770 --> 00:38:28.800
people will seek out media
that's really disturbing.

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:31.180
They'll traumatize and
re-traumatize themselves

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:32.760
on a daily basis.

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:35.460
So that could be viewed as
the repetition compulsion

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:37.900
or the person will have
the same relationship

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.050
with seven different,

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:41.630
same abusive relationship
with seven different partners

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:44.310
in sequence seems terrible.

00:38:44.310 --> 00:38:45.143
And yet,

00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:47.100
as I say this,

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:49.700
it also is becoming clear
to me how this almost seems

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:52.480
like a poor, but desperate attempt

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:54.145
to resolve it in some way.

00:38:54.145 --> 00:38:54.978
- Yes.

00:38:57.642 --> 00:39:00.183
- And so the fork in the road,
if I understand correctly,

00:39:01.206 --> 00:39:03.490
is to really get to the seed incident,

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:05.630
really get to the thing
that started it all,

00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:07.530
as opposed to repeating it all.

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:08.363
- [Paul] Yes.

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:12.850
- Does that have to be done in
the presence of a therapist?

00:39:15.205 --> 00:39:17.490
Is there a benefit to
taking a walk and thinking

00:39:17.490 --> 00:39:20.220
about these things,
breaking down and crying,

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:23.670
if that's what's necessary
or feeling angrier,

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:25.203
if that's what comes up?

00:39:26.486 --> 00:39:31.486
The reason I ask it this
way is because I worry,

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:34.180
I'll just speak to my own experience,

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.340
I worry that in reactivating

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:39.160
or touching into the
emotions around something

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:41.610
that is itself a form of
the repetition compulsion,

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:43.339
because you're feeling it all over again.

00:39:43.339 --> 00:39:44.172
- [Paul] Right.

00:39:44.172 --> 00:39:46.570
- You're not seeking out
something to evoke that feeling.

00:39:46.570 --> 00:39:47.950
So I realize this is a little bit

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:50.240
of a circular argument, right?

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:51.700
Or question.

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:53.650
But I think it's one that
I really struggle with

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:58.003
in trying to parse all the,

00:39:58.996 --> 00:40:01.420
the outcome based
therapies that I hear about

00:40:03.352 --> 00:40:04.750
and the recommendations that people make.

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:06.670
I mean, how should we conceptualize this?

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:08.020
Something happens.

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:10.420
Sounds like we need to deal
with that thing directly.

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:12.400
Do we need to do that with somebody else?

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:13.490
Can we do that on our own?

00:40:13.490 --> 00:40:14.323
If we're,

00:40:14.323 --> 00:40:18.140
we don't have resources and
we have to do it on our own,

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:20.396
can't hire someone, can't
pay someone to work with us.

00:40:20.396 --> 00:40:21.229
- [Paul] Right.

00:40:21.229 --> 00:40:22.070
- How do we do that in a way

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:24.530
that isn't retraumatizing ourself

00:40:24.530 --> 00:40:26.623
in a major way, or in a minor way.

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:30.670
How do we know where we
are in that landscape?

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:31.650
- Right.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:33.810
Again, those are, I
think, great questions.

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:37.143
And I think it starts
with real introspection.

00:40:38.233 --> 00:40:40.080
When things are bouncing
around in our minds,

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:42.370
often, it's very, non-productive right?

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:44.763
It's the same thing over and over again,

00:40:45.861 --> 00:40:47.100
and that's not helpful for us, right?

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:48.880
So there's an idea which sometimes

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:51.200
gets called an observing ego, right?

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:52.450
The ability to stop and look

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:54.600
at what's going on inside of ourselves.

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:57.250
And so if we're just thinking about it

00:40:57.250 --> 00:40:58.610
and we're thinking in the same way,

00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:00.900
we sort of, in a sense,
always think about it,

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:03.560
then all we're doing is
reinforcing the trauma, right?

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:06.770
But if we can distance
enough to be like, huh, it's,

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:09.270
I'm interested in what's
going on inside of me, right?

00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:10.830
I can think of a certain person

00:41:11.920 --> 00:41:13.470
who really loves music.

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:16.920
I mean, and at some point
in our therapy work,

00:41:16.920 --> 00:41:20.170
I learned like she was taking long drives,

00:41:20.170 --> 00:41:22.182
but the, the radio wasn't on.

00:41:22.182 --> 00:41:23.015
And I was like, well,

00:41:23.015 --> 00:41:23.848
what what's going on, right?

00:41:23.848 --> 00:41:25.202
And I asked,

00:41:25.202 --> 00:41:26.470
and what was going on
is she was running over

00:41:26.470 --> 00:41:27.910
and over again in her head, like,

00:41:27.910 --> 00:41:29.750
I'm a loser, I'm a loser, right?

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.880
And she didn't want the music
on because the music would

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.903
drown out what she felt she
had to say to herself, right?

00:41:37.111 --> 00:41:41.524
And it was that like, wow,
that's interesting, right?

00:41:41.524 --> 00:41:44.210
And then her ability to
observe that and to think,

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:46.670
why am I doing that when
it comes into her mind?

00:41:46.670 --> 00:41:49.043
Like, what does that trace to,
when did I start doing that?

00:41:49.043 --> 00:41:50.750
Like I say, you know,

00:41:50.750 --> 00:41:52.630
I'm saying it for a point
of exaggeration, we're like,

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:54.290
nobody comes out of the womb

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:57.700
programmed to think I'm a loser, right?

00:41:57.700 --> 00:41:59.470
So we don't think that
when we're born, right?

00:41:59.470 --> 00:42:01.350
So where does that come from?

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:03.420
Then, we can think in ways

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.360
that allow us to have new thoughts, right?

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:07.193
That we weren't having,

00:42:07.193 --> 00:42:09.420
It's not just bouncing
around in our minds.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:11.680
And if we speak or write,

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:13.180
there are even more mechanisms

00:42:13.180 --> 00:42:15.330
that come online in our brains, right?

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:17.220
That are then sort of
monitoring mechanisms.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:21.283
We think in a different way,
if we're using words, right?

00:42:22.159 --> 00:42:25.310
And we are better able often
to bring in that observing ego,

00:42:25.310 --> 00:42:27.679
like what's going on inside of me?

00:42:27.679 --> 00:42:29.260
So it can be very helpful to think,

00:42:29.260 --> 00:42:30.880
it can be helpful to talk to someone,

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:33.650
to a trusted other, you
know, friend, family,

00:42:33.650 --> 00:42:35.820
clergy to write, I mean,

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:37.490
these are things that can be done

00:42:37.490 --> 00:42:39.850
without expending any resources, right?

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:42.747
And sometimes it can make
really a big difference, right?

00:42:42.747 --> 00:42:44.810
It was a way, when did
I start thinking that?

00:42:44.810 --> 00:42:46.440
And like, interestingly,
in this case, okay,

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:47.890
we did it in therapy,

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:52.556
but it became very clear what
that was rooted to, right?

00:42:52.556 --> 00:42:53.450
And then in the therapy,

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:54.770
which was still relatively young,

00:42:54.770 --> 00:42:57.415
but we'd done several sessions

00:42:57.415 --> 00:42:58.390
and we weren't talking at all

00:42:58.390 --> 00:43:00.200
about what we needed to talk about, right?

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:03.290
But that's what got us to
what we needed to talk about.

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:04.480
And when did that start,

00:43:04.480 --> 00:43:07.730
and now we're in that same
place of exploring that

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:09.300
and what was the reflex to it

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:11.500
and the sense of guilt and sense of shame.

00:43:11.500 --> 00:43:13.410
And it's where all of that came from

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:15.923
that just got boiled down
to I'm a loser, right?

00:43:17.461 --> 00:43:19.290
Which this person didn't
even have in their mind.

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:22.080
Like, I didn't think about
myself that way, right?

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:23.620
And that's is so interesting, right?

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:26.830
That our memories don't in
and of themselves have meaning

00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:30.220
it's like they're flat
or colorless, right?

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:32.380
And they're colored in by the emotions

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:33.963
that we attach to them, right?

00:43:35.417 --> 00:43:37.670
So, the idea that certain memories now,

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:40.630
before the trauma were changed, right?

00:43:40.630 --> 00:43:41.633
By the trauma.

00:43:42.618 --> 00:43:43.451
So I tell the story,

00:43:43.451 --> 00:43:45.110
sometimes of a person
who like won an award

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:46.950
when they were in high
school that they thought was,

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:48.140
oh my gosh, like it shows,

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.480
like I can do it, right?

00:43:49.480 --> 00:43:51.880
I get out there, that after trauma,

00:43:51.880 --> 00:43:54.860
they saw the award with the
negative emotion attached to it.

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:55.693
That was like, oh,

00:43:55.693 --> 00:43:57.300
it was given to me and
I didn't deserve it.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:43:58.770
And almost it was mocking.

00:43:58.770 --> 00:44:01.080
Like, it was going to be the
greatest achievement in my life

00:44:01.080 --> 00:44:04.510
and I was 17 or so, and to
have someone think like,

00:44:04.510 --> 00:44:07.640
that's not how they felt
about that at the time.

00:44:07.640 --> 00:44:11.420
It's the trauma that
changed, the self talk,

00:44:11.420 --> 00:44:13.990
the internal state going forward

00:44:13.990 --> 00:44:17.250
and talking about miraculous
in a negative way,

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:20.163
also changed that going backward, right?

00:44:21.156 --> 00:44:21.989
And when we can really look at that,

00:44:21.989 --> 00:44:23.470
like where did that come from?

00:44:23.470 --> 00:44:26.870
And we can start
unraveling it, it changes.

00:44:26.870 --> 00:44:30.463
So in those cases, you
know, often it's helpful

00:44:30.463 --> 00:44:33.370
to have a good therapist,
it's not always necessary.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:35.840
And it certainly, it's not
always possible, right?

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:38.090
So we need other strategies.

00:44:38.090 --> 00:44:39.140
And some of those,

00:44:39.140 --> 00:44:41.546
I write about some of those in the book

00:44:41.546 --> 00:44:43.170
of how can we sort of get at trauma

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:45.203
without those formalized mechanisms.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:48.670
And sometimes if the symptoms
are significant enough,

00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:51.750
like we really do need to
talk to somebody professional

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:54.370
who can help us get to
the root of the trauma.

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:56.100
And there's so many times,

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:58.630
that's the answer to what's
going on with people.

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:00.860
People I've seen have had
five residential stays.

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:03.730
I'm not exaggerating this,
for mental health reasons,

00:45:03.730 --> 00:45:04.750
for substance reasons,

00:45:04.750 --> 00:45:07.100
and no one's ever taken
a trauma history, right?

00:45:07.100 --> 00:45:09.050
And then when you take a
trauma history, you say,

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:11.940
well, that's obviously where
this is all coming from, right?

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:14.250
Like that's when the drug
use started truly thereafter,

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:16.210
the negative self talk
and the negative feelings

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:17.560
that led to the drug use.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:19.700
Then you go after the trauma

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:21.240
and you can change things.

00:45:21.240 --> 00:45:24.240
Whereas trying to change
things without looking,

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:26.483
introspecting, talking about the trauma,

00:45:27.866 --> 00:45:29.316
I think of course was futile.

00:45:30.550 --> 00:45:31.850
- Do you think that people

00:45:33.361 --> 00:45:34.900
can start to have negative fantasies?

00:45:34.900 --> 00:45:35.880
I mean, you mentioned this woman

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:38.793
who would take these long
drives to berate herself.

00:45:40.187 --> 00:45:42.570
I'm not familiar with that, but I'll,

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:43.670
I'll give a little bit of
personal disclosure here.

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:46.090
I've felt several times in my life

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:49.670
that I will start to create a narrative

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:52.080
about something that truly hasn't happened

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:55.020
about something terrible
that somebody is going to do.

00:45:55.020 --> 00:45:55.860
- [Paul] Yes.

00:45:55.860 --> 00:45:57.640
- That's going to upset me.

00:45:57.640 --> 00:45:58.473
- [Paul] Yes.

00:45:58.473 --> 00:45:59.804
- And for the longest time,

00:45:59.804 --> 00:46:00.690
I wonder why am I doing this?

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:03.973
And I have a couple ideas about why, one,

00:46:05.302 --> 00:46:08.007
is that I was attempting
to just avoid thinking

00:46:08.007 --> 00:46:09.360
about other things.

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:11.210
It's just, you know, anger is such

00:46:11.210 --> 00:46:13.620
an attractive emotional force in this.

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:15.480
It's an attracting, it's not attractive.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:16.313
We don't like it.

00:46:16.313 --> 00:46:18.970
And yet, oftentimes anger is a great way

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:20.980
to replace feeling something else.

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:21.813
- [Paul] Yes.

00:46:21.813 --> 00:46:23.530
- Feeling sad or having to come up

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.660
or to do work or to do something useful.

00:46:26.660 --> 00:46:31.660
So it has this kind of a like
gravitational force to it.

00:46:32.240 --> 00:46:33.750
That was one idea.

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:37.413
The other idea was in imagining
kind of worst outcomes,

00:46:38.289 --> 00:46:40.070
then actually that relationship were,

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:41.915
could actually seem a
lot better in reality.

00:46:41.915 --> 00:46:42.748
- Hm hm.

00:46:42.748 --> 00:46:44.170
- [Andrew] It's almost like
creating this negative contrast.

00:46:44.170 --> 00:46:45.003
- Yes.

00:46:45.003 --> 00:46:46.883
It's like, oh, well
then it's not that bad.

00:46:48.049 --> 00:46:50.550
And then the third possibility
is I have no idea why,

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:51.990
but it seemed like a reflex.

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:53.390
And I spent some time thinking about it.

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:55.320
I can't say I've resolved it completely,

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:56.660
but why would somebody

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:00.960
have a narrative or a default
narrative when driving

00:47:00.960 --> 00:47:04.445
or when walking of I'm just
going to spend some time

00:47:04.445 --> 00:47:06.080
and think about how terrible
this thing is going to turn out

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:07.340
or how someone's going to upset me

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:10.790
or harm me or how terrible I am.

00:47:10.790 --> 00:47:14.210
It seems, again, like
maladaptive thinking,

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:15.760
maladaptive wiring.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:18.810
And yet I have to assume
that it serves some purpose.

00:47:18.810 --> 00:47:20.398
- Yeah, yeah.

00:47:20.398 --> 00:47:21.780
I mean, I think there are three factors

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:24.070
there and they're all bad.

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:27.360
And I think you spoke to at
least two of them, right?

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:29.760
They I think speak so powerfully

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:34.410
to how insidious trauma
is and how these are real

00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:36.330
brain changes inside of us.

00:47:36.330 --> 00:47:39.830
So I would say that the
three factors, punishment,

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:42.143
avoidance and control, right?

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:44.660
So the trauma inside of us,

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:47.120
that makes guilt and shame.

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:50.740
So often, so often leads

00:47:50.740 --> 00:47:53.397
to a desire to punish oneself, right?

00:47:53.397 --> 00:47:55.470
And the idea that, oh, that was my fault.

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:56.340
Or I deserve that.

00:47:56.340 --> 00:47:58.080
Well, what do we do if something

00:47:58.080 --> 00:47:59.255
is someone's fault and someone

00:47:59.255 --> 00:48:01.200
now deserves punishment, right?

00:48:01.200 --> 00:48:02.860
I mean, we we punish them, right?

00:48:02.860 --> 00:48:04.890
We send 'em to jail, we
give them a fine, right?

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:05.780
We punish them.

00:48:05.780 --> 00:48:08.750
And so what, what we do is
punish ourselves, right?

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:10.480
And if we tell ourselves we're a loser

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:12.590
or this awful thing is
going to happen, right?

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:14.570
Then part of what we're
doing is saying to ourselves,

00:48:14.570 --> 00:48:16.170
see, right, you deserve that.

00:48:16.170 --> 00:48:17.640
You're not going to have
anything better, right?

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:18.650
It's a negative.

00:48:18.650 --> 00:48:23.310
It's a very negative
way that the brain tries

00:48:23.310 --> 00:48:24.570
to make us in a sense,

00:48:24.570 --> 00:48:27.350
to do better by hurting
us more for the things

00:48:27.350 --> 00:48:29.340
that we couldn't and
shouldn't have been able to,

00:48:29.340 --> 00:48:32.270
weren't expected to control
in the first place, right?

00:48:32.270 --> 00:48:34.070
The second is distraction.

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:35.280
As you said, anger,

00:48:35.280 --> 00:48:37.980
that kind of fantasy can distract us

00:48:40.237 --> 00:48:41.720
from affect feeling and emotion.

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:43.390
That can be much more negative.

00:48:43.390 --> 00:48:46.070
Anger, it can be more gratifying

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:47.690
than certainly than guilt or shame,

00:48:47.690 --> 00:48:50.020
although guilt or shame can
serve a punishment purpose.

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:52.743
But if anger is directed also
towards ourselves, right,

00:48:52.743 --> 00:48:54.640
then it can serve punishment too.

00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:57.000
So punishment, avoidance,

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:00.280
and the sense of control
that if you think ahead

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:01.510
to something awful,

00:49:01.510 --> 00:49:03.870
that you're imagining is going to happen,

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:06.020
well, maybe that will
let you avoid it, right?

00:49:06.020 --> 00:49:08.820
I mean, you can see the
brain here in a sense,

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:09.980
really confused.

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:12.160
I mean, part of the brain wants to punish.

00:49:12.160 --> 00:49:14.250
part of the brain doesn't
want to think about it at all

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:16.720
and part of the brain
wants to make it better.

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:18.980
And then how all of that resolves,

00:49:18.980 --> 00:49:20.150
if we're not aware that,

00:49:20.150 --> 00:49:22.060
hey, this is in the context of our brains

00:49:22.060 --> 00:49:24.490
being deeply impacted by trauma.

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:27.590
So what's going on here
is all maladaptive, right?

00:49:27.590 --> 00:49:30.020
'Cause these negative
fantasies of the future,

00:49:30.020 --> 00:49:32.980
they may help us feel better
about something in the present,

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:35.670
but they don't help us make
anything better, right?

00:49:35.670 --> 00:49:37.840
They don't help us make anything better.

00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:40.520
So this is kind of the sequela.

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:44.120
This is where trauma and
all this reflexive stuff

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:47.260
that happens after trauma
ultimately lead us.

00:49:47.260 --> 00:49:49.090
And you can see how we get lost,

00:49:49.090 --> 00:49:52.750
how I've seen over and
over again in my own life,

00:49:52.750 --> 00:49:54.790
in the lives of other people,

00:49:54.790 --> 00:49:59.140
how man we get stuck in those situations

00:49:59.140 --> 00:50:00.501
and that's why I see people sometimes.

00:50:00.501 --> 00:50:04.020
This has been going on for
30 years, 40 years, right?

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:07.240
And it's just been going on
over and over and over again

00:50:08.216 --> 00:50:10.680
because there's no natural
end to any of this, right?

00:50:10.680 --> 00:50:12.302
Unless we,

00:50:12.302 --> 00:50:13.400
we look at it in a different way,

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:15.660
that we have knowledge and
information like, whoa,

00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:17.200
this isn't the way it has to be.

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:19.680
Let me bring a novel perspective to this.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:21.130
It doesn't change on its own.

00:50:23.651 --> 00:50:25.220
- I'm struck by your statement

00:50:26.466 --> 00:50:29.380
that these thoughts or behaviors
can make us feel better,

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:31.250
but they don't actually
make anything better.

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:32.690
In that way,

00:50:32.690 --> 00:50:36.490
this mode of imagining terrible outcomes

00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:40.149
starts to immediately
seem like taking opioids.

00:50:40.149 --> 00:50:41.820
You feel better in the moment,

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:43.307
but it doesn't actually
make anything better.

00:50:43.307 --> 00:50:45.230
And it probably makes things worse.

00:50:45.230 --> 00:50:46.063
- Yes.

00:50:47.380 --> 00:50:50.330
- And just a question of how much worse

00:50:51.711 --> 00:50:53.093
and in what direction, yes.

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:57.550
And so I just want to just
pause on that concept,

00:50:57.550 --> 00:51:00.720
because I think that concept
of makes us feel better,

00:51:00.720 --> 00:51:01.940
but doesn't make anything better.

00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:05.740
I think it answers an earlier question

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:10.250
about what seems to be a totally
maladaptive wiring diagram.

00:51:10.250 --> 00:51:11.410
We need to confront the thing,

00:51:11.410 --> 00:51:14.431
but we don't want to go into
the repetition compulsion.

00:51:14.431 --> 00:51:16.640
So it's a knife edge there,

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:18.450
to navigate through trauma.

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:19.283
- [Paul] Yes.

00:51:20.130 --> 00:51:23.210
- Working with a very skilled
clinician like yourself,

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:26.263
I think is the ideal
circumstance for people.

00:51:28.643 --> 00:51:31.010
And of course there are people
who can't access support

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:33.150
from somebody, for whatever reason.

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:35.030
You've talked about journaling.

00:51:35.030 --> 00:51:36.930
- Hmm, yes.

00:51:36.930 --> 00:51:38.450
- As a useful tool.

00:51:38.450 --> 00:51:39.380
Could you maybe highlight

00:51:39.380 --> 00:51:42.580
some of the other things that
people can do on their own.

00:51:42.580 --> 00:51:44.930
And then I'd also like to talk about

00:51:44.930 --> 00:51:47.600
what makes for a good therapist.

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:48.580
What should people look for

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:50.310
for those that are seeking therapy,

00:51:50.310 --> 00:51:51.750
especially nowadays when a lot of therapy

00:51:51.750 --> 00:51:55.630
is being done remotely, but
let's just start with the,

00:51:55.630 --> 00:51:57.590
let's just call them self-generated

00:51:57.590 --> 00:52:00.529
or zero cost sorts of things,

00:52:00.529 --> 00:52:02.650
journaling being the first

00:52:02.650 --> 00:52:04.822
and then what are some of the others

00:52:04.822 --> 00:52:05.780
and what kind of structure
would you recommend

00:52:05.780 --> 00:52:07.700
someone put around journaling,

00:52:07.700 --> 00:52:09.660
carry a journal around all
day and jot things down

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:10.860
as they come up or sit down

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:13.230
and spend an hour writing
in complete sentences,

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:14.260
for instance.

00:52:14.260 --> 00:52:15.093
- Yeah.

00:52:15.093 --> 00:52:18.110
If I could add something
to what you had just said

00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:19.550
before the question, right?

00:52:19.550 --> 00:52:21.640
That we have these short-term

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:23.790
coping mechanisms in us, right?

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:25.200
And in a way it makes sense, right?

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:29.470
If we find ourselves in
just terrible situations,

00:52:29.470 --> 00:52:31.360
then a short-term coping mechanism

00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:33.000
can get us through them, right?

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:34.650
So our brains are built that way

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:36.930
and that's part of survival too, right?

00:52:36.930 --> 00:52:40.210
And whether now in the modern
world, whether it's food,

00:52:40.210 --> 00:52:43.640
it's drugs, it's sex, it's alcohol, right?

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:45.980
Or it's negative thoughts, right?

00:52:45.980 --> 00:52:48.770
This is short-term soothing.

00:52:48.770 --> 00:52:49.787
Even the negative thoughts,

00:52:49.787 --> 00:52:51.570
And anger is short-term soothing

00:52:51.570 --> 00:52:55.460
at the expense of long-term change, right?

00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:59.510
And that's where addictive
pathways can come into play.

00:52:59.510 --> 00:53:01.690
And that's where, again, our,

00:53:01.690 --> 00:53:04.760
how we're built
evolutionarily for survival,

00:53:04.760 --> 00:53:08.200
doesn't help us, you know, in
the way humans have evolved.

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:10.440
Like we haven't lived this way throughout,

00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:13.793
99.9% something percent
of human history, right?

00:53:15.022 --> 00:53:16.230
So we're not adapted to this.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:17.570
So I want to just make a point of saying

00:53:17.570 --> 00:53:19.140
that about the short-term soothing

00:53:19.140 --> 00:53:22.630
at the expense of any of long-term change,

00:53:22.630 --> 00:53:25.490
And then the question you had
asked about say journaling

00:53:25.490 --> 00:53:28.950
or what can we do that's
outside of professional.

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.020
I think the hallmark of it

00:53:31.020 --> 00:53:33.770
has to be bringing new eyes to it, right?

00:53:33.770 --> 00:53:36.630
Like thinking about self with a curiosity,

00:53:36.630 --> 00:53:40.400
instead of just a simple
automaticity or repetition, right?

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:42.600
Like, why am I thinking about this?

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:44.150
When did this start?

00:53:44.150 --> 00:53:45.670
Why is this in me?

00:53:45.670 --> 00:53:46.503
Right?

00:53:48.390 --> 00:53:50.470
Whether it's words or
whether we're writing,

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:51.620
that's so important.

00:53:51.620 --> 00:53:53.480
So I think for journaling,

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:55.000
it depends on the person.

00:53:55.000 --> 00:53:56.530
I mean, we don't want
somebody carrying around

00:53:56.530 --> 00:53:57.390
a journal all day,

00:53:57.390 --> 00:53:59.020
if then there's a compulsion to,

00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:00.130
I need to write about everything

00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:01.210
that's going on in my mind, right?

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:02.660
Like that might be good to okay.

00:54:02.660 --> 00:54:04.300
Write a little bit at night, right?

00:54:04.300 --> 00:54:06.050
Or someone who might think,

00:54:06.050 --> 00:54:08.517
sometimes this really comes
into my mind in a strong way

00:54:08.517 --> 00:54:09.890
and it could be unpredictable, right?

00:54:09.890 --> 00:54:11.830
I want to have the journal with me.

00:54:11.830 --> 00:54:14.870
So, ah, that thing is back in my mind now,

00:54:14.870 --> 00:54:16.480
let me write about it, right?

00:54:16.480 --> 00:54:18.190
Because then putting words to it

00:54:18.190 --> 00:54:20.520
and then being able to
read those words, right?

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:21.610
And when people read,

00:54:21.610 --> 00:54:22.970
even do a little bit of journaling

00:54:22.970 --> 00:54:25.350
and they read like, oh,

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:28.480
I thought again about
how I'm a terrible person

00:54:28.480 --> 00:54:29.973
who can't have a good life,

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:32.970
because I was in such a bad car accident

00:54:32.970 --> 00:54:34.670
or because that person attacked me

00:54:35.683 --> 00:54:36.516
or because when I was in school,

00:54:36.516 --> 00:54:38.640
I was bullied because I looked different

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:39.730
than everyone else, right?

00:54:39.730 --> 00:54:41.480
Or acted different from everyone else.

00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:44.840
Wow, to actually see that written out.

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:48.150
It's a little bit of that,

00:54:48.150 --> 00:54:49.740
it's a little bit of that.

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:51.240
Like when you're saying it to someone

00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:52.860
as if it were someone else, right?

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:54.810
Because now there's
enough distance from it.

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:56.513
Like I'm looking at the
words I wrote, right?

00:54:56.513 --> 00:54:59.760
That we get some distance
and we can start to integrate

00:54:59.760 --> 00:55:02.850
some of the, not just the compassion,

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:05.410
but integrating compassion
and logic, right?

00:55:05.410 --> 00:55:06.243
Of like, okay.

00:55:06.243 --> 00:55:08.520
I feel a sense of compassion
now, wait, what does this mean?

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:11.305
What really happened here, right?

00:55:11.305 --> 00:55:13.420
And gosh, I did start thinking
differently after that.

00:55:13.420 --> 00:55:15.430
I started, that's where
this came from, right?

00:55:15.430 --> 00:55:17.040
That's why I'm saying this,

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:19.910
it's those kind of
revelations that we can have

00:55:19.910 --> 00:55:22.540
through again, the written or spoken word.

00:55:22.540 --> 00:55:25.223
And I think again, that
involves a trusted other,

00:55:26.496 --> 00:55:27.329
or writing, right?

00:55:27.329 --> 00:55:31.134
And I think that those
are ways we can do this,

00:55:31.134 --> 00:55:34.290
where we bring some de novo
perspective to something

00:55:34.290 --> 00:55:36.880
that often has been bouncing
around inside of us.

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:38.733
And it's amazing to me that,

00:55:38.733 --> 00:55:42.910
I can see such intelligent
empathically, attuned people

00:55:42.910 --> 00:55:45.500
who've had the same thing

00:55:45.500 --> 00:55:49.280
running over and over again
in their mind for years.

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:51.190
And it just points out that our brains

00:55:51.190 --> 00:55:53.790
don't automatically
say, hey, wait a second.

00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:56.470
I've been spinning wheels
here for a long, long time.

00:55:56.470 --> 00:55:58.400
Like, was there another
way to look at this?

00:55:58.400 --> 00:56:00.290
We need something from the outside,

00:56:00.290 --> 00:56:02.040
which can just be knowledge, right?

00:56:02.040 --> 00:56:03.900
Which is why I think what we're doing here

00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:07.800
or the reason I wrote the
book that I wrote was like,

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:11.830
apprehending this like
amazing surprise to me, right?

00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:12.950
Which is like, wow,

00:56:12.950 --> 00:56:17.060
like some huge percentage
of everything I'm treating

00:56:17.060 --> 00:56:21.230
is rooted in trauma and the
opacity of trauma, right?

00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:24.240
Which is why we don't see
that, oh, the depression,

00:56:24.240 --> 00:56:27.500
the panic attacks, the
life change, the addiction,

00:56:27.500 --> 00:56:29.250
the maladaptive choices like, oh,

00:56:29.250 --> 00:56:32.814
this is all coming from
trauma because it hides itself

00:56:32.814 --> 00:56:34.300
in that opacity.

00:56:34.300 --> 00:56:36.830
So we need a de novo perspective

00:56:36.830 --> 00:56:38.250
if we're doing it on our own.

00:56:38.250 --> 00:56:40.440
And we need that if we're
doing it in therapy,

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:42.900
which might link like finding
the right therapist, right?

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:44.590
Which is also part of your question.

00:56:44.590 --> 00:56:46.590
- Yeah, yeah I definitely want to know

00:56:47.524 --> 00:56:49.270
about how to assess and
find the right therapist.

00:56:49.270 --> 00:56:52.850
Before we cover that, however,

00:56:52.850 --> 00:56:57.060
something came up in the
course of your answer.

00:56:57.060 --> 00:57:00.620
I can immediately relate to this idea that

00:57:00.620 --> 00:57:03.070
certain behaviors are really maladaptive

00:57:05.139 --> 00:57:07.890
and are stuffing things
down or avoiding the topic

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:10.120
is problematic and bringing a curiosity

00:57:10.120 --> 00:57:12.780
and an introspection and
almost a third personing

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.440
of the experience

00:57:15.440 --> 00:57:18.100
that we've had in order
to try and address it

00:57:18.100 --> 00:57:20.150
from a new, truly from a new perspective.

00:57:21.080 --> 00:57:24.210
It occurred to me as we
were discussing this,

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:26.480
however that some people, and yes,

00:57:26.480 --> 00:57:29.926
maybe I'm talking a little
bit about my own experience.

00:57:29.926 --> 00:57:31.676
We have a sense of our own identity

00:57:32.778 --> 00:57:35.510
and how people view us and
our ability to be functional

00:57:35.510 --> 00:57:37.170
in the world in ways that we like,

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:39.070
effective at work or a good brother

00:57:39.070 --> 00:57:43.290
or a good mother or father,
human being in the world.

00:57:43.290 --> 00:57:44.660
We have relationships.

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:47.893
And I think that one
thing that I have heard,

00:57:48.970 --> 00:57:52.690
and maybe I've experienced
is that sometimes those

00:57:52.690 --> 00:57:54.650
maladaptive thoughts or behaviors,

00:57:54.650 --> 00:57:57.100
the things that generate a kind

00:57:57.100 --> 00:58:00.070
of a repetition of anger
or of arousal or activation

00:58:00.070 --> 00:58:03.430
or sadness, that we have
some internal process

00:58:03.430 --> 00:58:06.670
where we funnel that into a
functionality in the world.

00:58:06.670 --> 00:58:09.453
So we I'll give a more concrete example.

00:58:10.350 --> 00:58:13.720
So in thinking about things
that have upset me in the past

00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:16.870
and in imagining bad
outcomes in the future,

00:58:16.870 --> 00:58:18.900
there's a certain
internal state of arousal

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:20.033
that comes about.

00:58:21.323 --> 00:58:23.510
And for many years, I
was able to use that,

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:24.910
not to feel angry,

00:58:24.910 --> 00:58:27.250
but rather to work an
extra three hours a day

00:58:27.250 --> 00:58:28.894
- [Paul] Right.

00:58:28.894 --> 00:58:32.580
- Or to pack my schedule with
work and social engagement.

00:58:32.580 --> 00:58:34.950
So I could show up in a way that I,

00:58:34.950 --> 00:58:37.025
hopefully was a very good brother
to my sister for instance.

00:58:37.025 --> 00:58:37.858
- [Paul] Right.

00:58:37.858 --> 00:58:39.620
- So in a way it was a,

00:58:39.620 --> 00:58:41.910
it was a transformation
of something negative

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:42.843
inside of me.

00:58:42.843 --> 00:58:43.676
- [Paul] Yes.

00:58:43.676 --> 00:58:46.029
- Into a functionality in the world

00:58:46.029 --> 00:58:47.749
that was actually very
rewarding and beneficial.

00:58:47.749 --> 00:58:49.129
- [Paul] Yes.

00:58:49.129 --> 00:58:50.180
- And yet in describing it,

00:58:50.180 --> 00:58:52.710
I can immediately see how it
would be wonderful if I could

00:58:52.710 --> 00:58:53.543
source from something else.

00:58:53.543 --> 00:58:54.490
- [Paul] Hm- hm.

00:58:54.490 --> 00:58:57.190
- And yet I, you can imagine,

00:58:57.190 --> 00:59:00.030
and I can imagine how
one would be reluctant,

00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:02.158
maybe even terrified of
giving up that source.

00:59:02.158 --> 00:59:02.991
- Yes.

00:59:02.991 --> 00:59:03.824
- [ Andrew] It's a fuel.

00:59:03.824 --> 00:59:04.785
- Yes.

00:59:04.785 --> 00:59:08.387
and I think in knowing some
of the traumas of other people

00:59:08.387 --> 00:59:11.050
and their reluctance
to work through those,

00:59:11.050 --> 00:59:12.550
obviously I'm not a therapist,

00:59:13.504 --> 00:59:14.990
I sense this over and over again,

00:59:14.990 --> 00:59:17.860
that one's positive identity

00:59:17.860 --> 00:59:19.971
can often be linked to something
difficult in their past.

00:59:19.971 --> 00:59:20.850
- [Paul] Yes.

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:23.500
- And so people are reluctant
to give up this fuel.

00:59:23.500 --> 00:59:24.333
- [Paul] Yes.

00:59:24.333 --> 00:59:27.003
- Because it it's in that
sense, it's functional.

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:32.010
The only thing that allowed
me to kind of start to address

00:59:32.010 --> 00:59:34.180
this and why I'm still
so curious about this,

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:35.780
'cause I don't think I've worked

00:59:35.780 --> 00:59:38.000
through this process completely,

00:59:38.000 --> 00:59:40.100
again, a little more
self-disclosure there,

00:59:42.537 --> 00:59:44.860
is that I was told that these words,

00:59:44.860 --> 00:59:47.150
just imagine how much better it would be

00:59:47.150 --> 00:59:50.970
if you could source from a different fuel,

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:52.230
a fuel that felt better.

00:59:52.230 --> 00:59:53.307
- Right.

00:59:53.307 --> 00:59:54.520
- Maybe it was on the, it was on this,

00:59:54.520 --> 00:59:55.353
this sentence.

00:59:55.353 --> 00:59:58.930
It was, maybe you could actually be

00:59:58.930 --> 01:00:00.150
much more effective.

01:00:00.150 --> 01:00:00.983
- [Paul] Yes.

01:00:00.983 --> 01:00:03.180
- Maybe you could be 10
times the better brother.

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:04.013
- [Paul] Yes.

01:00:04.013 --> 01:00:05.500
- Maybe you could have 10 times

01:00:05.500 --> 01:00:08.330
more insight or work capacity, etcetera.

01:00:08.330 --> 01:00:11.750
So it's on that hint of a promise that I,

01:00:11.750 --> 01:00:14.690
at least I was inspired to
start looking into these things

01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:17.030
and reading about trauma
in your book and elsewhere,

01:00:17.030 --> 01:00:18.260
and start to think about this.

01:00:18.260 --> 01:00:21.270
So again, I realize this
is a long winded question

01:00:21.270 --> 01:00:23.300
and a somewhat complex idea, but I think,

01:00:23.300 --> 01:00:25.330
or I hope that people will
be able to resonate with this

01:00:25.330 --> 01:00:27.430
idea that sometimes we
want to stay attached

01:00:28.448 --> 01:00:30.870
to this short term soothing
that the punishment

01:00:30.870 --> 01:00:33.940
distraction or control
because it evokes this arousal

01:00:33.940 --> 01:00:35.873
and then we can apply that arousal.

01:00:35.873 --> 01:00:37.240
- Yes, yes.

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:39.180
I think what you're describing maps

01:00:39.180 --> 01:00:41.770
I think clinically to what
gets called sublimation.

01:00:41.770 --> 01:00:44.610
So there's something
negative inside of us,

01:00:44.610 --> 01:00:46.520
but we sort of transfer that energy,

01:00:46.520 --> 01:00:48.440
we transfer that into something

01:00:48.440 --> 01:00:50.770
that is adaptive or
that is positive, right?

01:00:50.770 --> 01:00:52.080
So the idea of the anger, right?

01:00:52.080 --> 01:00:54.350
When I think of that thing
and it makes anger in me,

01:00:54.350 --> 01:00:56.470
I channel that into harder work, right?

01:00:56.470 --> 01:00:57.680
Or I channel that into like,

01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:59.640
I'm going to go be nicer
to my brothers, some right,

01:00:59.640 --> 01:01:00.640
something like that.

01:01:02.197 --> 01:01:04.380
And there's validity to that, right?

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:07.670
But it can become like self
justifying if a person thinks,

01:01:07.670 --> 01:01:10.050
well, look at what this
is doing for me, right?

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:11.830
I wouldn't work as hard without it.

01:01:11.830 --> 01:01:15.330
Right now we start to become
attached to the trauma.

01:01:15.330 --> 01:01:19.090
Whereas I think what you
had said is absolutely true

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:22.230
that just because we can sublimate

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:25.140
some of the negative
affect, feeling, emotion

01:01:25.140 --> 01:01:26.900
that comes from trauma into something

01:01:26.900 --> 01:01:30.577
productive doesn't mean
that that's best, right?

01:01:30.577 --> 01:01:33.000
I mean we can get to our destination

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:35.140
by taking a very circuitous route, right?

01:01:35.140 --> 01:01:37.700
We might waste an hour getting
there, but we get there.

01:01:37.700 --> 01:01:40.260
That doesn't mean that that's best.

01:01:40.260 --> 01:01:42.210
And it also doesn't look
at all the negative, right?

01:01:42.210 --> 01:01:45.210
In this example, the wasted
fuel, the wasted time, right?

01:01:45.210 --> 01:01:48.830
We get somewhere, but
we are not optimizing.

01:01:48.830 --> 01:01:51.320
And I have yet to see one person

01:01:52.477 --> 01:01:53.840
who has addressed the trauma

01:01:53.840 --> 01:01:56.130
and become less functional, right?

01:01:56.130 --> 01:01:59.070
It's always either,
they're just as functional,

01:01:59.070 --> 01:02:00.730
but they're happier, right?

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:03.320
Or more functional
because as you said, like,

01:02:03.320 --> 01:02:06.766
just because we may be
able to sublimate, well,

01:02:06.766 --> 01:02:08.980
maybe what's going on will
be 10 times better, right?

01:02:08.980 --> 01:02:11.200
If we weren't sublimating

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:13.000
because the sublimation limits us, right?

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:16.080
It limits our perspective
to only what we can see

01:02:16.080 --> 01:02:18.033
and do through the lens of the trauma.

01:02:19.182 --> 01:02:22.473
And that is never better
than the alternative.

01:02:23.350 --> 01:02:24.183
- Thank you for that.

01:02:24.183 --> 01:02:25.490
- Yeah, you're welcome, yeah.

01:02:26.640 --> 01:02:29.650
- Let's discuss how one could

01:02:29.650 --> 01:02:32.753
or should go about finding
a really good therapist.

01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:39.330
Typically in my experience,
this is done by word of mouth.

01:02:39.330 --> 01:02:41.085
There's this person you
might want to work with them

01:02:41.085 --> 01:02:41.918
and they're really great,

01:02:41.918 --> 01:02:42.923
but what are some of the characteristics

01:02:42.923 --> 01:02:44.810
that one should look for?

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:48.000
And should we take into account whether

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:51.810
or not we are a person who for instance,

01:02:51.810 --> 01:02:55.170
I've heard this from listeners,
although I'm clearly,

01:02:55.170 --> 01:02:56.780
I'm definitely not talking about myself

01:02:56.780 --> 01:02:59.510
here in cloaking something.

01:02:59.510 --> 01:03:00.730
Some people will say,

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:03.980
I want to work with a somatic
therapist because I've actually

01:03:03.980 --> 01:03:05.960
heard someone say, I think in fields,

01:03:05.960 --> 01:03:07.680
I feel stuff in my body.

01:03:07.680 --> 01:03:08.750
So I want to work with someone

01:03:08.750 --> 01:03:10.220
who can really acknowledge that

01:03:10.220 --> 01:03:11.960
or someone else will say,

01:03:11.960 --> 01:03:14.710
I want to work with somebody
who has this orientation

01:03:14.710 --> 01:03:19.380
or that orientation or is open
to my particular lifestyle,

01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:22.540
or isn't going to tell me that
I have to leave my relationship.

01:03:22.540 --> 01:03:24.530
I feel like people already
show up to the question

01:03:24.530 --> 01:03:27.150
of who to work with with
all these, you know,

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:27.983
things internally,

01:03:27.983 --> 01:03:30.540
some of which are voiced
and some of which aren't.

01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:33.120
So I'd love for you to talk
about maybe some of the,

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:35.940
the core features of a
really good therapist

01:03:36.941 --> 01:03:38.500
and then how to look for a therapist.

01:03:38.500 --> 01:03:40.310
And also how to think about oneself

01:03:40.310 --> 01:03:41.398
in looking for a therapist.

01:03:41.398 --> 01:03:42.419
- [Paul] Right.

01:03:42.419 --> 01:03:43.760
- Because of these kind
of predispositions.

01:03:43.760 --> 01:03:44.860
- [Paul] Right, right.

01:03:46.446 --> 01:03:48.810
Well, there's a lot of data about this

01:03:48.810 --> 01:03:49.670
over the years,

01:03:49.670 --> 01:03:52.090
if you look at what are the top 10

01:03:52.090 --> 01:03:54.600
important factors to find in a therapist,

01:03:54.600 --> 01:03:58.210
just repeat rapport 10 times, right?

01:03:58.210 --> 01:03:59.530
I mean, that's the key.

01:03:59.530 --> 01:04:00.960
And if you think about that,

01:04:00.960 --> 01:04:02.100
it's pretty amazing, right?

01:04:02.100 --> 01:04:05.500
Because therapeutic modalities
can be so different, right?

01:04:05.500 --> 01:04:09.330
And I think what that's
telling us is, in a way,

01:04:09.330 --> 01:04:10.530
something very obvious, right?

01:04:10.530 --> 01:04:11.880
Like what does rapport mean?

01:04:11.880 --> 01:04:12.750
Like, you know, it's somebody

01:04:12.750 --> 01:04:13.850
that's paying attention, right?

01:04:13.850 --> 01:04:15.768
It's trust, it's a back and forth.

01:04:15.768 --> 01:04:18.910
It's like, yeah. even though
I'm doing something difficult,

01:04:18.910 --> 01:04:20.560
I'm doing it with someone
who's really helping me,

01:04:20.560 --> 01:04:22.070
someone who's in it with me, right?

01:04:22.070 --> 01:04:23.480
Someone who's really paying attention.

01:04:23.480 --> 01:04:25.110
Wants me to be better.

01:04:25.110 --> 01:04:27.940
That's indispensable, I mean,
it's just indispensable.

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:29.550
And I write in the book is someone,

01:04:29.550 --> 01:04:31.130
a therapist not making eye contact

01:04:31.130 --> 01:04:32.620
or this is the way they do it, right?

01:04:32.620 --> 01:04:33.453
And you know,

01:04:33.453 --> 01:04:35.540
you got to fit into the
box of the way they do it.

01:04:35.540 --> 01:04:38.140
That is not going to be helpful.

01:04:38.140 --> 01:04:39.630
And then what I,

01:04:39.630 --> 01:04:42.920
what I think about that is
the different modalities.

01:04:42.920 --> 01:04:44.500
It doesn't actually tell us that,

01:04:44.500 --> 01:04:45.990
oh, the modalities are irrelevant.

01:04:45.990 --> 01:04:47.800
I think that's not true.

01:04:47.800 --> 01:04:51.480
I think that good therapists
are not pigeonholed

01:04:51.480 --> 01:04:52.970
by a certain modality.

01:04:52.970 --> 01:04:55.720
They may come at the world largely

01:04:55.720 --> 01:04:59.300
through a psychodynamic
or a CBT or a DBT lens.

01:04:59.300 --> 01:05:00.820
There's lots of different,

01:05:00.820 --> 01:05:02.360
ways to do therapy.

01:05:02.360 --> 01:05:04.150
But when you really talk to those people,

01:05:04.150 --> 01:05:06.400
really good experience therapists,

01:05:06.400 --> 01:05:09.570
it's all coming through
the vehicle of the rapport,

01:05:09.570 --> 01:05:12.890
but they're practically shifting
to what the person needs.

01:05:12.890 --> 01:05:15.530
I don't understand the idea that like, oh,

01:05:15.530 --> 01:05:17.590
I just do this, right?

01:05:17.590 --> 01:05:18.423
I don't do that.

01:05:18.423 --> 01:05:20.370
And when people are pigeonholed that way,

01:05:20.370 --> 01:05:23.170
I don't think they help their
patients very well, right?

01:05:24.056 --> 01:05:25.460
We have to be diverse enough to say, hey,

01:05:25.460 --> 01:05:28.020
I want all the arrows
in the quiver, right?

01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:30.290
And even though there might
be one that I favor and that's

01:05:30.290 --> 01:05:32.000
the lens I see things through,

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:34.130
no, I can be versatile, I can shift,

01:05:34.130 --> 01:05:36.660
I can adapt to what this person needs.

01:05:36.660 --> 01:05:39.850
And I think if you have
that, you've got to,

01:05:39.850 --> 01:05:42.230
if you have that, you've
got a winning combination.

01:05:42.230 --> 01:05:44.820
- Great, so people should
perhaps try a few therapists

01:05:44.820 --> 01:05:47.860
and maybe have a session or
two or three to see if they,

01:05:47.860 --> 01:05:49.900
the rapport feels like it's taking root.

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:50.733
Is that?

01:05:50.733 --> 01:05:51.566
- [Paul] Yeah.

01:05:51.566 --> 01:05:52.399
- Do you have that right?

01:05:52.399 --> 01:05:53.232
- Yeah, and I think that's why word

01:05:53.232 --> 01:05:54.065
of mouth is important, right?

01:05:54.065 --> 01:05:54.898
If someone you trust tells you,

01:05:54.898 --> 01:05:57.360
hey, this is a good person
that says a lot, right?

01:05:57.360 --> 01:05:59.680
It already makes the pretest probability,

01:05:59.680 --> 01:06:01.160
is quite high.

01:06:01.160 --> 01:06:03.673
But yes, it's interesting to see

01:06:03.673 --> 01:06:06.070
when like people have a therapist

01:06:06.070 --> 01:06:07.210
or they called their insurance

01:06:07.210 --> 01:06:08.400
and they're assigned a therapist.

01:06:08.400 --> 01:06:09.410
This thought that like, oh,

01:06:09.410 --> 01:06:11.090
that's the person I have to have now.

01:06:11.090 --> 01:06:12.810
And it's like, no, you should look at that

01:06:12.810 --> 01:06:15.330
like anyone you'd be interviewing, right,

01:06:15.330 --> 01:06:17.300
for a job, right?

01:06:17.300 --> 01:06:18.380
But you got to bring again,

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:22.930
the right set of thoughts
to that to be helped, right?

01:06:22.930 --> 01:06:25.460
Which is that I want someone
who has rapport with me.

01:06:25.460 --> 01:06:27.860
I don't want someone who's
going to make it easy, right?

01:06:27.860 --> 01:06:29.640
Who's like, well, it's,
gosh, it's kind of pleasant,

01:06:29.640 --> 01:06:31.030
because then that means
they're not talking

01:06:31.030 --> 01:06:32.450
about the difficult things, right?

01:06:32.450 --> 01:06:34.840
So if one brings, like, I know
this isn't going to be easy.

01:06:34.840 --> 01:06:36.870
I got to talk about
difficult things, right?

01:06:36.870 --> 01:06:38.410
Even if one doesn't recognize

01:06:38.410 --> 01:06:40.380
or I got to talk about
the trauma in me, right?

01:06:40.380 --> 01:06:43.530
But to go to therapy
thinking, no, it's, I mean,

01:06:43.530 --> 01:06:45.920
sometimes it's enjoyable,
but a lot of times, right,

01:06:45.920 --> 01:06:46.820
it's not, right?

01:06:46.820 --> 01:06:47.653
It's hard work.

01:06:47.653 --> 01:06:48.590
It can be excruciating.

01:06:48.590 --> 01:06:50.570
We can cry during it, but to say,

01:06:50.570 --> 01:06:52.130
right, that that's how
I'm going to be helped.

01:06:52.130 --> 01:06:55.730
And I want someone who's
going to do that with me,

01:06:55.730 --> 01:06:58.300
who's really looking at,
what's going on inside of me,

01:06:58.300 --> 01:06:59.320
how do we help me?

01:06:59.320 --> 01:07:02.400
And I can feel sort of
the robustness of that.

01:07:02.400 --> 01:07:05.540
If one brings that approach
and then looks at the therapist

01:07:05.540 --> 01:07:06.790
through that lens,

01:07:06.790 --> 01:07:09.440
you're very likely to
then move on from someone

01:07:09.440 --> 01:07:11.030
who's not a good choice, right?

01:07:11.030 --> 01:07:12.910
And really stick with someone who is,

01:07:12.910 --> 01:07:15.930
even though that doesn't mean
it's always like pleasant

01:07:15.930 --> 01:07:16.763
and enjoyable.

01:07:16.763 --> 01:07:19.127
I mean, it has to not be that sometimes.

01:07:19.127 --> 01:07:20.003
- Right.

01:07:21.020 --> 01:07:22.310
Maybe we could drill a little deeper

01:07:22.310 --> 01:07:24.510
into the mechanics of therapy.

01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:27.830
I put out a few questions to
audience asking what they want

01:07:27.830 --> 01:07:30.060
to know about therapy and it was amazing.

01:07:30.060 --> 01:07:33.590
I got hundreds, if not
thousands of responses saying,

01:07:33.590 --> 01:07:35.070
how should I show up to therapy?

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:35.903
So for instance,

01:07:35.903 --> 01:07:39.270
should people take a five minute
meditative drop in before?

01:07:39.270 --> 01:07:42.453
Or should they just show
up cold and let it emerge.

01:07:43.490 --> 01:07:45.310
During therapy,

01:07:45.310 --> 01:07:46.680
is it a good idea to take notes

01:07:46.680 --> 01:07:50.340
or to not take notes
and then post therapy,

01:07:50.340 --> 01:07:51.770
how should clients,

01:07:51.770 --> 01:07:54.870
patients as they're sometimes
called, one or the other,

01:07:54.870 --> 01:07:55.910
I never know which,

01:07:55.910 --> 01:07:58.330
how should they process that information?

01:07:58.330 --> 01:08:00.840
Should they take some
designated time afterwards

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:02.570
and in an ideal world,

01:08:02.570 --> 01:08:04.920
take a 30 minute walk
afterwards and think about

01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:06.930
the material or should they set it aside

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:07.763
and come back to it?

01:08:07.763 --> 01:08:11.110
Of course there are constraints,
work and family, etcetera.

01:08:11.110 --> 01:08:12.880
But you know we,

01:08:12.880 --> 01:08:14.830
there's a lot of knowledge out
there about how to best show

01:08:14.830 --> 01:08:17.690
up to a workout, warm
up for five, 10 minutes,

01:08:17.690 --> 01:08:19.620
then do this, etcetera
and then the cool down.

01:08:19.620 --> 01:08:22.890
I mean, here, we're talking
about hard psychological work

01:08:22.890 --> 01:08:24.340
aimed at bettering oneself.

01:08:24.340 --> 01:08:26.310
So to my knowledge,

01:08:26.310 --> 01:08:29.420
I've not ever seen this
information anywhere.

01:08:29.420 --> 01:08:31.830
It'd be very useful to hear,
hear your thoughts on this.

01:08:31.830 --> 01:08:32.730
- Yeah.

01:08:32.730 --> 01:08:34.890
Well, I'm not trying to duck the question,

01:08:34.890 --> 01:08:37.810
but I think it varies so much by person.

01:08:37.810 --> 01:08:39.530
So if you think about the
first part of your question,

01:08:39.530 --> 01:08:42.020
I think was how to show
up to therapy, right?

01:08:42.020 --> 01:08:43.720
And I think the answer would be whatever

01:08:43.720 --> 01:08:46.820
lets you be fully present
when you're in therapy.

01:08:46.820 --> 01:08:50.010
Now for some people that's
going to be, I show up early,

01:08:50.010 --> 01:08:52.540
I say it, I call myself,
I meditate a little bit.

01:08:52.540 --> 01:08:54.420
I mean, that's how then
they're present, right?

01:08:54.420 --> 01:08:56.780
For other people, you know,
they just, they show up,

01:08:56.780 --> 01:08:59.170
walk into the room, they can
stop another present, right?

01:08:59.170 --> 01:09:01.470
So it's whatever works for that person.

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:03.670
So that they're really
there, their thoughts,

01:09:03.670 --> 01:09:06.240
their energy is really in what's going on.

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:08.710
And the same thing
applies on the other end.

01:09:08.710 --> 01:09:12.740
There are people who are
really well served by,

01:09:12.740 --> 01:09:14.010
going for a walk if they can,

01:09:14.010 --> 01:09:15.660
or sitting quietly after therapy,

01:09:15.660 --> 01:09:17.180
kind of putting that in order, right?

01:09:17.180 --> 01:09:18.840
Otherwise they lose some of it, right?

01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:20.753
Or like some of the ahas, right?

01:09:20.753 --> 01:09:22.920
Or the, oh, that's an interesting thought

01:09:22.920 --> 01:09:24.290
that they really need to put it in order.

01:09:24.290 --> 01:09:27.540
Maybe that involves taking some
notes during therapy, right?

01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:29.870
For other people, they need
to do the exact opposite.

01:09:29.870 --> 01:09:32.880
They need to like leave,
not think about that at all.

01:09:32.880 --> 01:09:35.560
And then they can reflect on
it later and learn from it.

01:09:35.560 --> 01:09:37.390
So we're so different.

01:09:37.390 --> 01:09:39.770
Human beings, there's
such a diversity in us

01:09:41.224 --> 01:09:43.418
that there's no hard answer to that,

01:09:43.418 --> 01:09:46.040
but it's like being present
when it's happening,

01:09:46.040 --> 01:09:48.580
then being able to sort of consolidate

01:09:49.903 --> 01:09:53.130
and retain what's been
gained is most important.

01:09:53.130 --> 01:09:55.870
And I think we have to figure
that out person by person.

01:09:55.870 --> 01:09:58.110
I mean, I try and do
that in the work of like

01:09:58.110 --> 01:09:59.460
what's serving this person best.

01:09:59.460 --> 01:10:00.670
And sometimes we,

01:10:00.670 --> 01:10:02.780
sometimes it evolves and
sometimes we talk about it,

01:10:02.780 --> 01:10:04.550
but it varies so much.

01:10:04.550 --> 01:10:05.383
- Hmm.

01:10:05.383 --> 01:10:06.980
- If someone were thinking about embarking

01:10:06.980 --> 01:10:08.950
on therapy or more therapy

01:10:08.950 --> 01:10:12.400
to address trauma or just
general issues of life,

01:10:12.400 --> 01:10:14.330
what is the frequency that you recommend?

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:16.320
I could imagine two extreme models.

01:10:16.320 --> 01:10:17.370
One is, okay,

01:10:17.370 --> 01:10:19.410
I'm going to finally tackle this trauma.

01:10:19.410 --> 01:10:22.160
I'm going to do therapy
three times a week,

01:10:22.160 --> 01:10:25.470
but for a shorter period
of time, six months,

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:30.400
over and out versus this open
ended model of once a week,

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:34.400
typically for as long as it takes.

01:10:34.400 --> 01:10:35.840
- Right, right.

01:10:35.840 --> 01:10:38.033
I think that also varies.

01:10:38.890 --> 01:10:41.940
And I work with people
in varied ways from oh,

01:10:41.940 --> 01:10:42.773
someone who's doing well

01:10:42.773 --> 01:10:45.720
and like we meet for a half
hour every six months, right?

01:10:45.720 --> 01:10:47.270
To doing week long,

01:10:47.270 --> 01:10:51.400
hourly sessions, to
spending three intense days

01:10:51.400 --> 01:10:53.160
with someone in a row, right?

01:10:53.160 --> 01:10:58.160
So I think as far as like
kind of guiding principles,

01:10:58.570 --> 01:11:01.080
what I have found in my own life,

01:11:01.080 --> 01:11:05.760
'cause I value my own
therapy tremendously.

01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:08.880
So I found in my own life
and in my own clinical work

01:11:08.880 --> 01:11:11.400
that if it's less than once a week,

01:11:11.400 --> 01:11:14.790
then it's hard for us to retain really.

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:17.750
We spend a lot of time
kind of catching up, okay,

01:11:17.750 --> 01:11:18.583
what's happened?

01:11:18.583 --> 01:11:20.960
Let's get back to the place
we were at before, right?

01:11:20.960 --> 01:11:23.260
Which is why I think if we're
really going to get somewhere,

01:11:23.260 --> 01:11:25.410
we're not just trying to
maintain something, right?

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:27.560
Then I think once a week for an hour

01:11:27.560 --> 01:11:29.423
is really kind of the minimum, right?

01:11:30.290 --> 01:11:31.593
But more intensive work.

01:11:31.593 --> 01:11:35.070
It's like the more I intense it is,

01:11:35.070 --> 01:11:36.190
it's not linear, right?

01:11:36.190 --> 01:11:37.480
It's an exponential gain.

01:11:37.480 --> 01:11:40.800
Like we do a lot of intensive work, right?

01:11:40.800 --> 01:11:44.170
where someone will come
and do 30 clinical hours

01:11:44.170 --> 01:11:46.460
with us over the course of a week.

01:11:46.460 --> 01:11:50.173
So five or six different
clinicians, 30 clinical hours.

01:11:51.121 --> 01:11:52.617
And you know,

01:11:52.617 --> 01:11:55.120
we've found that the benefits
of doing that are immense.

01:11:55.120 --> 01:11:56.480
It's like let's say a year's worth

01:11:56.480 --> 01:11:59.590
of therapy consolidated and
you take well, 30 hours,

01:11:59.590 --> 01:12:01.870
let's say, we go almost every week,

01:12:01.870 --> 01:12:03.940
maybe that's 45 or 50 hours,

01:12:03.940 --> 01:12:07.530
but 30 hours with that kind of intensity

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:09.890
is worth probably 60 hours,

01:12:09.890 --> 01:12:11.800
done in a different way,

01:12:11.800 --> 01:12:15.040
because then it's in us
in an active way, right?

01:12:15.040 --> 01:12:16.800
It's in the therapist in an active way,

01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:18.820
it becomes very, very dynamic.

01:12:18.820 --> 01:12:20.910
So I think turning up the intensity,

01:12:20.910 --> 01:12:23.680
if there's something that
we really need to process,

01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:24.820
absolutely makes sense.

01:12:24.820 --> 01:12:25.910
And I do that in my own life

01:12:25.910 --> 01:12:27.940
is something now's
like, whoa, it's really,

01:12:27.940 --> 01:12:29.590
somebody is really distressing me

01:12:30.562 --> 01:12:32.387
and it's linking into prior trauma

01:12:32.387 --> 01:12:33.240
and I can see what's going on in me.

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:35.310
Now I start to have
ruminative thoughts, you know,

01:12:35.310 --> 01:12:38.170
with negativity, I'm like,
I got to go more, right?

01:12:38.170 --> 01:12:40.190
Because I got to do that processing.

01:12:40.190 --> 01:12:41.990
So I can get to the place that I am,

01:12:41.990 --> 01:12:43.213
which is not that,

01:12:43.213 --> 01:12:45.200
it's not that the trauma
has no impact on me, right?

01:12:45.200 --> 01:12:48.030
It' that the impact is much
less than it was before

01:12:48.030 --> 01:12:51.820
the therapy and that I most often

01:12:51.820 --> 01:12:54.650
and more often than not
have an ability to see

01:12:54.650 --> 01:12:57.410
when it's now intruding into my thoughts.

01:12:57.410 --> 01:12:59.340
And it's taking me away from like

01:12:59.340 --> 01:13:00.610
what I really think and believe,

01:13:00.610 --> 01:13:02.970
or being able to draw
logic and emotion together

01:13:02.970 --> 01:13:04.350
and make good decisions.

01:13:04.350 --> 01:13:08.300
Turning up the intensity
then absolutely makes sense.

01:13:08.300 --> 01:13:13.260
- This very deep, intensive
work of 30 hours in a week.

01:13:13.260 --> 01:13:18.120
What brings somebody to some,
the type of work of that sort?

01:13:18.120 --> 01:13:22.260
Is it a suicide risk or a
severe addiction situation?

01:13:22.260 --> 01:13:24.240
I mean, how does one
gauge how much therapy

01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:29.240
they ought to be doing
and should it always be

01:13:29.450 --> 01:13:32.143
on the therapist to decide that frequency?

01:13:34.410 --> 01:13:36.254
What would bring someone

01:13:36.254 --> 01:13:38.100
to a situation of five
therapists in 30 hours

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:40.070
a week in one week?

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:41.998
- Right, right.

01:13:41.998 --> 01:13:45.720
Yeah, it's usually a person
who is really distressed

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:48.010
by something whether that's,

01:13:48.010 --> 01:13:50.530
it's so negatively impacting their life

01:13:51.490 --> 01:13:53.330
or sometimes a person
comes to realization.

01:13:53.330 --> 01:13:54.700
I just can't take this anymore, right?

01:13:54.700 --> 01:13:56.740
I'm sick of the cyclical depression.

01:13:56.740 --> 01:13:58.090
I got to stop having panic attacks.

01:13:58.090 --> 01:13:59.890
I need help, right?

01:13:59.890 --> 01:14:01.920
But it's usually some,

01:14:01.920 --> 01:14:05.650
crisis point with the idea
of crisis in the meaning of,

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:07.820
okay, something comes
to a head and after it,

01:14:07.820 --> 01:14:08.970
things are going to be different, right?

01:14:08.970 --> 01:14:11.750
Not a crisis and things are
going to be negative afterwards,

01:14:11.750 --> 01:14:15.230
but a point where, where then
that cognitive flexibility

01:14:15.230 --> 01:14:16.063
comes to the fore of like,

01:14:16.063 --> 01:14:18.360
well, I need to do
something different, right?

01:14:18.360 --> 01:14:20.630
So that's often what brings us.

01:14:20.630 --> 01:14:23.140
Sometimes it's other
people pointing it out

01:14:23.140 --> 01:14:26.370
or somebody's had an
intervention somewhere or yes,

01:14:26.370 --> 01:14:29.170
that person's been hospitalized
after a suicide attempt

01:14:29.170 --> 01:14:31.020
or they've gone back to

01:14:31.020 --> 01:14:33.077
rehab again for the third or fourth time.

01:14:33.077 --> 01:14:35.080
And their life is really in danger.

01:14:35.080 --> 01:14:36.050
Sometimes it's that.

01:14:36.050 --> 01:14:39.200
And sometimes it's a person
realizing, yeah, I just want to,

01:14:39.200 --> 01:14:42.230
I want to look at myself, I want
to understand myself better.

01:14:42.230 --> 01:14:44.060
I know that what's going on in me,

01:14:44.060 --> 01:14:46.620
isn't as good as it can be, right?

01:14:46.620 --> 01:14:47.930
So I think people can come to it

01:14:47.930 --> 01:14:50.590
for all sorts of different ways.

01:14:50.590 --> 01:14:51.423
And I think, yes,

01:14:51.423 --> 01:14:54.380
I think a lot of times it
would be the therapist to say,

01:14:54.380 --> 01:14:58.320
more work, more intensive
work or can make a difference.

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:00.990
But I think the person also needs to,

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:02.250
take ownership, right?

01:15:02.250 --> 01:15:03.170
Of their own therapy and say,

01:15:03.170 --> 01:15:04.950
if I don't feel helped enough,

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:06.710
well, I have to think about that, right?

01:15:06.710 --> 01:15:08.450
And talk to the therapist
about that, 'cause it,

01:15:08.450 --> 01:15:10.960
maybe that therapist isn't a match, right?

01:15:10.960 --> 01:15:13.040
Or maybe you talk to the
therapist and the therapist

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:15.660
can change his or her approach, right?

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:18.086
Or maybe you talk to the therapist

01:15:18.086 --> 01:15:19.270
and increase the frequency, right?

01:15:19.270 --> 01:15:22.380
But the idea is to be aware of it, right?

01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:23.350
And if one's needs,

01:15:23.350 --> 01:15:26.397
aren't being met to
acknowledge that, right?

01:15:26.397 --> 01:15:28.440
'Cause people can get
into a rhythm of therapy

01:15:28.440 --> 01:15:30.820
where it's really not helping them, right?

01:15:30.820 --> 01:15:33.240
But they either feel sort
of nihilistic about it.

01:15:33.240 --> 01:15:36.659
Like, oh, I'm no better and
I'm going to therapy, right?

01:15:36.659 --> 01:15:38.730
Or sometimes there's a sense
that while I'm in therapy,

01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:40.780
so I'm kind of checking
that box of doing something

01:15:40.780 --> 01:15:43.340
for myself, but it's not
really getting me anywhere.

01:15:43.340 --> 01:15:45.160
And then the part of the
brain that's controlled

01:15:45.160 --> 01:15:47.200
by the guilt and shame and avoidance

01:15:47.200 --> 01:15:49.030
thinks that's a great idea, right?

01:15:49.030 --> 01:15:51.280
So again, this ability
to observe ourselves

01:15:51.280 --> 01:15:52.490
and like what's going on,

01:15:52.490 --> 01:15:53.950
am I being helped in the way,

01:15:53.950 --> 01:15:55.670
do I feel helped, right?

01:15:55.670 --> 01:15:57.560
Am I in some ways,

01:15:57.560 --> 01:15:59.919
even like happy that
I'm not feeling helped.

01:15:59.919 --> 01:16:00.752
'Cause I don't have to face

01:16:00.752 --> 01:16:02.180
this thing I don't want to face, right?

01:16:02.180 --> 01:16:04.640
Or am I too afraid to say
I need more help, right?

01:16:04.640 --> 01:16:06.653
Do we really need to look at ourselves?

01:16:07.840 --> 01:16:08.673
And this is where the insurance systems

01:16:08.673 --> 01:16:10.441
often are very difficult,

01:16:10.441 --> 01:16:11.550
'cause it's hard sometimes
for a person to say,

01:16:11.550 --> 01:16:14.500
I need more therapy 'cause that
may not be possible, right?

01:16:15.823 --> 01:16:17.650
So there are sort of negative factors

01:16:17.650 --> 01:16:18.700
in the world around us.

01:16:18.700 --> 01:16:22.218
But ultimately I think the
answer to the question comes down

01:16:22.218 --> 01:16:24.200
to observing ourselves
and taking ownership

01:16:24.200 --> 01:16:26.800
of like what's going on in
us and how we're feeling.

01:16:27.757 --> 01:16:28.857
And then feeling that,

01:16:30.480 --> 01:16:32.260
that commitment to self
or to self-care to say,

01:16:32.260 --> 01:16:33.730
I need to go change this.

01:16:33.730 --> 01:16:35.950
- And for those that
maybe don't have the means

01:16:35.950 --> 01:16:39.560
or insurance or access to do even one day

01:16:39.560 --> 01:16:42.490
a week therapy in the journaling model.

01:16:42.490 --> 01:16:43.350
- [Paul] Yes.

01:16:43.350 --> 01:16:46.300
- Could one perhaps take an entire day

01:16:47.445 --> 01:16:49.376
as awful as it might seem,

01:16:49.376 --> 01:16:51.470
to do a lot of journaling
and thinking and walking,

01:16:51.470 --> 01:16:54.420
do a self-generated intensive.

01:16:54.420 --> 01:16:56.473
Do you think there's utility to that?

01:16:57.441 --> 01:16:58.274
- I mean there could be,

01:16:58.274 --> 01:17:00.330
but again it depends by person 'cause

01:17:00.330 --> 01:17:02.120
there could also be
something negative about that

01:17:02.120 --> 01:17:04.920
if it's someone who's not at the point,

01:17:04.920 --> 01:17:05.910
not ready for that, right?

01:17:05.910 --> 01:17:07.483
I mean we don't come at,

01:17:08.367 --> 01:17:10.510
we don't come directly at
the trauma immediately,

01:17:10.510 --> 01:17:13.800
at least most of the time
we don't do that, right?

01:17:13.800 --> 01:17:16.610
And we often don't explore it in depth.

01:17:16.610 --> 01:17:17.610
Like this idea that, oh,

01:17:17.610 --> 01:17:20.340
that person now has to go through
every second of the trauma

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:21.350
is actually not true.

01:17:21.350 --> 01:17:24.100
I mean sometimes it is, but that's,

01:17:24.100 --> 01:17:26.380
that's not the common situation, right?

01:17:26.380 --> 01:17:28.810
So more often that
person has to acknowledge

01:17:28.810 --> 01:17:31.500
like the example of like
I was sexually abused

01:17:32.406 --> 01:17:33.840
and have to acknowledge
that and to, and say,

01:17:33.840 --> 01:17:35.120
okay, like, gosh,

01:17:35.120 --> 01:17:36.410
what has that done to me?

01:17:36.410 --> 01:17:37.243
That doesn't mean,

01:17:37.243 --> 01:17:38.610
well let's parse out every moment

01:17:38.610 --> 01:17:41.600
of like how that was and
the terror of that, right?

01:17:41.600 --> 01:17:44.123
So that can lead people
to a worse place, right?

01:17:45.248 --> 01:17:49.480
So, I think the idea of
biting off small pieces,

01:17:49.480 --> 01:17:51.960
so to speak where a
person is writing, right?

01:17:51.960 --> 01:17:53.070
Or is talking.

01:17:53.070 --> 01:17:54.410
But I think if one is writing,

01:17:54.410 --> 01:17:57.330
it is good to communicate
with another, right?

01:17:57.330 --> 01:17:59.190
Another trusted person.

01:17:59.190 --> 01:18:01.760
And if there's not someone
in one's personal life,

01:18:01.760 --> 01:18:02.680
there are clergy members,

01:18:02.680 --> 01:18:05.649
even if one isn't a affiliated
with an organized religion,

01:18:05.649 --> 01:18:07.340
you could probably go places

01:18:07.340 --> 01:18:09.640
and get clergy to want to help you, right?

01:18:09.640 --> 01:18:10.730
I mean, there are people out there

01:18:10.730 --> 01:18:12.000
who want to help other people.

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:15.090
So we say, what if someone
has no one, I mean,

01:18:15.090 --> 01:18:17.160
almost never do we have
no one here, right?

01:18:17.160 --> 01:18:19.635
'Cause we could probably go find someone,

01:18:19.635 --> 01:18:22.010
but we need to kind of
take that in pieces.

01:18:22.010 --> 01:18:25.430
So there's some risk like trying
to do the intensive thing,

01:18:25.430 --> 01:18:27.080
you know, on one's own.

01:18:27.080 --> 01:18:28.723
And that's where I would put in,

01:18:29.564 --> 01:18:31.000
if a person's having suicidal thoughts

01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:33.620
or even thoughts of death,
of not wanting to be alive,

01:18:33.620 --> 01:18:34.810
I don't deserve to be alive.

01:18:34.810 --> 01:18:37.430
I mean, these are warning
signs for really getting help.

01:18:37.430 --> 01:18:38.890
So there are some signs that say, hey,

01:18:38.890 --> 01:18:40.800
don't try and do that on your own, right?

01:18:40.800 --> 01:18:42.400
Go try and find a resource.

01:18:42.400 --> 01:18:46.220
And it's things that get to
that level of severity of,

01:18:46.220 --> 01:18:47.063
and often a person knows that.

01:18:47.063 --> 01:18:49.450
I mean, am I in a place where I know

01:18:49.450 --> 01:18:50.340
I'm not healthy

01:18:50.340 --> 01:18:53.945
and I'm having kind of scary thoughts,

01:18:53.945 --> 01:18:55.088
then we need,

01:18:55.088 --> 01:18:55.921
that's a person who
really shouldn't be doing

01:18:55.921 --> 01:18:57.080
that on their own.

01:18:57.080 --> 01:18:57.950
- Great, thank you for that.

01:18:57.950 --> 01:18:59.360
- Yeah, you're welcome.

01:18:59.360 --> 01:19:01.420
- So we've been talking
a lot about talking.

01:19:01.420 --> 01:19:02.620
- Huhm.

01:19:02.620 --> 01:19:05.680
- And now I'd like to talk a
little bit about chemistry.

01:19:05.680 --> 01:19:06.513
- Yes.

01:19:06.513 --> 01:19:07.346
- Drugs.

01:19:07.346 --> 01:19:08.179
- [Paul] Yes.

01:19:09.070 --> 01:19:11.641
- So maybe first we could
talk prescription drugs.

01:19:11.641 --> 01:19:13.810
I mean you're a psychiatrist,
so you're approved to,

01:19:13.810 --> 01:19:18.310
and presumably do prescribe
medication where appropriate.

01:19:18.310 --> 01:19:20.330
I mean, this is a vast
landscape of course.

01:19:20.330 --> 01:19:23.340
We've got ADHD and I should just tell you,

01:19:23.340 --> 01:19:26.470
I get more questions about
ADHD and the drugs related

01:19:26.470 --> 01:19:31.290
to ADHD and dopamine than any
other topic, any other topic.

01:19:31.290 --> 01:19:35.470
So there's ADHD, there's
OCD, there's depression,

01:19:35.470 --> 01:19:37.453
there's antidepressants and so forth.

01:19:38.690 --> 01:19:40.710
Is there some way that we can,

01:19:40.710 --> 01:19:42.800
wrap our arms around all of that

01:19:42.800 --> 01:19:45.250
as a way of waiting into this,

01:19:45.250 --> 01:19:48.710
this drug question and just address,

01:19:48.710 --> 01:19:51.280
how does one decide when
medication is useful?

01:19:51.280 --> 01:19:52.810
Because in the end,

01:19:52.810 --> 01:19:54.970
the dissection tool that the psychiatrist

01:19:54.970 --> 01:19:56.830
or therapist has is language.

01:19:56.830 --> 01:19:57.663
And at some point,

01:19:57.663 --> 01:20:00.780
one has to make an assessment
about dopamine or serotonin

01:20:00.780 --> 01:20:02.770
or whether or not a given drug would help.

01:20:02.770 --> 01:20:05.333
And most therapies,

01:20:06.307 --> 01:20:09.220
I believe don't involve putting
someone in a brain scanner.

01:20:09.220 --> 01:20:10.728
And to my knowledge,

01:20:10.728 --> 01:20:12.690
there still is not a very
good blood test to assess,

01:20:12.690 --> 01:20:14.670
oh, is this person's dopamine low or high,

01:20:14.670 --> 01:20:15.960
correct me if I'm wrong.

01:20:15.960 --> 01:20:17.920
And ultimately that,

01:20:17.920 --> 01:20:19.160
and I know there are companies out there,

01:20:19.160 --> 01:20:20.690
so I'm not trying to
undermine those companies.

01:20:20.690 --> 01:20:22.100
But if I happen to do
that in this statement,

01:20:22.100 --> 01:20:24.146
if you take a blood test
and find that your serotonin

01:20:24.146 --> 01:20:26.680
metabolites are low,

01:20:26.680 --> 01:20:29.470
my understanding is it's
possible that you are too low

01:20:29.470 --> 01:20:30.610
in serotonin in the brain,

01:20:30.610 --> 01:20:32.170
but that's a very indirect window

01:20:32.170 --> 01:20:33.680
into what's really going on.

01:20:33.680 --> 01:20:34.513
So how does,

01:20:36.176 --> 01:20:39.510
how do you think about
prescription drugs in the context

01:20:39.510 --> 01:20:44.020
of treating trauma and other
conditions and then maybe

01:20:44.020 --> 01:20:46.490
we'll drill into some of the
more specific conditions?

01:20:46.490 --> 01:20:48.001
- Sure, I mean,

01:20:48.001 --> 01:20:50.150
I would first comment that
right there aren't tests

01:20:50.150 --> 01:20:51.810
for these things.

01:20:51.810 --> 01:20:52.660
And I think the tests for metabolites,

01:20:52.660 --> 01:20:54.530
I mean, things are so different.

01:20:54.530 --> 01:20:58.180
By the time, what we're talking
about has been metabolized,

01:20:58.180 --> 01:21:00.150
often to some very significant extent.

01:21:00.150 --> 01:21:01.030
Left the brain,

01:21:01.030 --> 01:21:02.280
now it's in the peripheral blood

01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:04.550
that we really don't
learn from that, right?

01:21:04.550 --> 01:21:09.310
I think that we tend to over
utilize medicines in this

01:21:09.310 --> 01:21:12.400
country because we have
a healthcare system

01:21:13.652 --> 01:21:16.200
that often that's so based
on throughput that we want to

01:21:16.200 --> 01:21:18.370
polish the hood when there's a
problem in the engine, right?

01:21:18.370 --> 01:21:22.030
So we overutilize medicines
often as an end point, right?

01:21:22.030 --> 01:21:23.730
Oh, we're going to make that
person's depression better

01:21:23.730 --> 01:21:25.460
with an antidepressant.

01:21:25.460 --> 01:21:27.250
Well, I mean maybe, right?

01:21:27.250 --> 01:21:30.790
But most of the time for
the person's depression

01:21:30.790 --> 01:21:32.410
to really get better and stay better,

01:21:32.410 --> 01:21:36.594
they need to unravel what's
driving the depression, right'

01:21:36.594 --> 01:21:38.310
So the first step is I think

01:21:38.310 --> 01:21:39.810
they're cut two steps to it, right?

01:21:39.810 --> 01:21:41.970
The first assessment step is,

01:21:41.970 --> 01:21:44.703
is there a diagnosis that,

01:21:45.877 --> 01:21:47.310
that the vast majority of the time,

01:21:47.310 --> 01:21:50.610
if not sometimes, all the time,
really warrants a medicine?

01:21:50.610 --> 01:21:55.030
So the bipolar disorder, OCD, ADD, right?

01:21:55.030 --> 01:21:57.190
These are diagnoses that we,

01:21:57.190 --> 01:21:59.930
we understand more about
them and what's going on

01:21:59.930 --> 01:22:03.880
in the brain and how medicines
can treat or stabilize them,

01:22:03.880 --> 01:22:05.690
which doesn't mean the
medicine is necessarily,

01:22:05.690 --> 01:22:08.921
it's not a substitute for therapy, right?

01:22:08.921 --> 01:22:11.240
But sometimes the medicine and
therapy can go hand in hand.

01:22:11.240 --> 01:22:15.780
So for OCD, for example,
warrants therapy, but it almost,

01:22:15.780 --> 01:22:18.930
not always, but it almost
always warrants medicine too,

01:22:18.930 --> 01:22:21.350
so that you can ease the
systems that are making

01:22:21.350 --> 01:22:23.870
the rigidity and the
repetition in the brain.

01:22:23.870 --> 01:22:27.010
So the first kind of branch point can be,

01:22:27.010 --> 01:22:28.130
what is the diagnosis?

01:22:28.130 --> 01:22:30.120
What is the level of severity, right?

01:22:30.120 --> 01:22:31.330
And I think that's very,

01:22:31.330 --> 01:22:34.039
very important where I
think it's a little more,

01:22:34.039 --> 01:22:38.650
maybe even interesting is using medicines

01:22:39.630 --> 01:22:42.930
to help the person engage in the therapy

01:22:42.930 --> 01:22:45.290
as productively as possible.

01:22:45.290 --> 01:22:47.590
And here's where I think we're so limited

01:22:49.036 --> 01:22:50.420
by how we categorize medicines

01:22:50.420 --> 01:22:53.750
and this sort of pharmaceutical

01:22:53.750 --> 01:22:57.470
insurance driven medical system

01:22:57.470 --> 01:23:01.840
we have that I think throws
us off in tremendous ways.

01:23:01.840 --> 01:23:04.010
So you think about how
medicines are categorized,

01:23:04.010 --> 01:23:05.800
so antidepressants.

01:23:05.800 --> 01:23:07.720
And the vast majority of people who are

01:23:07.720 --> 01:23:09.270
helped by antidepressants,

01:23:09.270 --> 01:23:10.340
they're not, they don't have

01:23:10.340 --> 01:23:12.490
clinically severe depression, right?

01:23:12.490 --> 01:23:16.610
Those medicines create more
distress tolerance in us, right?

01:23:16.610 --> 01:23:18.980
And if you think about
how helpful that can be,

01:23:18.980 --> 01:23:19.813
if you're going to go,

01:23:19.813 --> 01:23:21.070
now you're going to do
something difficult, right?

01:23:21.070 --> 01:23:23.550
You're going to bring that
trauma or the stressors

01:23:23.550 --> 01:23:25.170
to the surface and you're going to process

01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:27.120
and you're going to try
and make life change.

01:23:27.120 --> 01:23:30.080
If we can make more
distress tolerance in us,

01:23:30.080 --> 01:23:32.840
that can be so, so much better, right?

01:23:32.840 --> 01:23:34.910
And think about the category
of medicines that are called

01:23:34.910 --> 01:23:38.870
antipsychotics, which really
puts people off, right?

01:23:38.870 --> 01:23:41.670
But most of the prescriptions
for antipsychotics

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:43.600
are not for psychosis, right?

01:23:43.600 --> 01:23:47.340
And there are ways in which
low dosing of some of those

01:23:47.340 --> 01:23:51.910
medicines can help intervene
in negative pathways, right?

01:23:51.910 --> 01:23:55.042
In pathways that are about distress.

01:23:55.042 --> 01:23:56.240
And sending out those tendrils

01:23:56.240 --> 01:23:58.530
of neurons that are about hyper vigilance

01:23:59.673 --> 01:24:00.506
and avoidance, right?

01:24:00.506 --> 01:24:01.430
In in our brain.

01:24:01.430 --> 01:24:03.460
And we can often get at that.

01:24:03.460 --> 01:24:05.770
And if you can improve
someone's distress tolerance

01:24:05.770 --> 01:24:07.390
and you can use medicines

01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:10.573
that take away what clinically
is rumination, right?

01:24:11.531 --> 01:24:13.330
Not the standard meaning of that word,

01:24:13.330 --> 01:24:14.860
but the clinical meaning of it,

01:24:14.860 --> 01:24:16.973
where there are distress
centers in our brain

01:24:16.973 --> 01:24:18.827
that are overactive.

01:24:18.827 --> 01:24:22.380
And then we get stuck in these
maladaptive negative pathways

01:24:22.380 --> 01:24:25.220
where we think about something
over and over and over again,

01:24:25.220 --> 01:24:28.000
with no real chance of solving
it because that's not what's

01:24:28.000 --> 01:24:29.580
going on inside of us.

01:24:29.580 --> 01:24:32.030
So medicines can help that,

01:24:32.030 --> 01:24:33.700
but we have to have some flexibility

01:24:33.700 --> 01:24:35.200
around their conception

01:24:36.438 --> 01:24:39.680
and the modern medical system
of like 15 minute visits,

01:24:39.680 --> 01:24:42.360
to a psychiatrist that are weeks apart.

01:24:42.360 --> 01:24:46.090
I mean, I don't understand
how that goes well, right?

01:24:46.090 --> 01:24:47.230
In the vast majority of times,

01:24:47.230 --> 01:24:50.340
I think it doesn't go well
because it's not enough time

01:24:50.340 --> 01:24:52.890
to do the therapy, even
generate the understanding.

01:24:53.804 --> 01:24:55.170
So then medicines get
thrown into the person.

01:24:55.170 --> 01:24:56.210
This is how,

01:24:56.210 --> 01:24:59.700
we use, I think approximately
five times as much medicine,

01:24:59.700 --> 01:25:03.281
I think across the board as say
the Dutch population, right?

01:25:03.281 --> 01:25:05.000
Then you think, well,
why is five times more,

01:25:05.000 --> 01:25:07.530
is a lot more medicine, right?

01:25:07.530 --> 01:25:09.690
And you know, they have
a healthcare system

01:25:09.690 --> 01:25:12.860
and a cultural system that to
the best of my understanding

01:25:12.860 --> 01:25:16.470
is more rooted in taking
responsibility for oneself, right?

01:25:16.470 --> 01:25:19.510
So if a person comes in and
cholesterol is high, right?

01:25:19.510 --> 01:25:21.500
The first order of business is,

01:25:21.500 --> 01:25:23.180
hey, you could take better
care of yourself, right?

01:25:23.180 --> 01:25:24.590
Like this person really needs to lose

01:25:24.590 --> 01:25:26.740
some weight exercise more, right'

01:25:26.740 --> 01:25:28.050
They're not just jumping to like,

01:25:28.050 --> 01:25:29.680
let me give you a medicine and you know,

01:25:29.680 --> 01:25:31.990
and shift you through
the healthcare system

01:25:31.990 --> 01:25:33.870
and out the other side of the door, right?

01:25:33.870 --> 01:25:36.423
And the same thing is
true in mental health,

01:25:37.630 --> 01:25:39.430
and I'm not trying to be critical

01:25:40.375 --> 01:25:42.710
to the psychiatrists or the
nurse practitioners or people

01:25:42.710 --> 01:25:44.570
who are practicing in that way,

01:25:44.570 --> 01:25:47.080
because oftentimes there
is no choice, right?

01:25:47.080 --> 01:25:49.240
If they're working in a
healthcare system that,

01:25:49.240 --> 01:25:54.240
that the standard is highly
spaced or spaced apart,

01:25:54.980 --> 01:25:58.290
15 minute visits, what
alternative is there, right.

01:25:58.290 --> 01:25:59.593
But to look at, okay,

01:25:59.593 --> 01:26:01.820
I'm going to use medicines
because I don't have another

01:26:01.820 --> 01:26:03.540
tool to bring to bear.

01:26:03.540 --> 01:26:06.670
So I think the healthcare system
and its focus on throughput

01:26:06.670 --> 01:26:08.420
and it's short term talk about,

01:26:08.420 --> 01:26:10.299
we talk about short term response, right?

01:26:10.299 --> 01:26:13.780
Short term soothing at the
expense of long-term health.

01:26:13.780 --> 01:26:16.430
And I think that is the metaphor for,

01:26:16.430 --> 01:26:18.750
that applies to our
healthcare system, right?

01:26:18.750 --> 01:26:21.025
Where if we,

01:26:21.025 --> 01:26:23.430
if we are going to try and
treat a symptom in a short term,

01:26:23.430 --> 01:26:25.010
we're going to do it in a 15 minute visit,

01:26:25.010 --> 01:26:26.740
that we're going to do it in a way

01:26:26.740 --> 01:26:28.080
that maybe it soothes a symptom,

01:26:28.080 --> 01:26:31.840
maybe it doesn't, but it
does not get at the problem.

01:26:31.840 --> 01:26:35.000
We need to invest more
resources to get at the problem

01:26:35.000 --> 01:26:37.710
and I think that's
where a sort of protest,

01:26:37.710 --> 01:26:39.740
if people, as a society, we say,

01:26:39.740 --> 01:26:42.590
look, we don't like the way
our healthcare is going.

01:26:42.590 --> 01:26:45.140
Like, we need more focus
on what the actual problems

01:26:45.140 --> 01:26:46.350
are that yes,

01:26:46.350 --> 01:26:48.430
we would spend more money,

01:26:48.430 --> 01:26:50.450
to treating people and
taking care of people

01:26:50.450 --> 01:26:52.120
'cause it's more human time,

01:26:52.120 --> 01:26:56.240
but ultimately about less
suffering, less death, right?

01:26:56.240 --> 01:26:57.900
And ultimately more productivity.

01:26:57.900 --> 01:26:59.410
I think as an economy,

01:26:59.410 --> 01:27:03.106
we would save so much money
if we spend money on the human

01:27:03.106 --> 01:27:05.350
aspects of mental healthcare,

01:27:05.350 --> 01:27:06.950
because people would be more functional.

01:27:06.950 --> 01:27:09.380
They're spending less time
in the hospital, right?

01:27:09.380 --> 01:27:11.563
They're more productive
when they're working.

01:27:11.563 --> 01:27:15.193
There's less entry into the
criminal justice system.

01:27:16.077 --> 01:27:18.960
So I think medicines get overused

01:27:18.960 --> 01:27:21.470
in part for systemic reasons,

01:27:21.470 --> 01:27:23.400
in large part, for systemic reasons.

01:27:23.400 --> 01:27:26.140
And also for some of these
categorization reasons,

01:27:26.140 --> 01:27:28.610
oh, that person meets
some technical criteria

01:27:28.610 --> 01:27:29.443
for depression.

01:27:29.443 --> 01:27:31.380
We got to give them this medicine
instead of really thinking,

01:27:31.380 --> 01:27:33.610
wait, what's going on in this person.

01:27:33.610 --> 01:27:34.960
And I see this over and over again.

01:27:34.960 --> 01:27:36.900
I see one who is on seven medicines

01:27:36.900 --> 01:27:39.013
and they're on seven medicines

01:27:39.013 --> 01:27:40.060
to treat seven different symptoms.

01:27:40.060 --> 01:27:41.250
And now they have side effects

01:27:41.250 --> 01:27:42.340
from all those seven medicines.

01:27:42.340 --> 01:27:44.140
Maybe two of them are to
treat the side effects

01:27:44.140 --> 01:27:45.663
from the other five, right?

01:27:47.149 --> 01:27:48.430
And that's bad, right?

01:27:48.430 --> 01:27:51.120
And if you really get at
what's going on in them,

01:27:51.120 --> 01:27:52.690
now they're doing much better

01:27:52.690 --> 01:27:55.060
and maybe they're on two medicines, right?

01:27:55.060 --> 01:27:58.240
So I dunno if that's a
helpful answer to that.

01:27:58.240 --> 01:28:00.030
- It is, it's a very helpful answer.

01:28:00.030 --> 01:28:02.570
I mean, I think at least in the spheres

01:28:02.570 --> 01:28:03.980
that I run these days,

01:28:03.980 --> 01:28:06.470
I hear a lot of negative
statements about antidepressants.

01:28:06.470 --> 01:28:08.680
I think, I'm old enough to remember

01:28:08.680 --> 01:28:10.557
the book, "Listening to Prozac."

01:28:11.580 --> 01:28:14.110
I remember when Prozac and its

01:28:14.110 --> 01:28:15.797
and things like it
first started showing up

01:28:15.797 --> 01:28:16.850
and the excitement.

01:28:16.850 --> 01:28:20.360
And then nowadays I hear more
about the problems with all

01:28:20.360 --> 01:28:22.300
these drugs and maybe that's just,

01:28:22.300 --> 01:28:24.500
'cause I have arms in
the, both the scientific,

01:28:24.500 --> 01:28:26.040
but also in the kind of wellness community

01:28:26.040 --> 01:28:28.140
where people think a lot
about behavioral change.

01:28:28.140 --> 01:28:30.593
Fortunately I think
that's that they do that.

01:28:31.460 --> 01:28:33.080
But of course these
drugs, as you mentioned,

01:28:33.080 --> 01:28:35.230
can have enormous utility as well.

01:28:35.230 --> 01:28:36.063
- [Paul] Yes.

01:28:36.063 --> 01:28:38.140
- I'd like to just pick up on one theme

01:28:38.140 --> 01:28:40.150
that I haven't heard a
lot about anywhere else,

01:28:40.150 --> 01:28:41.360
which is the short term versus

01:28:41.360 --> 01:28:43.040
the long term use of these drugs.

01:28:43.040 --> 01:28:44.190
'Cause I could imagine,

01:28:45.040 --> 01:28:47.760
someone feeling like they're
finally going to tackle something

01:28:47.760 --> 01:28:50.150
that's been inside them
for a long time either

01:28:50.150 --> 01:28:51.730
because they're really struggling

01:28:51.730 --> 01:28:53.760
or because they're just done with

01:28:53.760 --> 01:28:55.230
not working it through

01:28:57.943 --> 01:29:01.130
and they decide to start a medication

01:29:01.130 --> 01:29:04.110
that would give them higher
levels of distress tolerance

01:29:04.110 --> 01:29:05.700
for a short while.

01:29:05.700 --> 01:29:07.605
I mean, is there anything to say

01:29:07.605 --> 01:29:11.860
that someone couldn't take a
properly prescribed medication

01:29:11.860 --> 01:29:15.920
for a week or for the first
three months of the work?

01:29:15.920 --> 01:29:16.753
- Yes.

01:29:16.753 --> 01:29:17.586
- And then know that they can come off it

01:29:17.586 --> 01:29:20.250
because I think that the black
and white model of, okay,

01:29:20.250 --> 01:29:22.470
you're either going to start
this drug and stay on it forever

01:29:22.470 --> 01:29:24.752
or be taking some drugs forever.

01:29:24.752 --> 01:29:25.971
- [Paul] Right.

01:29:25.971 --> 01:29:27.050
- Or you're not going to take anything.

01:29:27.050 --> 01:29:27.950
I mean, that just seems to,

01:29:27.950 --> 01:29:29.110
life doesn't have,

01:29:29.110 --> 01:29:30.780
does life have to work that way?

01:29:30.780 --> 01:29:31.613
- [Paul] Right.

01:29:31.613 --> 01:29:33.690
Is there a short term use
that can be effective?

01:29:33.690 --> 01:29:35.300
- Yeah, absolutely, yes.

01:29:35.300 --> 01:29:38.720
In American medicine we are
so much better at starting

01:29:38.720 --> 01:29:42.517
medicines than we are at
taking them away, right?

01:29:42.517 --> 01:29:46.010
And part of that I think
is driven by such a strong

01:29:46.010 --> 01:29:47.750
presence of the pharmaceutical industry

01:29:47.750 --> 01:29:49.467
and the pharmaceutical industry

01:29:49.467 --> 01:29:52.820
does a lot of very good things, right?

01:29:52.820 --> 01:29:54.330
But you know,

01:29:54.330 --> 01:29:57.130
there's such thing as too
much of a good thing, right?

01:29:57.130 --> 01:30:00.440
And then as a society, when
something seems positive,

01:30:00.440 --> 01:30:01.970
this I think also is human nature.

01:30:01.970 --> 01:30:03.810
We can overinvest in it, right?

01:30:03.810 --> 01:30:06.580
So you think about when Prozac
and those kinds of medicines

01:30:06.580 --> 01:30:09.130
came out, they were safer medicines,

01:30:09.130 --> 01:30:11.310
they're billed as antidepressants
and the thought was,

01:30:11.310 --> 01:30:13.320
well, they're going to
fix depression, right?

01:30:13.320 --> 01:30:15.940
And it's not how that works, right?

01:30:15.940 --> 01:30:19.170
So if we look at them as tools, right,

01:30:19.170 --> 01:30:22.460
then we can deploy them
sometimes for the longer term

01:30:22.460 --> 01:30:24.240
'cause sometimes that's necessary.

01:30:24.240 --> 01:30:28.010
But absolutely for the
shorter term, absolutely.

01:30:28.010 --> 01:30:31.070
If we thought of Prozac and
those kind of medicines,

01:30:31.070 --> 01:30:33.920
not as, oh they're
antidepressants, we think, look,

01:30:33.920 --> 01:30:35.950
what they do is they,

01:30:35.950 --> 01:30:39.390
they seem to make there be
more serotonin in certain

01:30:39.390 --> 01:30:42.564
circuits that are important
for mood regulation,

01:30:42.564 --> 01:30:45.670
anxiety regulation, distress tolerance.

01:30:45.670 --> 01:30:47.930
So those medicines can
really help somebody

01:30:47.930 --> 01:30:49.520
if they're very severely depressed

01:30:49.520 --> 01:30:51.900
and we want to sort of
get them feeling better.

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:55.550
They can also help someone if
they could use more distress

01:30:55.550 --> 01:30:58.540
tolerance in a discrete
period of time, right?

01:30:58.540 --> 01:31:00.430
When we think about them that way,

01:31:00.430 --> 01:31:03.147
we think about them as
tools that we could apply

01:31:03.147 --> 01:31:04.970
for short term or long term.

01:31:04.970 --> 01:31:07.520
We don't see them as fixes, right?

01:31:07.520 --> 01:31:10.480
And we don't see them as then substitutes

01:31:10.480 --> 01:31:12.380
for the human to human work

01:31:12.380 --> 01:31:13.350
that needs to be done.

01:31:13.350 --> 01:31:15.840
I mean, I've been sort of in my training

01:31:15.840 --> 01:31:17.890
at times in healthcare systems

01:31:17.890 --> 01:31:20.030
and I've seen in many other circumstances

01:31:20.030 --> 01:31:23.580
that look at medicines as
answers and this idea that,

01:31:23.580 --> 01:31:26.070
that person is a, and a lot of times

01:31:26.070 --> 01:31:27.647
there'll be a number, right, right?

01:31:27.647 --> 01:31:29.820
And the number is the diagnosis

01:31:29.820 --> 01:31:32.330
and that number gets this medicine.

01:31:32.330 --> 01:31:35.907
And I'm not sure we could
be more misguided than that

01:31:35.907 --> 01:31:38.023
and that's what leads to adding
medicines, adding medicines,

01:31:38.023 --> 01:31:38.970
it's not working.

01:31:38.970 --> 01:31:40.520
Of course it's not working,

01:31:40.520 --> 01:31:42.520
because no one's really paying
attention to what's going on.

01:31:42.520 --> 01:31:44.990
So add more medicines and then
medicines for the medicines.

01:31:44.990 --> 01:31:47.440
And I mean, we know this is true.

01:31:47.440 --> 01:31:48.860
We know this is true,

01:31:48.860 --> 01:31:51.980
but we haven't had the
wherewithal as a society

01:31:51.980 --> 01:31:54.310
to say like with a lot
of things in society,

01:31:54.310 --> 01:31:56.320
to say like this isn't okay, right?

01:31:56.320 --> 01:31:57.500
I mean, we need more.

01:31:57.500 --> 01:31:59.340
Like give these people
who are trying to help us.

01:31:59.340 --> 01:32:01.050
They need more latitude to help us.

01:32:01.050 --> 01:32:03.520
We need more human to human contact

01:32:03.520 --> 01:32:06.070
to get at what's really going on, and yes,

01:32:06.070 --> 01:32:09.340
that's an investment of
time and energy and money

01:32:09.340 --> 01:32:11.120
in the short term and
sometimes that's money

01:32:11.120 --> 01:32:12.560
from the systems, right?

01:32:12.560 --> 01:32:15.330
But if we do that, my
goodness, look at the,

01:32:15.330 --> 01:32:17.533
look at the payoff of that.

01:32:18.660 --> 01:32:22.141
- What is your thought about anxiety

01:32:22.141 --> 01:32:24.283
and ADHD as a separate phenomena,

01:32:25.310 --> 01:32:27.240
in terms of medication.

01:32:27.240 --> 01:32:30.020
Again, ADHD is the thing

01:32:30.020 --> 01:32:31.920
that seems to come up most in questions.

01:32:31.920 --> 01:32:34.820
I can't tell you the number
of especially students,

01:32:34.820 --> 01:32:37.180
but also young working professionals

01:32:38.410 --> 01:32:40.060
and even people who are

01:32:40.060 --> 01:32:45.000
outside those categories
who are interested or taking

01:32:46.770 --> 01:32:50.870
Ritalin, Adderall, Modafinil
or Armodafinil or Vyvanse,

01:32:50.870 --> 01:32:53.640
because they seem to
struggle focusing without it.

01:32:53.640 --> 01:32:55.190
Or, and I don't know,

01:32:55.190 --> 01:32:57.470
'cause I'm not one of those individuals,

01:32:57.470 --> 01:33:01.590
or because they seem to just
like how well they can focus

01:33:01.590 --> 01:33:03.533
when they do take those compounds.

01:33:05.448 --> 01:33:07.380
And so my understanding is
these compounds mainly increase

01:33:07.380 --> 01:33:09.340
dopaminergic transmission in the brain,

01:33:09.340 --> 01:33:12.440
also adrenaline, epinephrine in the brain.

01:33:12.440 --> 01:33:13.680
So they're more or less stimulants.

01:33:13.680 --> 01:33:15.270
They look a lot like, at least chemically,

01:33:15.270 --> 01:33:17.080
they look a lot like
cocaine and amphetamine,

01:33:17.080 --> 01:33:20.010
although they're not quite
cocaine and amphetamine.

01:33:20.010 --> 01:33:22.230
So should we be concerned about this?

01:33:22.230 --> 01:33:24.623
Is this a different sort of epidemic?

01:33:25.540 --> 01:33:27.840
Can these drugs be used to train the brain

01:33:27.840 --> 01:33:30.870
to focus and then people can
withdraw from these drugs?

01:33:30.870 --> 01:33:33.010
I mean, I think this is a huge topic

01:33:33.010 --> 01:33:35.833
and one that maybe warrants
its own episode entirely,

01:33:36.836 --> 01:33:37.669
but as long as we're on the topic,

01:33:37.669 --> 01:33:40.080
what are your thoughts
about medication for ADHD?

01:33:40.080 --> 01:33:43.350
- Sure, I think medication for ADHD

01:33:43.350 --> 01:33:44.890
can be extremely effective

01:33:44.890 --> 01:33:47.700
and the studies show us that, right?

01:33:47.700 --> 01:33:49.787
They show us that if there is ADD,

01:33:50.650 --> 01:33:53.020
then medication for ADD,

01:33:53.020 --> 01:33:56.200
is very, very helpful and
that's true in youths,

01:33:56.200 --> 01:34:00.780
it seems to be true if adults
have adult ADHD or ADD,

01:34:00.780 --> 01:34:02.193
we kind of know that's true,

01:34:03.505 --> 01:34:05.090
but all attention deficit

01:34:05.090 --> 01:34:07.890
is not Attention Deficit Disorder, right?

01:34:07.890 --> 01:34:11.890
And there we go to the
reflexive 15 minute visits,

01:34:11.890 --> 01:34:13.810
throw medicines at things, right?

01:34:13.810 --> 01:34:17.590
Attention deficit can come
from many, many places.

01:34:17.590 --> 01:34:20.053
And one of them is anxiety, right?

01:34:20.053 --> 01:34:23.870
There's so many other reasons
depression affects attention,

01:34:23.870 --> 01:34:25.460
poor sleep affects attention,

01:34:25.460 --> 01:34:27.320
poor diet can affect attention,

01:34:27.320 --> 01:34:29.750
stress in life can affect attention.

01:34:29.750 --> 01:34:31.520
So, and, and certainly trauma.

01:34:31.520 --> 01:34:32.353
And the thing,

01:34:33.315 --> 01:34:35.909
the problems that trauma spins
off can affect attention.

01:34:35.909 --> 01:34:36.742
So this is really the,

01:34:36.742 --> 01:34:40.695
this is really the truth that
while teaching once about

01:34:40.695 --> 01:34:42.870
medicines and pharmacology,

01:34:42.870 --> 01:34:46.740
I was frustrated about how
the answer to everything

01:34:46.740 --> 01:34:47.890
was like, what medicine do we use?

01:34:47.890 --> 01:34:49.670
What medicine do we
use, as opposed to like,

01:34:49.670 --> 01:34:51.590
this is just one piece of the puzzle.

01:34:51.590 --> 01:34:54.360
And I told an anecdote, which,

01:34:54.360 --> 01:34:56.450
I think it was a clinical anecdote,

01:34:56.450 --> 01:34:57.460
like what do you think is going on?

01:34:57.460 --> 01:35:01.530
And I think that if I
told that to, I dunno,

01:35:01.530 --> 01:35:02.740
middle school students or something,

01:35:02.740 --> 01:35:03.980
they would probably say,

01:35:03.980 --> 01:35:07.410
you just told a story of a
person with a rock in their shoe,

01:35:07.410 --> 01:35:08.540
which is what I, the story

01:35:08.540 --> 01:35:10.423
that I was actually telling, right?

01:35:11.744 --> 01:35:13.240
But several people I was talking to,

01:35:13.240 --> 01:35:15.202
they're physicians, right?

01:35:15.202 --> 01:35:16.660
ADD, right?

01:35:16.660 --> 01:35:17.601
It's like, no,

01:35:17.601 --> 01:35:19.270
every time the person
steps down the rock hurts

01:35:19.270 --> 01:35:21.580
and they're not able to
maintain attention, right?

01:35:21.580 --> 01:35:22.940
Like that's what's going on.

01:35:22.940 --> 01:35:27.100
But we're so programmed
to think about medicines

01:35:27.100 --> 01:35:30.310
and inappropriate use of
ADD medicines, as you said,

01:35:30.310 --> 01:35:32.360
there's dopaminergic impact.

01:35:32.360 --> 01:35:34.550
There's epinephrine,
norepinephrine impact.

01:35:34.550 --> 01:35:36.630
We're affecting what are called

01:35:36.630 --> 01:35:37.463
prefrontal alpha 2 receptors

01:35:37.463 --> 01:35:41.350
that like really need to be
helped if there's real ADD

01:35:41.350 --> 01:35:44.570
but if there isn't, that
is not a good thing to do,

01:35:44.570 --> 01:35:46.640
which is why it is quite fascinating

01:35:46.640 --> 01:35:48.950
that when people have ADD,

01:35:48.950 --> 01:35:52.160
they tolerate generally
stimulants very well,

01:35:52.160 --> 01:35:55.180
without the other problems
that can come of stimulants.

01:35:55.180 --> 01:35:57.670
And again, I don't know, I
don't claim to know why that is,

01:35:57.670 --> 01:35:59.730
but we see that phenomenon.

01:35:59.730 --> 01:36:02.820
But when people are being treated for ADD

01:36:02.820 --> 01:36:04.080
and they don't have ADD,

01:36:04.080 --> 01:36:05.860
which sometimes they
know they don't have ADD,

01:36:05.860 --> 01:36:07.500
but the stimulants make
them function better.

01:36:07.500 --> 01:36:10.030
So they go to somebody
and get the stimulants.

01:36:10.030 --> 01:36:12.100
That's not a good thing to do, right?

01:36:12.100 --> 01:36:15.040
'Cause stimulants, when
they're not needed over time,

01:36:15.040 --> 01:36:16.970
they do affect our physical function.

01:36:16.970 --> 01:36:18.510
They affect our judgment, right?

01:36:18.510 --> 01:36:20.810
There are a lot of negative
things that come from that.

01:36:20.810 --> 01:36:23.739
They can affect the
vigilance inside of us.

01:36:23.739 --> 01:36:26.630
So, yes, it's a valid diagnosis,

01:36:26.630 --> 01:36:30.510
but it gets made when it's
not present very often,

01:36:30.510 --> 01:36:32.460
which we see with a lot of diagnoses

01:36:32.460 --> 01:36:33.630
that you can throw medicine at.

01:36:33.630 --> 01:36:35.800
We see the same thing
with bipolar disorder.

01:36:35.800 --> 01:36:38.480
True bipolar disorder
is extremely important

01:36:38.480 --> 01:36:40.580
to utilize medicines effectively,

01:36:40.580 --> 01:36:43.160
but how many people are
diagnosed with bipolar disorder

01:36:43.160 --> 01:36:46.780
who have, they absolutely
don't have bipolar disorder,

01:36:46.780 --> 01:36:49.600
but it can be a catchall diagnosis

01:36:50.745 --> 01:36:51.578
because there is in a sense,

01:36:51.578 --> 01:36:53.840
"something to do for it," right?

01:36:53.840 --> 01:36:55.550
And you can throw medicine at it, right?

01:36:55.550 --> 01:36:57.650
So I mean, what do we expect, right?

01:36:57.650 --> 01:36:59.793
If we have a healthcare
system where you get 15 minute

01:36:59.793 --> 01:37:01.500
visits with your psychiatrist,

01:37:01.500 --> 01:37:03.460
of course we're going to
throw medicines at everything.

01:37:03.460 --> 01:37:05.090
And then the training paradigms

01:37:05.090 --> 01:37:07.330
are going to look at it through that lens.

01:37:07.330 --> 01:37:09.040
And then very often again,

01:37:09.040 --> 01:37:11.640
I give the example of seeing
somebody on seven medicines.

01:37:11.640 --> 01:37:14.249
I mean the first thought I
have is how many of those

01:37:14.249 --> 01:37:17.060
medicines are actually counterproductive?

01:37:17.060 --> 01:37:19.130
And a lot of the time it's not like, oh,

01:37:19.130 --> 01:37:21.020
every now and then one
is counterproductive.

01:37:21.020 --> 01:37:22.550
No, that's the case.

01:37:22.550 --> 01:37:24.340
That's the case a lot of the time.

01:37:24.340 --> 01:37:26.120
And again, I come back to,

01:37:26.120 --> 01:37:28.710
if we're not putting thought into it,

01:37:28.710 --> 01:37:30.733
what other result would we expect?

01:37:32.020 --> 01:37:34.010
- Thank you for that answer.

01:37:34.010 --> 01:37:37.340
I'm very curious what
constitutes negative effects

01:37:37.340 --> 01:37:38.173
of stimulants.

01:37:38.173 --> 01:37:40.990
So if somebody's taking
Adderall or Ritalin in order

01:37:40.990 --> 01:37:43.190
to work longer hours or focus

01:37:43.190 --> 01:37:44.770
because they have attention deficit,

01:37:44.770 --> 01:37:46.880
but not necessarily ADHD.

01:37:46.880 --> 01:37:48.580
And again, I'm not
recommending anyone do this.

01:37:48.580 --> 01:37:51.310
I've just heard the numbers
that have come back at least

01:37:51.310 --> 01:37:52.880
from surveys and discussions

01:37:52.880 --> 01:37:55.050
with colleagues at Stanford and elsewhere,

01:37:55.050 --> 01:37:58.780
other college campuses that
upwards of 75% of college

01:37:58.780 --> 01:38:02.110
students use semi
regularly, these drugs off,

01:38:02.110 --> 01:38:05.120
not by prescription, just
to study and to learn.

01:38:05.120 --> 01:38:06.170
- [Paul] Yes.

01:38:06.170 --> 01:38:08.450
- I can imagine sleep
issues because people,

01:38:08.450 --> 01:38:09.810
because these are stimulants,

01:38:09.810 --> 01:38:12.940
what sorts of other issues
can they create for people

01:38:12.940 --> 01:38:14.330
problems that they can create?

01:38:14.330 --> 01:38:15.413
- Sure, I mean,

01:38:15.413 --> 01:38:16.850
I think a touchstone maybe

01:38:16.850 --> 01:38:19.416
that's running through
our conversation, right,

01:38:19.416 --> 01:38:21.620
is prioritizing the short term benefit

01:38:21.620 --> 01:38:24.550
over solving a long-term problem, right?

01:38:24.550 --> 01:38:27.060
Which we might say is a human tendency

01:38:27.060 --> 01:38:30.660
and we see it across the
topics that we're discussing.

01:38:30.660 --> 01:38:32.590
So, short-term use of stimulants.

01:38:32.590 --> 01:38:34.070
Sure, people are more alert.

01:38:34.070 --> 01:38:35.660
They can stay awake more,

01:38:35.660 --> 01:38:38.360
they can study more intensely and longer.

01:38:38.360 --> 01:38:39.415
Okay, there's some
short-term benefit of that,

01:38:39.415 --> 01:38:43.420
over there, even there,
there can be problems, right?

01:38:43.420 --> 01:38:44.790
But we can say,

01:38:44.790 --> 01:38:47.030
let's just say for sake of
argument that in the short term,

01:38:47.030 --> 01:38:49.563
there's something to be
gained by doing that, right?

01:38:50.481 --> 01:38:53.460
But oh my goodness,
there's so much that is,

01:38:53.460 --> 01:38:55.210
there's so much risk to that, right?

01:38:55.210 --> 01:38:57.110
And how many times have I seen someone

01:38:57.110 --> 01:38:57.960
who they're doing that

01:38:57.960 --> 01:39:00.180
and they're just doing
that to study, right?

01:39:00.180 --> 01:39:02.870
And now they're addicted
to the amphetamines

01:39:02.870 --> 01:39:06.330
and their behavior changes
and they don't know it.

01:39:06.330 --> 01:39:10.390
Talk about shifting our brain
towards a more defensive,

01:39:10.390 --> 01:39:13.480
sort of suspicious, outward look,

01:39:13.480 --> 01:39:16.110
view of the world that
we see a lot of that.

01:39:16.110 --> 01:39:18.660
So we see judgment impairment,

01:39:18.660 --> 01:39:20.870
we see heightened levels of anxiety.

01:39:20.870 --> 01:39:24.210
We see more impulsivity
in decision-making.

01:39:24.210 --> 01:39:25.530
And sometimes we,

01:39:25.530 --> 01:39:28.220
it can get to the point
of seeing Frank psychosis.

01:39:28.220 --> 01:39:29.660
Now, that's not common,

01:39:29.660 --> 01:39:32.010
but have I seen young people

01:39:32.010 --> 01:39:34.070
who've done exactly what
you're describing, right?

01:39:34.070 --> 01:39:37.380
They're using Adderall or
they're using Ritalin to study.

01:39:37.380 --> 01:39:39.960
And then I see them when they're
coming into the hospital,

01:39:39.960 --> 01:39:42.597
they're screaming about how
someone's trying to hurt them.

01:39:42.597 --> 01:39:45.610
Boy, it's the worst case scenario,

01:39:45.610 --> 01:39:47.960
but it shows like that's
where that can go.

01:39:47.960 --> 01:39:49.900
And how much is there between the,

01:39:49.900 --> 01:39:53.150
oh, I'm just using it to
study and that severe,

01:39:53.150 --> 01:39:56.700
outcome that is actually
quite negative for a person

01:39:56.700 --> 01:39:57.900
and it might change how they think

01:39:57.900 --> 01:40:00.280
about that friendship or
that relationship, right?

01:40:00.280 --> 01:40:03.660
A lot negative happens
when we change our brains

01:40:03.660 --> 01:40:05.310
without an ability to see like,

01:40:05.310 --> 01:40:06.700
what is it actually doing to us?

01:40:06.700 --> 01:40:09.570
So, which is part of my whole
theme about trauma, right?

01:40:09.570 --> 01:40:12.420
It changes our brains and
we don't know it, right?

01:40:12.420 --> 01:40:14.636
Well the same can be,

01:40:14.636 --> 01:40:17.920
the same is often true of
amphetamines used inappropriately.

01:40:17.920 --> 01:40:19.283
It shifts our brain.

01:40:20.399 --> 01:40:22.270
And we don't realize that we're
a little bit more impulsive

01:40:22.270 --> 01:40:24.780
in our decision making, a
little bit less trusting.

01:40:24.780 --> 01:40:26.770
These are significant negative things

01:40:26.770 --> 01:40:27.877
that if we don't know it,

01:40:27.877 --> 01:40:29.560
the person will just say, yeah,

01:40:29.560 --> 01:40:30.690
oh, I'm just using it to study.

01:40:30.690 --> 01:40:32.420
I'm using it to work more.

01:40:32.420 --> 01:40:33.940
That's not, you know,

01:40:33.940 --> 01:40:36.703
that's not without it's
high level of risk.

01:40:37.730 --> 01:40:39.630
- What are your thoughts on cannabis?

01:40:39.630 --> 01:40:41.580
I've said it many times on this podcast

01:40:41.580 --> 01:40:42.530
before and I'll say again,

01:40:42.530 --> 01:40:45.020
I feel fortunate that I've
never really been attracted

01:40:45.020 --> 01:40:47.607
to alcohol or drugs of any kind ,

01:40:48.508 --> 01:40:52.430
so much so that if all the
alcohol and all the marijuana

01:40:52.430 --> 01:40:55.870
and all the cocaine
amphetamine disappeared,

01:40:55.870 --> 01:40:58.373
I wouldn't notice any
change in my life, right?

01:40:59.362 --> 01:41:00.240
And I feel lucky in that way,

01:41:00.240 --> 01:41:01.940
'cause I know a lot of people feel

01:41:03.120 --> 01:41:04.684
an attraction to these things

01:41:04.684 --> 01:41:05.623
that it is almost a gravitational force.

01:41:05.623 --> 01:41:06.456
- [Paul] Yes.

01:41:06.456 --> 01:41:08.370
- From their first drink, they just feel,

01:41:08.370 --> 01:41:09.950
I once heard it described in this,

01:41:09.950 --> 01:41:12.180
I think it was an Augusten
Burroughs book, "Dry"

01:41:12.180 --> 01:41:13.390
where he was an alcoholic.

01:41:13.390 --> 01:41:15.410
He said that the first drink he had,

01:41:15.410 --> 01:41:17.250
it felt like this magic elixir

01:41:17.250 --> 01:41:20.380
that meshed with the
physiology of his blood

01:41:20.380 --> 01:41:21.690
in the most seamless way

01:41:21.690 --> 01:41:22.860
and as I was reading this, I thought,

01:41:22.860 --> 01:41:25.070
oh my goodness, first of all,

01:41:25.070 --> 01:41:27.290
that's the most foreign experience

01:41:27.290 --> 01:41:29.050
for me in terms of alcohol.

01:41:29.050 --> 01:41:31.980
And second gosh, that must be terrible.

01:41:31.980 --> 01:41:32.813
And you can,

01:41:32.813 --> 01:41:34.450
but at the same time you
could really understand

01:41:34.450 --> 01:41:36.030
why someone would be drawn to that.

01:41:36.030 --> 01:41:37.041
- Yes.

01:41:37.041 --> 01:41:39.190
So cannabis nowadays is legal

01:41:39.190 --> 01:41:41.410
or decriminalized in
many areas of the U.S.

01:41:42.370 --> 01:41:45.313
A lot of people seem to use the argument,

01:41:46.670 --> 01:41:47.920
it's better than drinking

01:41:49.894 --> 01:41:53.113
or they only do it for
sleep or anxiety management.

01:41:54.623 --> 01:41:57.670
I'm not looking to demonize
or support the cannabis.

01:41:57.670 --> 01:41:59.220
So what are your thoughts about cannabis

01:41:59.220 --> 01:42:01.390
for anxiety management, depression?

01:42:01.390 --> 01:42:03.770
And maybe even for ADHD for that matter.

01:42:03.770 --> 01:42:04.603
Sure.

01:42:04.603 --> 01:42:07.880
- If I could make an
alcohol comment, right?

01:42:07.880 --> 01:42:10.030
The number of times I've seen alcohol

01:42:10.030 --> 01:42:12.390
like having been a good idea for coping

01:42:12.390 --> 01:42:14.813
with something approaches zero, right?

01:42:14.813 --> 01:42:18.950
Like the alcohol for
coping is just never good.

01:42:18.950 --> 01:42:22.120
And there's an additional risk
factor that there's certain

01:42:22.120 --> 01:42:26.440
genetic profiles where people
respond strongly to alcohol.

01:42:26.440 --> 01:42:28.130
Like, as you're saying, it's not just,

01:42:28.130 --> 01:42:31.420
oh, there's a little bit of
short term relief of distress,

01:42:31.420 --> 01:42:33.420
but there's this sort of euphoric response

01:42:33.420 --> 01:42:34.930
and those genetics,

01:42:34.930 --> 01:42:36.330
we don't understand them completely.

01:42:36.330 --> 01:42:39.570
They seem to be in Northern
European populations,

01:42:39.570 --> 01:42:42.210
more prevalent as you head
west in Northern Europe.

01:42:42.210 --> 01:42:45.660
So we understand where risk
factors are demographically,

01:42:45.660 --> 01:42:47.950
but we can't pinpoint
that for any one person.

01:42:47.950 --> 01:42:50.020
And there's a tremendous risk of that,

01:42:50.020 --> 01:42:53.460
when a person responds so
strongly to alcohol or habituates

01:42:53.460 --> 01:42:54.663
coping to alcohol.

01:42:55.510 --> 01:42:57.500
Cannabis is a little bit
of a different story.

01:42:57.500 --> 01:43:00.690
I mean, how I have seen that play out,

01:43:00.690 --> 01:43:02.840
and again, this isn't
coming from any expertise

01:43:02.840 --> 01:43:06.427
around the neuro the
neuropharmacology of it,

01:43:06.427 --> 01:43:08.230
like how is this really
working in the brain?

01:43:08.230 --> 01:43:09.670
But it comes from an observation

01:43:09.670 --> 01:43:14.180
that what it seems to do is
to narrow our attentional

01:43:14.180 --> 01:43:15.220
perspective, right?

01:43:15.220 --> 01:43:17.410
So it's why people will
say, well, they want to,

01:43:17.410 --> 01:43:18.410
they want to use cannabis

01:43:18.410 --> 01:43:20.080
before like watching a movie with friends

01:43:20.080 --> 01:43:20.913
or something, right?

01:43:20.913 --> 01:43:22.090
And, and I think, okay,

01:43:22.090 --> 01:43:24.210
I think why people are doing that

01:43:24.210 --> 01:43:27.320
is because our cognitive spectrum narrows.

01:43:27.320 --> 01:43:29.470
And then instead of worrying
about that thing at work

01:43:29.470 --> 01:43:32.630
or that relationship issue,
one can just be present, right?

01:43:32.630 --> 01:43:36.450
It gates out other
attentional intrusions, right?

01:43:36.450 --> 01:43:38.320
So in some ways, I mean,

01:43:38.320 --> 01:43:40.670
I've absolutely seen it
be helpful to people.

01:43:40.670 --> 01:43:44.270
I mean, it's been legalized
in Oregon, which is where my,

01:43:44.270 --> 01:43:45.337
I spent a lot of my time

01:43:45.337 --> 01:43:47.387
and it's not where all of my practice is,

01:43:48.419 --> 01:43:50.290
but what I have seen is
it is at times helpful,

01:43:50.290 --> 01:43:51.500
safe around sleep, right?

01:43:51.500 --> 01:43:54.250
Because a person can gate
out other intrusive thoughts

01:43:54.250 --> 01:43:56.360
and they can just relax and go to sleep.

01:43:56.360 --> 01:43:58.830
But there can be another side of that too,

01:43:58.830 --> 01:44:01.090
that at higher levels of distress,

01:44:01.090 --> 01:44:03.080
at higher levels of tension,

01:44:03.080 --> 01:44:05.660
what it can do is narrow the focus

01:44:05.660 --> 01:44:08.603
of cognition to the thing
that is negative, right?

01:44:09.540 --> 01:44:13.270
So the idea that, oh, like,
oh, this is a treatment for,

01:44:13.270 --> 01:44:17.010
depression, anxiety,
trauma, is not true, right?

01:44:17.010 --> 01:44:19.840
Can it be helpful under
certain circumstances?

01:44:19.840 --> 01:44:21.460
Like I think the answer to that is, yeah.

01:44:21.460 --> 01:44:22.700
I mean, I know the answer to that is yes.

01:44:22.700 --> 01:44:24.870
'Cause I've seen it play
out clinically that way,

01:44:24.870 --> 01:44:27.290
but think it can also be harmful too.

01:44:27.290 --> 01:44:30.230
So there, again, like
anything that has any power,

01:44:30.230 --> 01:44:32.090
power to influence our brains,

01:44:32.090 --> 01:44:34.063
we want to be thoughtful
and careful about it.

01:44:34.063 --> 01:44:36.710
I mean, do I think that
it's safer than alcohol?

01:44:36.710 --> 01:44:38.340
Yes, I mean, I mean, like we,

01:44:38.340 --> 01:44:39.970
we so clearly see that.

01:44:39.970 --> 01:44:40.984
Does that mean?

01:44:40.984 --> 01:44:42.300
Or it's just uniformly safe?

01:44:42.300 --> 01:44:43.360
No, right.

01:44:43.360 --> 01:44:45.700
So we want to be respectful of anything

01:44:45.700 --> 01:44:47.827
that can change how our brain is working

01:44:47.827 --> 01:44:49.090
and I think that includes,

01:44:49.090 --> 01:44:50.340
certainly includes alcohol.

01:44:50.340 --> 01:44:52.690
And I think it certainly
includes cannabis too.

01:44:53.670 --> 01:44:58.430
- I'd love to talk about
psychedelics for two reasons.

01:44:58.430 --> 01:45:01.470
One, there seems to be a
tremendous amount of interest

01:45:01.470 --> 01:45:04.600
in psychedelics as a
therapeutic clinical tool.

01:45:04.600 --> 01:45:07.220
I know there's also recreational
use and I'll just preface

01:45:07.220 --> 01:45:12.220
all this by saying that
my stance is we absolutely

01:45:12.380 --> 01:45:14.640
know for sure that these
are controlled substances,

01:45:14.640 --> 01:45:15.700
they're illegal to possess,

01:45:15.700 --> 01:45:18.780
sell or use in most of the country.

01:45:18.780 --> 01:45:21.760
There are few areas where
it, they are decriminalized.

01:45:21.760 --> 01:45:23.070
- [Paul] Yes.

01:45:23.070 --> 01:45:25.680
And psychedelics is a
broad category, of course.

01:45:25.680 --> 01:45:28.580
And we can touch on some of
the different, different ones,

01:45:30.250 --> 01:45:32.250
but whereas five years or so,

01:45:32.250 --> 01:45:34.930
five years ago or so I
was truly afraid to say

01:45:34.930 --> 01:45:37.433
the word psychedelics in
any kind of public venue,

01:45:38.400 --> 01:45:41.440
there are laboratories at
Stanford working on ketamine,

01:45:41.440 --> 01:45:45.560
psilocybin, MDMA, mostly in animal models.

01:45:45.560 --> 01:45:48.380
There's terrific work going on

01:45:48.380 --> 01:45:50.460
at Johns Hopkins University
School of Medicine,

01:45:50.460 --> 01:45:51.590
Matthew Johnson's Lab,

01:45:51.590 --> 01:45:53.760
and others looking at the
clinical applications,

01:45:53.760 --> 01:45:56.370
mainly of high dose psilocybin and LSD.

01:45:56.370 --> 01:45:58.316
There's the maps trials with MDMA.

01:45:58.316 --> 01:45:59.149
- [Paul] Yes.

01:45:59.149 --> 01:46:01.780
- So nowadays it's safe
for an academic like me

01:46:01.780 --> 01:46:03.000
to say the word psychedelics.

01:46:03.000 --> 01:46:05.070
And I'm, I'd love to
approach this question

01:46:05.070 --> 01:46:06.630
of psychedelics from a place

01:46:06.630 --> 01:46:08.790
of true exploration and curiosity.

01:46:08.790 --> 01:46:12.270
But with the preface that
we're talking about this

01:46:12.270 --> 01:46:14.740
in a legal clinical setting.

01:46:14.740 --> 01:46:16.014
- [Paul] Yes.

01:46:16.014 --> 01:46:19.200
- And the legality is something
that's now in process.

01:46:19.200 --> 01:46:22.300
I don't think it's completed,
but that's my understanding,

01:46:22.300 --> 01:46:24.150
but there are trials.

01:46:24.150 --> 01:46:29.120
You can go to clinical
trials.gov and put in MDMA,

01:46:29.120 --> 01:46:30.770
and you'll see a bunch of clinical trials

01:46:30.770 --> 01:46:33.630
that are happening in
the recruiting subjects.

01:46:33.630 --> 01:46:36.520
So I think it's safe to
have the conversation now,

01:46:36.520 --> 01:46:38.910
and I'd love your thoughts
about psychedelics.

01:46:38.910 --> 01:46:43.520
Maybe we could start
with psilocybin and LSD

01:46:43.520 --> 01:46:45.240
as a broad category of drugs,

01:46:45.240 --> 01:46:47.190
that at least my
understanding is they touch on

01:46:47.190 --> 01:46:49.390
mainly the serotonin system,

01:46:49.390 --> 01:46:52.313
some specific receptor
activation and modulation,

01:46:53.505 --> 01:46:55.990
tend to change notions of space and time,

01:46:55.990 --> 01:46:57.550
adjust internal state.

01:46:57.550 --> 01:46:58.767
Maybe we would start there.

01:46:58.767 --> 01:46:59.803
- [Paul] Yes.

01:46:59.803 --> 01:47:00.790
- And then maybe venture
into some of the other ones.

01:47:00.790 --> 01:47:03.120
So what are your thoughts on
these drugs for therapeutic

01:47:03.120 --> 01:47:07.870
potential also potential
hazards, etcetera.

01:47:07.870 --> 01:47:10.380
- Yeah, I think if we look
at the true psychedelics,

01:47:10.380 --> 01:47:12.190
so psilocybin and LSD, right?

01:47:12.190 --> 01:47:14.020
Because ketamine and MDMA,

01:47:14.020 --> 01:47:15.640
they're different categories of medicine.

01:47:15.640 --> 01:47:19.070
They're these sort of novel
tools to bring to bear.

01:47:19.070 --> 01:47:23.010
But if we start with psilocybin,
LSD, true psychedelics,

01:47:23.010 --> 01:47:25.010
I think why it is,

01:47:25.010 --> 01:47:28.370
why they have gained so much momentum over

01:47:28.370 --> 01:47:31.690
the last several years is
because the data coming

01:47:31.690 --> 01:47:35.670
from the labs and the academic centers

01:47:35.670 --> 01:47:38.080
is so powerfully positive.

01:47:38.080 --> 01:47:39.080
And as someone who's,

01:47:39.080 --> 01:47:40.730
I'm interested in anything

01:47:40.730 --> 01:47:42.680
that's potentially helpful, right?

01:47:42.680 --> 01:47:44.530
And I want to learn and understand that

01:47:44.530 --> 01:47:47.568
because a lot of things that
are potentially helpful,

01:47:47.568 --> 01:47:48.401
you know, you go and look at the data

01:47:48.401 --> 01:47:50.830
and you see that that's not
helpful, that's harmful.

01:47:50.830 --> 01:47:54.861
I think what we have
seen with psychedelics

01:47:54.861 --> 01:47:56.870
is that they're so helpful, right?

01:47:56.870 --> 01:47:58.630
And the trials are bearing that out.

01:47:58.630 --> 01:48:01.130
And of course these are
used in professional hands

01:48:01.130 --> 01:48:02.527
and with the right kind of guidance

01:48:02.527 --> 01:48:04.350
are extremely powerful tools,

01:48:04.350 --> 01:48:05.940
but used in the right way

01:48:07.002 --> 01:48:09.491
by someone who knows how to utilize them

01:48:09.491 --> 01:48:13.150
in the right setting can have
an immense positive impact.

01:48:13.150 --> 01:48:14.940
And that's why I think

01:48:14.940 --> 01:48:18.370
that the thought is there
across people and more and more

01:48:18.370 --> 01:48:21.243
people feel comfortable saying
it and talking about it,

01:48:21.243 --> 01:48:23.090
I mean we're in the state of Oregon now

01:48:23.090 --> 01:48:24.920
where the thought is,

01:48:24.920 --> 01:48:27.490
we're moving towards
legalization of psilocybin

01:48:27.490 --> 01:48:29.800
early in 2023.

01:48:29.800 --> 01:48:32.290
And it's part the new data, right?

01:48:32.290 --> 01:48:34.670
And how it meshes with
the older data, right?

01:48:34.670 --> 01:48:37.530
How it meshes with data
from the 60s and 70s

01:48:37.530 --> 01:48:40.150
that showed such a strong, powerful impact

01:48:40.150 --> 01:48:41.300
of these medicines.

01:48:41.300 --> 01:48:43.825
And I have a whole set of
thoughts about what's happening

01:48:43.825 --> 01:48:46.403
there and they're just
their conjectures, right?

01:48:47.363 --> 01:48:49.200
But my read of, you know,

01:48:49.200 --> 01:48:51.820
as best I can try and
understand the neuroscience

01:48:51.820 --> 01:48:55.833
and the clinical applicability
and the changes is,

01:48:56.720 --> 01:48:59.630
what happens is we see less communication

01:48:59.630 --> 01:49:02.897
or less chatter in the outer
parts of the brain, right?

01:49:02.897 --> 01:49:04.740
the the outer parts of the cortex.

01:49:04.740 --> 01:49:07.690
And I think that as human beings,

01:49:07.690 --> 01:49:12.510
we sort of glorify the parts
of the brain that only we have.

01:49:12.510 --> 01:49:15.079
I mean, certainly in my growing up, right?

01:49:15.079 --> 01:49:16.110
I mean, what did I learn?

01:49:16.110 --> 01:49:16.950
Even if you think about like,

01:49:16.950 --> 01:49:18.880
learning about the brain
in high school, right?

01:49:18.880 --> 01:49:20.110
I learned that like, wow,

01:49:20.110 --> 01:49:22.447
we're great as humans
'cause we have language

01:49:22.447 --> 01:49:24.010
and other animals don't

01:49:24.010 --> 01:49:26.660
and we can use tools and
like aren't we so great

01:49:26.660 --> 01:49:29.120
because we have this part of
the brain that other animals

01:49:29.120 --> 01:49:31.713
don't and it lets us function, right?

01:49:32.640 --> 01:49:34.569
Okay, there's some truth to that.

01:49:34.569 --> 01:49:37.693
That we can do things others can't do.

01:49:40.092 --> 01:49:43.210
But we get lost often in the
outer parts of the cortex,

01:49:43.210 --> 01:49:46.360
which I think are about survival, right?

01:49:46.360 --> 01:49:47.620
So we come back to the things you

01:49:47.620 --> 01:49:49.740
and I talked about early on of like,

01:49:49.740 --> 01:49:51.233
why are these trauma
mechanisms in us, right?

01:49:51.233 --> 01:49:53.130
So like so much of what's going on

01:49:53.130 --> 01:49:54.970
in our brains is about survival.

01:49:54.970 --> 01:49:59.070
And I think living so to
speak in the cortex, right?

01:49:59.070 --> 01:50:00.630
And the outer part of the brain

01:50:00.630 --> 01:50:03.550
is consistent with a focus on survival.

01:50:03.550 --> 01:50:05.110
So if you think that's where language is,

01:50:05.110 --> 01:50:06.440
that's where vision is,

01:50:06.440 --> 01:50:08.040
that's where executive function is.

01:50:08.040 --> 01:50:11.470
So planning and task execution,

01:50:11.470 --> 01:50:14.430
so much of that is about making our way

01:50:14.430 --> 01:50:16.440
in the world around us.

01:50:16.440 --> 01:50:18.490
So we tend to glorify
that and think, well,

01:50:18.490 --> 01:50:21.410
that's in a sense where
our existence is, right?

01:50:21.410 --> 01:50:24.160
And I believe that is not true, right?

01:50:24.160 --> 01:50:26.920
And again, can I say that for
sure, of course not, right?

01:50:26.920 --> 01:50:29.440
But my read of 20 years
of doing clinical work

01:50:29.440 --> 01:50:31.410
and thinking about all sorts of medicines

01:50:31.410 --> 01:50:34.190
and thinking about the psychedelics

01:50:34.190 --> 01:50:35.760
in a lot of depth,

01:50:35.760 --> 01:50:37.570
I think that what they do is they take us

01:50:37.570 --> 01:50:38.723
out of the cortex, right?

01:50:38.723 --> 01:50:41.360
Because that's where we
run into these problems.

01:50:41.360 --> 01:50:43.443
That's where we bounce
things over and over again

01:50:43.443 --> 01:50:45.597
that the distress centers deepen our brain

01:50:45.597 --> 01:50:49.820
and the brain stem kind of
align with the outer parts

01:50:49.820 --> 01:50:51.590
of the cortex and they say, right,

01:50:51.590 --> 01:50:52.710
we we're in distress.

01:50:52.710 --> 01:50:54.210
We want to stay alive.

01:50:54.210 --> 01:50:57.490
Often a lot of us have had
trauma that makes these changes

01:50:57.490 --> 01:50:59.820
in the brain and then we're
thinking all the time,

01:50:59.820 --> 01:51:02.640
like what would I do
if, if there were war,

01:51:02.640 --> 01:51:05.511
what would I do if there's
civil war, if someone bombs us,

01:51:05.511 --> 01:51:07.780
what will I do if the
economy collapses, right?

01:51:07.780 --> 01:51:09.410
What will I do if somebody gets sick?

01:51:09.410 --> 01:51:11.970
We're thinking all this future projection

01:51:13.127 --> 01:51:15.670
that is all coming from
a place of fear, right?

01:51:15.670 --> 01:51:18.097
It's all coming from a
desire to think about things

01:51:18.097 --> 01:51:20.720
and control the future
with this part of the brain

01:51:20.720 --> 01:51:23.710
that is so uniquely human, right?

01:51:23.710 --> 01:51:26.250
And I think when we take
the neuro transmission

01:51:26.250 --> 01:51:28.140
out of those places, right?

01:51:28.140 --> 01:51:30.170
And we set it in a part of the brain

01:51:30.170 --> 01:51:32.220
and say the insular cortex, right?

01:51:32.220 --> 01:51:33.280
The parts of the brain

01:51:33.280 --> 01:51:35.008
that are sort of in the middle, right?

01:51:35.008 --> 01:51:39.450
Which I think, I believe is
where our humanness really is.

01:51:39.450 --> 01:51:42.590
So the psychedelics make there
be less chatter communication

01:51:42.590 --> 01:51:44.457
in these other parts of the brain.

01:51:44.457 --> 01:51:48.010
And then we become seated
in the part of the brain

01:51:48.010 --> 01:51:51.150
that I believe is most
about our experience

01:51:51.150 --> 01:51:52.630
of true humanness,

01:51:52.630 --> 01:51:55.010
which is why, when you read about,

01:51:55.010 --> 01:51:56.877
people who have experiences
and I've heard about them

01:51:56.877 --> 01:51:59.436
and people talk to me about this, right?

01:51:59.436 --> 01:52:00.269
They've utilized it.

01:52:00.269 --> 01:52:01.310
They talk with me.

01:52:01.310 --> 01:52:03.300
So whether it's someone
telling me their story

01:52:03.300 --> 01:52:05.630
or it's coming from research data,

01:52:05.630 --> 01:52:08.610
it's why people can sort
of see with clarity that,

01:52:08.610 --> 01:52:13.610
oh, that trauma, that thing
is not my fault, right?

01:52:13.929 --> 01:52:15.230
Like we feel a sense of
compassion for ourselves.

01:52:15.230 --> 01:52:18.010
We relieve ourselves,
release ourselves from guilt

01:52:18.010 --> 01:52:21.410
and you say, why is this
so helpful to people?

01:52:21.410 --> 01:52:24.210
And I think it's because it can do

01:52:24.210 --> 01:52:27.130
what we are trying to
get at in good therapy,

01:52:27.130 --> 01:52:30.810
but it can really catalyze
that by just putting a person

01:52:30.810 --> 01:52:33.390
in that part of the brain that can see it

01:52:33.390 --> 01:52:34.630
for what it is,

01:52:34.630 --> 01:52:37.250
without all that chatter
in the cortex about,

01:52:37.250 --> 01:52:39.088
hey, got to think it's your
father, you won't avoid it again

01:52:39.088 --> 01:52:40.990
and that makes the repetition compulsion.

01:52:40.990 --> 01:52:42.467
How do I think ahead to the
next thing that might happen

01:52:42.467 --> 01:52:43.940
and what else bad might happen?

01:52:43.940 --> 01:52:46.430
I mean, we don't get anywhere doing that.

01:52:46.430 --> 01:52:49.860
And I think where we get somewhere
is when we seed ourselves

01:52:49.860 --> 01:52:52.280
deeper in the brain,
which I think we do if,

01:52:52.280 --> 01:52:54.560
if we're like doing really
good therapy and we're,

01:52:54.560 --> 01:52:57.760
we're in the deep parts
of the brain, but these,

01:52:57.760 --> 01:52:59.390
these psychedelics,

01:52:59.390 --> 01:53:02.780
the medicinal value I believe
is putting us in that part

01:53:02.780 --> 01:53:05.580
of the brain where a person
can really find truth.

01:53:05.580 --> 01:53:07.790
And that's why I think that,

01:53:07.790 --> 01:53:10.350
that's come so far in these few years

01:53:10.350 --> 01:53:14.340
because I think that is
very clinically evident.

01:53:14.340 --> 01:53:16.810
And I think we're going to
see more and more of the value

01:53:16.810 --> 01:53:21.170
of that and how, what the
psychedelics do can become,

01:53:21.170 --> 01:53:23.920
I believe a heuristic for
understanding like wait,

01:53:23.920 --> 01:53:26.620
how are our brains really functioning?

01:53:26.620 --> 01:53:28.680
And what are the parts that really matter

01:53:28.680 --> 01:53:31.560
to our experience of being human?

01:53:31.560 --> 01:53:32.717
It's those parts of the brain, right?

01:53:32.717 --> 01:53:35.880
The deep parts of the
brain, the insular cortex

01:53:35.880 --> 01:53:39.210
and the areas around it that
say light up when a person

01:53:39.210 --> 01:53:42.730
has an experience of spiritual ecstasy

01:53:42.730 --> 01:53:45.743
or an experience of connection
with another person, right?

01:53:46.892 --> 01:53:49.040
We kind of have these telltale
markers that something

01:53:49.040 --> 01:53:52.300
is going on there that's very
important and very special.

01:53:52.300 --> 01:53:54.620
And I think we're more
attracted to the outer parts

01:53:54.620 --> 01:53:56.743
of the brain in part 'cause
they're easier to study, right?

01:53:56.743 --> 01:53:58.690
I mean, as you know, better than I do,

01:53:58.690 --> 01:54:01.210
we started studying the brain
through lesion studies, right?

01:54:01.210 --> 01:54:02.043
'Cause it was easy to,

01:54:02.043 --> 01:54:04.190
or to see if a person got
hurt in this part of the brain

01:54:04.190 --> 01:54:06.810
or had a stroke in that part
of the brain, what changes?

01:54:06.810 --> 01:54:08.650
So we look at the cortex 'cause one,

01:54:08.650 --> 01:54:11.590
it's easier to study and
we tend to glorify it.

01:54:11.590 --> 01:54:14.740
And I think that has been misguided.

01:54:14.740 --> 01:54:18.120
And I think that we're learning.

01:54:18.120 --> 01:54:20.100
about how that's been misguided

01:54:20.100 --> 01:54:22.300
through the study of these

01:54:22.300 --> 01:54:24.680
novel modalities from
Western perspectives,

01:54:24.680 --> 01:54:26.380
would of course they've
been used for a long,

01:54:26.380 --> 01:54:28.450
long time in other cultures,

01:54:28.450 --> 01:54:30.670
but novel from our perspective.

01:54:30.670 --> 01:54:33.015
- Yeah, I'm fascinated by this idea

01:54:33.015 --> 01:54:34.580
that in these middle brain structures is,

01:54:34.580 --> 01:54:36.960
is where our humanity lies

01:54:36.960 --> 01:54:37.900
and as you said,

01:54:37.900 --> 01:54:42.420
I also wonder whether or not
other animals experience life

01:54:42.420 --> 01:54:45.190
more from that orientation
with less chatter.

01:54:45.190 --> 01:54:47.338
We can only guess, but.

01:54:47.338 --> 01:54:48.171
- [Paul] right.

01:54:48.171 --> 01:54:49.950
You know, that a dog lover

01:54:51.821 --> 01:54:53.900
and being in the presence of
animals that seem to just be

01:54:53.900 --> 01:54:56.530
present in what's happening in
their immediate environment,

01:54:56.530 --> 01:54:57.825
not too much anticipation.

01:54:57.825 --> 01:54:59.697
- Right, I mean, through sentient right?

01:54:59.697 --> 01:55:00.530
I mean what you're talking about

01:55:00.530 --> 01:55:01.923
is sentience, it's important.

01:55:02.932 --> 01:55:04.490
And sentience is extremely
important, right?

01:55:04.490 --> 01:55:06.920
And if we're going to
overvalue say language,

01:55:06.920 --> 01:55:09.490
then I think we undervalue
sentience, right?

01:55:09.490 --> 01:55:13.230
Which is why I think we tend
to undervalue animals, right?

01:55:13.230 --> 01:55:14.370
And their suffering, we say,

01:55:14.370 --> 01:55:16.160
well, they're not saying
anything about it, right?

01:55:16.160 --> 01:55:18.370
And you know, they're not
writing about it, so, okay.

01:55:18.370 --> 01:55:20.840
It's easy to ignore and we think about,

01:55:20.840 --> 01:55:22.550
again the hubris of that right though,

01:55:22.550 --> 01:55:24.290
because we can think and talk and write,

01:55:24.290 --> 01:55:26.280
like we must be feeling more

01:55:26.280 --> 01:55:28.290
than species that don't do that.

01:55:28.290 --> 01:55:29.123
I mean I think,

01:55:29.123 --> 01:55:31.070
I think that that is so true

01:55:31.070 --> 01:55:33.680
and that we're going to
understand more about sentience

01:55:33.680 --> 01:55:35.560
in other species and how,

01:55:35.560 --> 01:55:37.933
that's at the core of existence.

01:55:39.653 --> 01:55:41.900
And my hope would be that we value

01:55:41.900 --> 01:55:43.750
more humans and animals, right?

01:55:43.750 --> 01:55:46.503
Through the evolution
of that understanding.

01:55:48.701 --> 01:55:51.670
- The hallucinations that
accompany psychedelics

01:55:52.670 --> 01:55:56.350
like LSD and psilocybin have
such an attractive force

01:55:56.350 --> 01:55:59.933
to them as a concept and as an experience.

01:56:01.592 --> 01:56:04.510
And so I think most often when
people hear hallucinogens,

01:56:04.510 --> 01:56:05.900
they think, and psychedelics,

01:56:05.900 --> 01:56:07.300
they think about hallucinating.

01:56:07.300 --> 01:56:08.215
- [Paul] Right.

01:56:08.215 --> 01:56:09.048
- It makes sense why they would.

01:56:09.048 --> 01:56:10.159
- [Paul] Right.

01:56:10.159 --> 01:56:12.470
- But what's so interesting to
me is nothing in your answer

01:56:12.470 --> 01:56:16.020
about psychedelics,
psilocybin and LSD focused

01:56:16.020 --> 01:56:17.850
on hallucinations, per se.

01:56:17.850 --> 01:56:19.290
It was more about feeling states,

01:56:19.290 --> 01:56:22.130
accessing a feeling state or a relation

01:56:22.130 --> 01:56:24.440
to an event or to a person or to oneself.

01:56:24.440 --> 01:56:25.273
Maybe even I,

01:56:25.273 --> 01:56:28.236
I caught hints of maybe
even empathy for one's self.

01:56:28.236 --> 01:56:29.069
- Yes.

01:56:29.069 --> 01:56:29.902
- [Andrew] For the first time.

01:56:29.902 --> 01:56:30.735
- Yes.

01:56:30.735 --> 01:56:34.080
- None of that had to
do with seeing sounds

01:56:34.080 --> 01:56:36.516
or hearing colors and these kind of cliche

01:56:36.516 --> 01:56:39.000
statements about hallucination.

01:56:39.000 --> 01:56:41.510
So I am aware of laboratories,

01:56:41.510 --> 01:56:44.070
one at a University of
California Davis in particular,

01:56:44.070 --> 01:56:45.190
but a few others that are trying

01:56:45.190 --> 01:56:48.110
to generate chemical variance

01:56:48.110 --> 01:56:52.920
of psychedelics that lack the
hallucinogenic properties,

01:56:52.920 --> 01:56:56.750
but maintain these other
properties as therapeutic tools.

01:56:56.750 --> 01:56:58.940
And as I say that, I realize that I,

01:56:58.940 --> 01:57:02.260
people in the psychedelic
community are probably thinking,

01:57:02.260 --> 01:57:03.093
oh, that's horrible.

01:57:03.093 --> 01:57:05.970
That's the dismantling of the core thing.

01:57:05.970 --> 01:57:07.620
But the simple question is,

01:57:07.620 --> 01:57:10.840
do you think the hallucinations
are valuable for anything?

01:57:10.840 --> 01:57:14.500
- And I think we're really
getting into the philosophical,

01:57:14.500 --> 01:57:15.910
right, the ontological, right?

01:57:15.910 --> 01:57:18.773
There's this sort of trying
to understand being, right?

01:57:20.267 --> 01:57:22.420
And I don't claim to
know the answer to that.

01:57:22.420 --> 01:57:26.730
I think that at times it
seems like the hallucinations

01:57:27.640 --> 01:57:30.193
have a metaphorical or a symbolic way

01:57:31.760 --> 01:57:32.860
of being helpful, right?

01:57:32.860 --> 01:57:36.030
Because people will come
to understand things

01:57:36.030 --> 01:57:41.030
that they hold dear and true
after the experience, right?

01:57:42.270 --> 01:57:45.814
That often, not always,
come through the lens

01:57:45.814 --> 01:57:46.930
of the hallucinations.

01:57:46.930 --> 01:57:50.240
So are the hallucinations necessary?

01:57:50.240 --> 01:57:53.776
Are those hallucinations
sometimes important sometimes not?

01:57:53.776 --> 01:57:55.870
I mean, I think we don't understand that.

01:57:55.870 --> 01:57:57.910
And I think we want to be respectful

01:57:57.910 --> 01:57:59.920
of the sort of mystery of that.

01:57:59.920 --> 01:58:02.980
But what I think is fascinating
is when you think about

01:58:02.980 --> 01:58:05.880
like substance abuse and
what that means is, well,

01:58:05.880 --> 01:58:08.580
one aspect of that is that
a person has experiences,

01:58:08.580 --> 01:58:10.700
thoughts, conceptions of self in the world

01:58:10.700 --> 01:58:12.950
with the substance that
without the substance,

01:58:12.950 --> 01:58:14.384
they know are wrong, right?

01:58:14.384 --> 01:58:16.247
People talk about, you
know, liquid courage, right?

01:58:16.247 --> 01:58:17.397
And okay, I feel better about myself

01:58:17.397 --> 01:58:19.410
and I feel courageous 'cause
I've had a couple of drinks.

01:58:19.410 --> 01:58:21.860
Now, when I, when I, after that,

01:58:21.860 --> 01:58:25.540
I feel like normal about myself
and that was false, right?

01:58:25.540 --> 01:58:26.373
And we see that like,

01:58:26.373 --> 01:58:29.720
that's part of what substance
intoxication means, right?

01:58:29.720 --> 01:58:33.470
But what we see with the
psychedelic medicines

01:58:33.470 --> 01:58:35.870
is something that's
incredibly different, right?

01:58:35.870 --> 01:58:39.290
That people are having
experiences that are so delinked

01:58:39.290 --> 01:58:42.150
from our normal experience of reality.

01:58:42.150 --> 01:58:45.110
And then when they come in a
sense back online with right,

01:58:45.110 --> 01:58:47.990
in a normal cognitive way,
they realize like, wow,

01:58:47.990 --> 01:58:49.780
now I'm applying all those mechanisms

01:58:49.780 --> 01:58:52.430
of trying to understand truth and to that,

01:58:52.430 --> 01:58:55.990
and what I see is that it's
true and wow, it's true.

01:58:55.990 --> 01:58:58.460
Like, I mean, we hear that
all the time, which tells me,

01:58:58.460 --> 01:59:01.120
hey, something different
is going on there.

01:59:01.120 --> 01:59:02.760
And of course these are powerful tools

01:59:02.760 --> 01:59:05.330
so misused like very
bad things can happen.

01:59:05.330 --> 01:59:07.870
But you think about the clinical
utility and what does it

01:59:07.870 --> 01:59:11.990
mean that so many people
change for the healthier

01:59:11.990 --> 01:59:15.520
or even change their
lives after an experience

01:59:15.520 --> 01:59:17.320
because it so resonates as like,

01:59:17.320 --> 01:59:19.200
oh, now I understand
something that's true.

01:59:19.200 --> 01:59:20.690
And it's not something bizarre.

01:59:20.690 --> 01:59:21.523
It's like,

01:59:21.523 --> 01:59:23.530
I wasn't responsible for
being raped that time

01:59:23.530 --> 01:59:27.973
or I'm not less than even
though my sexuality or my gender

01:59:27.973 --> 01:59:31.560
identity's different from some
silly binary concept, right?

01:59:31.560 --> 01:59:33.580
Like people kind of often
get it and they feel

01:59:33.580 --> 01:59:34.760
differently about themselves

01:59:34.760 --> 01:59:36.660
and guilt and shame are impacted.

01:59:36.660 --> 01:59:39.598
So I think we're likely to
see that they are powerful

01:59:39.598 --> 01:59:42.660
anti-trauma mechanisms again,

01:59:42.660 --> 01:59:44.593
used clinically in the right hands.

01:59:45.520 --> 01:59:47.640
And I think that we're also going
to see that they're heuristic

01:59:47.640 --> 01:59:50.770
for understanding our brain
that goes against what I see

01:59:50.770 --> 01:59:53.250
as some of the reflexive hubris of,

01:59:53.250 --> 01:59:55.761
well, the outer parts must be the best

01:59:55.761 --> 01:59:56.594
because that's what makes

01:59:56.594 --> 01:59:58.040
us human and other animals don't have it.

01:59:58.040 --> 01:59:59.300
And we're better because we're human.

01:59:59.300 --> 02:00:01.130
I mean, it makes no sense, you know?

02:00:01.130 --> 02:00:03.820
- I'd like to talk about MDMA

02:00:03.820 --> 02:00:05.090
and I'll preface this by saying

02:00:05.090 --> 02:00:06.790
I was a participant actually,

02:00:06.790 --> 02:00:09.630
technically I'm still a
participant in a clinical trial.

02:00:09.630 --> 02:00:12.390
So I have experience of doing it twice

02:00:13.934 --> 02:00:16.823
at the trial involves three
separate dosings of this.

02:00:18.260 --> 02:00:21.370
I was reluctant to do it
outside of a clinical trial,

02:00:21.370 --> 02:00:23.670
mostly because I was aware

02:00:23.670 --> 02:00:25.310
there can be some cardiac effects.

02:00:25.310 --> 02:00:27.410
And I liked the idea there
would be a clinician on hand.

02:00:27.410 --> 02:00:31.170
And I'll just say that
I found the experiences

02:00:31.170 --> 02:00:35.780
to be profound, beneficial
and very different

02:00:36.945 --> 02:00:39.110
from one session to the next.

02:00:39.110 --> 02:00:41.120
The first one felt

02:00:43.140 --> 02:00:44.540
a whole collection

02:00:44.540 --> 02:00:48.430
of ideas and relational
things came up that felt very

02:00:48.430 --> 02:00:49.770
powerful and transformative.

02:00:49.770 --> 02:00:51.900
And I do think that I learned there,

02:00:51.900 --> 02:00:53.870
I exported a number of things,

02:00:53.870 --> 02:00:56.090
my particular experience
isn't relevant here,

02:00:56.090 --> 02:00:59.010
but the second time I expected
it to be the same way.

02:00:59.010 --> 02:01:03.200
And it was very mellow and relaxing

02:01:04.427 --> 02:01:07.560
and was deeply tied to kind
of notions of acceptance.

02:01:07.560 --> 02:01:11.230
So there weren't all these
revelations and wow new insights.

02:01:11.230 --> 02:01:13.860
It was very much about sort of grounding

02:01:13.860 --> 02:01:16.530
into a kind of a calmer state.

02:01:16.530 --> 02:01:18.900
So I have the personal experience

02:01:18.900 --> 02:01:21.680
of benefiting from these in
ways that I think still benefit

02:01:21.680 --> 02:01:25.470
me and was very struck
by the power of MDMA.

02:01:25.470 --> 02:01:28.820
And my very crude understanding
of the pharmacology

02:01:28.820 --> 02:01:31.140
and the state that is being under MDMA

02:01:31.140 --> 02:01:33.550
is that it encourages or increases

02:01:33.550 --> 02:01:35.270
dopaminergic transmission,

02:01:35.270 --> 02:01:36.827
but also serotonergic transmission.

02:01:36.827 --> 02:01:37.660
- Yeah.

02:01:37.660 --> 02:01:38.600
- Which is to my knowledge,

02:01:38.600 --> 02:01:41.320
a kind of a rare state
for the brain to be in

02:01:41.320 --> 02:01:44.170
that typically it's more
of a seesaw dopaminergic

02:01:44.170 --> 02:01:46.170
drive towards external goals

02:01:46.170 --> 02:01:48.230
or more serotonergic drive towards

02:01:49.240 --> 02:01:52.470
more plasticity or comfort
with what one already has.

02:01:52.470 --> 02:01:55.020
And so with both those systems amplified,

02:01:55.020 --> 02:01:57.270
the only way I can describe
it subjectively is that it,

02:01:57.270 --> 02:01:58.610
everything sort of funneled back in,

02:01:58.610 --> 02:02:03.223
and it was almost like a
pursuit of inner landscape.

02:02:04.350 --> 02:02:07.380
And I can only imagine what it
would be like in the context

02:02:07.380 --> 02:02:11.620
of doing this with somebody
else also taking MDMA.

02:02:11.620 --> 02:02:13.640
I have no idea what that's like.

02:02:13.640 --> 02:02:16.240
That's my report of the experience.

02:02:16.240 --> 02:02:18.823
I know that the experience can vary.

02:02:18.823 --> 02:02:20.770
What are your thoughts about the chemistry

02:02:20.770 --> 02:02:22.920
and what sorts of states

02:02:22.920 --> 02:02:24.910
do you think MDMA is creating

02:02:26.150 --> 02:02:29.470
that can explain why it's
a useful therapeutic tool

02:02:29.470 --> 02:02:32.970
in some cases and what sorts
of cases those might be.

02:02:32.970 --> 02:02:34.053
- Sure, sure.

02:02:35.290 --> 02:02:37.730
To clarify, I think part
of what we're starting

02:02:38.606 --> 02:02:40.870
with is this is very different
than the psychedelics, right?

02:02:40.870 --> 02:02:43.410
Which are seeding our
consciousness in these deep centers

02:02:43.410 --> 02:02:44.520
of the brain, right?

02:02:44.520 --> 02:02:48.110
Whereas what MDMA is
doing is sort of flooding

02:02:48.110 --> 02:02:50.480
with positive neurotransmitters, right?

02:02:50.480 --> 02:02:51.820
In certain parts of the brain.

02:02:51.820 --> 02:02:55.460
And I think what that creates
is a greater permissiveness

02:02:55.460 --> 02:03:00.460
inside to entertain or approach
different things, right?

02:03:00.940 --> 02:03:03.770
So I think where we see it's tremend,

02:03:03.770 --> 02:03:06.250
my read of the data is around potentially

02:03:06.250 --> 02:03:07.940
and we're seeing in some
of the trials, right?

02:03:07.940 --> 02:03:10.860
Tremendous benefit for trauma, right?

02:03:10.860 --> 02:03:12.500
And you think about what we
were talking about earlier,

02:03:12.500 --> 02:03:14.850
how this reflexive, guilt, shame,

02:03:14.850 --> 02:03:17.023
hypervigilance, avoidance, right?

02:03:18.201 --> 02:03:19.034
And when these systems

02:03:19.034 --> 02:03:21.510
are flooded with these neurotransmitters,

02:03:21.510 --> 02:03:23.657
it's more permissive to sort
of think about that, right?

02:03:23.657 --> 02:03:25.580
And to think about that without again,

02:03:25.580 --> 02:03:27.850
all the chatter of that's your fault,

02:03:27.850 --> 02:03:30.078
or you're never going to get
anywhere because of that,

02:03:30.078 --> 02:03:31.490
or you know what that means, right?

02:03:31.490 --> 02:03:33.640
They could kind of go away
and then we can think about it

02:03:33.640 --> 02:03:36.800
in a way that isn't through
the lens of fear, right?

02:03:36.800 --> 02:03:38.140
And I think that's,

02:03:38.140 --> 02:03:40.236
the power there is that

02:03:40.236 --> 02:03:42.360
it's permissive of approaching something,

02:03:42.360 --> 02:03:43.823
contemplating something,

02:03:45.170 --> 02:03:48.600
a different, a novelty as we
talk about a de novo approach.

02:03:48.600 --> 02:03:51.010
And I think that's also why the experience

02:03:51.010 --> 02:03:54.060
can vary because you could also see how,

02:03:54.060 --> 02:03:56.650
if you're not thinking
about something, right?

02:03:56.650 --> 02:03:59.260
So there's not a clinical guidance to it,

02:03:59.260 --> 02:04:01.835
you could be in a state where like,

02:04:01.835 --> 02:04:02.668
hey, I just feel good, right?

02:04:02.668 --> 02:04:04.470
And I'm thinking about good things.

02:04:04.470 --> 02:04:06.155
And like, that can feel good, right?

02:04:06.155 --> 02:04:09.530
But it, but that's not necessarily
problem solving, right?

02:04:09.530 --> 02:04:11.240
So the clinical guidance says,

02:04:11.240 --> 02:04:14.860
hey, let's take that state and
do something with it, right?

02:04:14.860 --> 02:04:16.489
Let's Now that you're in this state,

02:04:16.489 --> 02:04:19.608
let's make hay while the
sun is shinning, right?

02:04:19.608 --> 02:04:21.130
You're in a state where we can look

02:04:21.130 --> 02:04:22.890
at things that are traumatic, right?

02:04:22.890 --> 02:04:25.800
We can approach them from
a de novo perspective.

02:04:25.800 --> 02:04:26.810
And I think it's part,

02:04:26.810 --> 02:04:28.540
I think that explains why
you had these different

02:04:28.540 --> 02:04:30.500
experiences from one to the other,

02:04:30.500 --> 02:04:32.060
because your brain is just in a state

02:04:32.060 --> 02:04:34.170
that's conducive to something, right?

02:04:34.170 --> 02:04:35.450
But if there's not

02:04:35.450 --> 02:04:37.700
the mechanism to have that thing happen,

02:04:37.700 --> 02:04:39.740
like conducive to something therapeutic,

02:04:39.740 --> 02:04:41.410
then you might go there on your own.

02:04:41.410 --> 02:04:43.260
Or you might just be in a state

02:04:43.260 --> 02:04:45.300
where you have a sense of wellbeing

02:04:45.300 --> 02:04:46.640
and you sit with that.

02:04:46.640 --> 02:04:48.350
- Which sort of seems like a waste to me.

02:04:48.350 --> 02:04:51.980
I mean, this is what I tell
people when they ask about MDMA.

02:04:51.980 --> 02:04:53.530
I said, at least from my experience

02:04:53.530 --> 02:04:57.140
that the potential hazard
there is that in that very high

02:04:57.140 --> 02:04:59.193
dopaminergic, serotonergic state,

02:05:00.250 --> 02:05:02.450
there were moments where I
felt like I could get excited

02:05:02.450 --> 02:05:06.250
about any one specific concept
that I might even just think

02:05:06.250 --> 02:05:08.130
about, for instance,

02:05:08.130 --> 02:05:09.800
water and how nourishing it is,

02:05:09.800 --> 02:05:12.300
and really just go down the path of water

02:05:12.300 --> 02:05:14.660
and the world and all
the water and you can,

02:05:14.660 --> 02:05:19.170
you're in a state that is
very prone to suggestion.

02:05:19.170 --> 02:05:20.003
- [Paul] Yes.

02:05:20.003 --> 02:05:20.900
- Internal suggestion.

02:05:20.900 --> 02:05:22.310
And so the guidance turned out,

02:05:22.310 --> 02:05:24.440
the guidance from the clinician
turned out to be immensely

02:05:24.440 --> 02:05:28.050
valuable in allowing me
to go into my own heads

02:05:28.050 --> 02:05:30.550
for bits of time, but
then also to resurface

02:05:30.550 --> 02:05:33.570
and share and exchange in a way to,

02:05:33.570 --> 02:05:35.570
I'm trying really get something out of it

02:05:35.570 --> 02:05:37.160
that was useful and that I could export

02:05:37.160 --> 02:05:38.430
because of course water is wonderful,

02:05:38.430 --> 02:05:39.870
but I'm not really interested in growing

02:05:39.870 --> 02:05:41.690
my relationship to water.

02:05:41.690 --> 02:05:42.570
- [Paul] Yes.

02:05:42.570 --> 02:05:44.610
And I really felt like in,
I could understand for the,

02:05:44.610 --> 02:05:46.650
I never went to raves
or anything growing up.

02:05:46.650 --> 02:05:48.583
I never did MDMA recreationally,

02:05:49.549 --> 02:05:50.440
but I understood for the first time

02:05:50.440 --> 02:05:51.700
how people could get really

02:05:51.700 --> 02:05:54.170
attached to an environment
and feel connected to things.

02:05:54.170 --> 02:05:56.020
Because I think with all that serotonin,

02:05:56.020 --> 02:05:58.500
you just feel connected
to everything around you.

02:05:58.500 --> 02:06:01.015
So I think it's a slippery slope there.

02:06:01.015 --> 02:06:01.848
- Yes.

02:06:01.848 --> 02:06:03.030
- And I don't know what the future

02:06:03.030 --> 02:06:05.610
of the clinical use of MDMA looks like,

02:06:05.610 --> 02:06:07.460
but I would hope that whoever's thinking

02:06:07.460 --> 02:06:09.820
about I'm guiding these sessions

02:06:09.820 --> 02:06:11.150
is really thinking carefully,

02:06:11.150 --> 02:06:12.710
also about evolving the practice

02:06:12.710 --> 02:06:15.590
to help people really move through

02:06:15.590 --> 02:06:16.423
in a sequential way

02:06:16.423 --> 02:06:18.900
so they can leave with something valuable.

02:06:18.900 --> 02:06:21.310
- Yes, 100%, 100%.

02:06:21.310 --> 02:06:23.710
These are such powerful tools.

02:06:23.710 --> 02:06:25.170
And again, if they're powerful tools

02:06:25.170 --> 02:06:29.760
and we're using them without
respect for them, right?

02:06:29.760 --> 02:06:32.330
Without clinical guidance,
we incur risk, right?

02:06:32.330 --> 02:06:33.180
I mean, you know,

02:06:33.180 --> 02:06:35.840
getting obsessed with water
while it probably isn't going to

02:06:35.840 --> 02:06:36.940
hurt you, right?

02:06:36.940 --> 02:06:40.770
But if someone is out using it,
they're around other people,

02:06:40.770 --> 02:06:43.050
what one can feel positively about

02:06:43.050 --> 02:06:44.920
or become sort of obsessed
in the short term about

02:06:44.920 --> 02:06:46.310
can be very counterproductive, right?

02:06:46.310 --> 02:06:47.590
It can be a lot of risk to that.

02:06:47.590 --> 02:06:52.370
So I think it anchors back to
these are very powerful tools.

02:06:52.370 --> 02:06:54.900
We're coming to understand
them much, much more.

02:06:54.900 --> 02:06:57.010
And we're coming to understand
that they have immense

02:06:57.010 --> 02:06:59.250
potential to be helpful to us.

02:06:59.250 --> 02:07:03.220
But I think and hope that
that only also increases

02:07:03.220 --> 02:07:08.220
our respect for those
modalities and what can come,

02:07:08.430 --> 02:07:11.860
what negative can happen
if we're not respectful.

02:07:11.860 --> 02:07:13.060
- It's going to be very interesting

02:07:13.060 --> 02:07:14.610
to see where all of this goes

02:07:14.610 --> 02:07:17.022
in the next few years, not
just in Oregon, but elsewhere.

02:07:17.022 --> 02:07:19.040
One way or another it's happening.

02:07:19.040 --> 02:07:22.180
It seems to have a momentum
that is not going to stop.

02:07:22.180 --> 02:07:25.700
So very exciting area to be sure.

02:07:25.700 --> 02:07:27.170
- I agree.

02:07:27.170 --> 02:07:29.670
- I have a question about language.

02:07:29.670 --> 02:07:30.503
In your book,

02:07:30.503 --> 02:07:32.820
you talk about how we need to be careful

02:07:32.820 --> 02:07:34.708
about the use of language around trauma.

02:07:34.708 --> 02:07:35.541
- [Paul] Yes.

02:07:35.541 --> 02:07:38.713
- Maybe problem solving and
problem describing in general.

02:07:39.939 --> 02:07:40.940
At one extreme,

02:07:40.940 --> 02:07:44.230
you hear that your
brain and your body hear

02:07:44.230 --> 02:07:46.850
every word you say, and, you know,

02:07:46.850 --> 02:07:48.963
we have to be so careful with language.

02:07:49.880 --> 02:07:51.810
And that actually frightened
me for a number of years,

02:07:51.810 --> 02:07:53.080
'cause I would hear that
and I thought, gosh,

02:07:53.080 --> 02:07:54.740
if I just think that something is bad

02:07:54.740 --> 02:07:56.200
now it's going to hurt me worse,

02:07:56.200 --> 02:07:58.090
which itself is part of that whole,

02:07:58.090 --> 02:08:00.910
packing down of an issue.

02:08:00.910 --> 02:08:04.940
Very hard to avoid thoughts
without distraction.

02:08:04.940 --> 02:08:05.941
- [Paul] Yes.

02:08:05.941 --> 02:08:09.020
- So that's one extreme,
on the other hand,

02:08:09.020 --> 02:08:12.790
I can say, I can tell somebody,

02:08:12.790 --> 02:08:15.870
I love them with a tone of hatred.

02:08:15.870 --> 02:08:18.230
I can tell somebody I hate
them with a tone of love.

02:08:18.230 --> 02:08:19.063
- [Paul] Yes.

02:08:19.964 --> 02:08:23.430
- So how should we think about
language in parsing trauma?

02:08:23.430 --> 02:08:25.290
And in your book you talk about,

02:08:25.290 --> 02:08:26.640
you give some cautionary notes

02:08:26.640 --> 02:08:29.197
about talking about depression, trauma

02:08:29.197 --> 02:08:32.910
and PTSD in terms that that might diminish

02:08:32.910 --> 02:08:36.893
their real severity in some cases.

02:08:37.910 --> 02:08:39.370
And I was really struck by that.

02:08:39.370 --> 02:08:40.770
So maybe just touch on

02:08:40.770 --> 02:08:44.450
how should we talk about these
things in a way that doesn't

02:08:44.450 --> 02:08:46.923
diminish them for ourselves
or for other people.

02:08:48.162 --> 02:08:49.460
And at the same time honors the fact

02:08:49.460 --> 02:08:51.113
that there's a lot of trauma out there.

02:08:51.113 --> 02:08:52.452
- [Paul] Right

02:08:52.452 --> 02:08:53.810
- And there's a lot of
depression out there

02:08:53.810 --> 02:08:55.740
and we need to talk about it.

02:08:55.740 --> 02:08:56.573
- Yeah.

02:08:56.573 --> 02:08:58.760
I think this a very complicated

02:08:58.760 --> 02:09:00.560
and in many ways convoluted topic,

02:09:00.560 --> 02:09:03.070
like I think it's wonderful
that we have language,

02:09:03.070 --> 02:09:06.840
but boy, language leads
us astray often too.

02:09:06.840 --> 02:09:08.060
You think about how we,

02:09:08.060 --> 02:09:09.749
how people define words?

02:09:09.749 --> 02:09:10.920
Someone says a word, what is it?

02:09:10.920 --> 02:09:13.500
Does that person know
what that word means?

02:09:13.500 --> 02:09:15.280
What nuance are they taking from? it

02:09:15.280 --> 02:09:18.730
We just have to be very
careful what we're saying

02:09:18.730 --> 02:09:20.400
and what we're communicating.

02:09:20.400 --> 02:09:22.580
And I think this doesn't mean because,

02:09:22.580 --> 02:09:25.140
there's a sort of phenomenon now

02:09:25.140 --> 02:09:27.190
where people are trying
to control language

02:09:27.190 --> 02:09:28.023
I think too much,

02:09:28.023 --> 02:09:30.120
like you can't say anything
that someone else might find

02:09:30.120 --> 02:09:32.280
hurtful or you have to refer to people

02:09:32.280 --> 02:09:34.150
in ways they choose to be referred to,

02:09:34.150 --> 02:09:37.250
even if those are ways that
others don't understand,

02:09:37.250 --> 02:09:41.280
or ways they themselves have
decided or ways that might be

02:09:41.280 --> 02:09:43.800
psychologically or clinically unhelpful.

02:09:43.800 --> 02:09:47.800
So I think the over control
of language is not good,

02:09:47.800 --> 02:09:49.930
but I think the specificity of language

02:09:49.930 --> 02:09:52.710
of what are we trying to
say, how are we defining it?

02:09:52.710 --> 02:09:53.543
Or even the word trauma, right?

02:09:53.543 --> 02:09:54.780
We're talking about trauma.

02:09:54.780 --> 02:09:56.710
So we want to define
what that means, right?

02:09:56.710 --> 02:09:57.920
It doesn't just mean like,

02:09:57.920 --> 02:09:59.660
oh, anything kind of negative, right?

02:09:59.660 --> 02:10:02.640
Because then that dilutes it
down to meaning nothing, right?

02:10:02.640 --> 02:10:04.853
It also doesn't just mean,

02:10:06.330 --> 02:10:07.730
injury and combat, right?

02:10:07.730 --> 02:10:09.500
Like we have to talk about what that is.

02:10:09.500 --> 02:10:11.790
So I think anchoring it
to something that rises

02:10:11.790 --> 02:10:13.960
to the magnitude of
overwhelming our coping skills

02:10:13.960 --> 02:10:15.050
and changing us,

02:10:15.050 --> 02:10:17.430
like then at least I define
it that way and I can

02:10:17.430 --> 02:10:20.160
communicate that to you
and we can understand

02:10:20.160 --> 02:10:21.670
what we're talking about, right?

02:10:21.670 --> 02:10:24.580
I think that another aspect
of language, while again,

02:10:24.580 --> 02:10:28.630
we need this middle ground and
I don't think that it is okay

02:10:28.630 --> 02:10:32.510
for the over control of language
to shut down expression,

02:10:32.510 --> 02:10:35.260
but we also have to acknowledge,

02:10:35.260 --> 02:10:38.050
how we're so much less
distanced from each other

02:10:38.050 --> 02:10:39.300
through social media.

02:10:39.300 --> 02:10:41.150
And I think social media can do very,

02:10:41.150 --> 02:10:44.000
very good things as hopefully
we're doing now, right?

02:10:44.000 --> 02:10:47.120
But it can also be used to harm people

02:10:47.120 --> 02:10:48.320
from a distance, right?

02:10:48.320 --> 02:10:52.030
And how much hatefulness
is there out there

02:10:52.030 --> 02:10:53.280
that I think comes from anger

02:10:53.280 --> 02:10:54.580
and frustration in people,

02:10:54.580 --> 02:10:56.010
back to trauma, right?

02:10:56.010 --> 02:10:57.620
Where people just want to be angry

02:10:57.620 --> 02:11:00.140
and it's not really issues
that they're talking about,

02:11:00.140 --> 02:11:02.280
but then there's a target of that anger

02:11:04.177 --> 02:11:06.978
and people feel beleaguered by that.

02:11:06.978 --> 02:11:09.270
And the words that people use sometimes

02:11:09.270 --> 02:11:11.890
are so awful that someone reading that,

02:11:11.890 --> 02:11:13.270
like if you're in the demographic

02:11:13.270 --> 02:11:14.990
that's being targeted, right?

02:11:14.990 --> 02:11:16.020
And you're reading that, I mean,

02:11:16.020 --> 02:11:20.220
how does a person not feel, not feel,

02:11:20.220 --> 02:11:22.310
be set upon vulnerable, right?

02:11:22.310 --> 02:11:24.170
And then I think that also fuels,

02:11:24.170 --> 02:11:26.040
things like we just had this

02:11:26.040 --> 02:11:27.640
terrible shooting in Buffalo, right?

02:11:27.640 --> 02:11:29.820
Like just hate motivated, right?

02:11:29.820 --> 02:11:31.130
And I think that,

02:11:31.130 --> 02:11:33.610
because that kind of language
becomes very real to people

02:11:33.610 --> 02:11:34.530
who may take it in,

02:11:34.530 --> 02:11:37.690
it fuels their hate and then
they do something to enact it,

02:11:37.690 --> 02:11:39.600
which of course creates greater feel

02:11:39.600 --> 02:11:41.390
and fear and vulnerability.

02:11:41.390 --> 02:11:44.760
And I think there was
some civility and decorum

02:11:44.760 --> 02:11:47.810
that was in our world,
not that long ago, right?

02:11:47.810 --> 02:11:49.430
I mean, you know, I'm in my early fifties,

02:11:49.430 --> 02:11:50.830
I'm not that old, right?

02:11:50.830 --> 02:11:52.330
But I remember a time

02:11:52.330 --> 02:11:54.160
when in political discourse,

02:11:54.160 --> 02:11:56.960
say people were civil
to one another, right?

02:11:56.960 --> 02:12:00.080
Now so much, I mean, it's
not all of it, right?

02:12:00.080 --> 02:12:01.560
But there's an acceptance of things

02:12:01.560 --> 02:12:03.670
that are just bombastic, right?

02:12:03.670 --> 02:12:06.771
It's a circus side show sometimes

02:12:06.771 --> 02:12:10.290
of people being just angry and aggressive

02:12:10.290 --> 02:12:11.880
and it's not really linked to anything,

02:12:11.880 --> 02:12:13.430
although it's allegedly
linked to something,

02:12:13.430 --> 02:12:15.910
but then other people's
anger can attach to it.

02:12:15.910 --> 02:12:17.260
And it's not about what it's about,

02:12:17.260 --> 02:12:20.107
but it's about aligning with the anger

02:12:20.107 --> 02:12:24.390
and I think that there is so much damage

02:12:24.390 --> 02:12:25.370
that comes from that.

02:12:25.370 --> 02:12:28.240
And I think, should we have,

02:12:28.240 --> 02:12:31.970
should it be okay that
people sometimes are talking,

02:12:31.970 --> 02:12:34.110
communicating, using language in ways

02:12:34.110 --> 02:12:37.340
that would like get us suspended
from middle school, right?

02:12:37.340 --> 02:12:39.700
Ways I don't want my
eight year old to see,

02:12:39.700 --> 02:12:41.770
I mean, is that really okay?

02:12:41.770 --> 02:12:43.650
Or do we need to take a stand

02:12:43.650 --> 02:12:45.150
for like rational use of language?

02:12:45.150 --> 02:12:47.870
I don't want my use of
language to be over controlled

02:12:47.870 --> 02:12:49.710
by someone who thinks
they sort of understand

02:12:49.710 --> 02:12:52.490
better than the rest of us
how to communicate with us.

02:12:52.490 --> 02:12:53.360
Okay, I don't want that.

02:12:53.360 --> 02:12:54.450
What's stereotypically

02:12:54.450 --> 02:12:57.530
a sort of idea of the left say, right?

02:12:57.530 --> 02:13:01.540
At least in our society,
but I also don't want,

02:13:01.540 --> 02:13:03.670
language, it can be so angry

02:13:03.670 --> 02:13:07.560
and so aggressive that it is perpetuating

02:13:07.560 --> 02:13:12.030
or spreading vulnerability and
that it facilitates trauma.

02:13:12.030 --> 02:13:15.280
And I think we could set
standards as a society

02:13:15.280 --> 02:13:18.060
where we say, look, I
don't want anybody in power

02:13:18.060 --> 02:13:19.600
who's going to behave that way, right?

02:13:19.600 --> 02:13:21.380
I don't care if their
whole agenda is like,

02:13:21.380 --> 02:13:22.990
make Paul Conti's life better.

02:13:22.990 --> 02:13:25.070
I'm still not going to
vote for you, right?

02:13:25.070 --> 02:13:26.790
If you're behaving towards others

02:13:26.790 --> 02:13:28.090
in a way that's denigrating,

02:13:28.090 --> 02:13:30.110
you're behaving in a way that I feel

02:13:30.110 --> 02:13:31.980
essentially ashamed of, right?

02:13:31.980 --> 02:13:33.150
And I feel that a lot, right?

02:13:33.150 --> 02:13:36.200
I see the politics, you
know, I see things play out.

02:13:36.200 --> 02:13:38.590
It's not always political, of
course not always political,

02:13:38.590 --> 02:13:40.840
but I see things play out
and I think, oh my gosh,

02:13:40.840 --> 02:13:41.800
I feel embarrassed.

02:13:41.800 --> 02:13:43.580
Like we we're somehow okay with this.

02:13:43.580 --> 02:13:45.731
Well, it doesn't matter which side

02:13:45.731 --> 02:13:47.440
of the political spectrum it's coming to.

02:13:47.440 --> 02:13:51.060
And I think that's an
indicator that what we're doing

02:13:51.060 --> 02:13:54.070
is really hurtful to us.

02:13:54.070 --> 02:13:55.450
People become more angry.

02:13:55.450 --> 02:13:57.620
They attach to the anger.

02:13:57.620 --> 02:13:59.070
People feel more beleaguered.

02:13:59.070 --> 02:14:00.570
There's more divisions between us.

02:14:00.570 --> 02:14:01.810
And it seems more and more like,

02:14:01.810 --> 02:14:04.280
well, we can only really
identify with people

02:14:04.280 --> 02:14:05.300
who are just like us

02:14:05.300 --> 02:14:06.350
and like, what does that really mean?

02:14:06.350 --> 02:14:09.340
I mean, the divisions
that it creates between us

02:14:09.340 --> 02:14:13.610
and that promotes so many
negative things, right?

02:14:13.610 --> 02:14:14.844
I mean, think about ways
in which it promotes

02:14:14.844 --> 02:14:16.602
white supremacy, right?

02:14:16.602 --> 02:14:17.740
It's just one example, right?

02:14:17.740 --> 02:14:22.575
And we've seen that play out
that this is really bad for us.

02:14:22.575 --> 02:14:23.443
And we've got to look at that.

02:14:23.443 --> 02:14:26.230
I mean, if we don't look at that,

02:14:26.230 --> 02:14:28.160
I don't think something
is going to happen.

02:14:28.160 --> 02:14:29.700
Like something is happening, right?

02:14:29.700 --> 02:14:31.040
It's happening now.

02:14:31.040 --> 02:14:33.820
- Yeah, and I'm, it really, to my mind,

02:14:33.820 --> 02:14:35.820
it really seeps down into the soil

02:14:35.820 --> 02:14:37.007
of everything that we're talking about.

02:14:37.007 --> 02:14:38.090
- Yes.

02:14:38.090 --> 02:14:39.170
- On all sides.

02:14:39.170 --> 02:14:40.003
- [Paul] Yes.

02:14:40.003 --> 02:14:41.330
- People are activated.

02:14:41.330 --> 02:14:45.380
People are upset about
one thing or the other.

02:14:45.380 --> 02:14:46.213
- Right.

02:14:46.213 --> 02:14:47.890
- No one is immune from upset

02:14:47.890 --> 02:14:50.014
regardless of political affiliation.

02:14:50.014 --> 02:14:50.847
- [Paul] Right.

02:14:50.847 --> 02:14:52.938
- Everybody seems to be upset nowadays.

02:14:52.938 --> 02:14:53.850
- [Paul] Right.

02:14:53.850 --> 02:14:56.098
- As I was hearing you talk about this,

02:14:56.098 --> 02:14:58.800
I feel a lot of resonance
with what you said

02:14:58.800 --> 02:15:01.263
and I also am hoping you run for office.

02:15:02.385 --> 02:15:03.771
- Thank you.

02:15:03.771 --> 02:15:05.630
I don't think I have the gumption for that

02:15:05.630 --> 02:15:06.463
but thank you for.

02:15:06.463 --> 02:15:08.050
- [Andrew] Well, that would be wonderful.

02:15:08.050 --> 02:15:09.200
- Thank you.

02:15:09.200 --> 02:15:11.570
I'd like to talk about a concept

02:15:12.524 --> 02:15:14.020
of taking care of one's self.

02:15:14.020 --> 02:15:15.690
This comes up in the book.

02:15:15.690 --> 02:15:16.637
- [Paul] Yes.

02:15:16.637 --> 02:15:18.250
- This is something we talk
a lot about on this podcast.

02:15:18.250 --> 02:15:20.370
I mean, I think people have
heard me blab endlessly,

02:15:20.370 --> 02:15:22.820
and I'll probably go into the grave

02:15:22.820 --> 02:15:25.130
telling people to get sunlight
in their eyes when they can

02:15:25.130 --> 02:15:28.480
and to try and get proper
sleep and to have a few tools

02:15:30.440 --> 02:15:32.300
for reducing their anxiety in real time

02:15:32.300 --> 02:15:33.542
and on and on and on.

02:15:33.542 --> 02:15:35.150
- [Paul] Right.

02:15:35.150 --> 02:15:38.010
- We hear about this concept
of taking care of oneself

02:15:38.010 --> 02:15:41.300
and I think at a surface level,

02:15:41.300 --> 02:15:44.460
it can sound a little bit
light, you know, oh, take care,

02:15:44.460 --> 02:15:46.123
take care, take good care.

02:15:47.600 --> 02:15:49.940
But to me it's a deep
and powerful concept.

02:15:49.940 --> 02:15:50.773
- [Paul] Yes.

02:15:50.773 --> 02:15:53.000
- And I was very happy
to see it in your book

02:15:53.000 --> 02:15:56.440
and also to learn a lot of ideas

02:15:56.440 --> 02:15:58.853
about what that really looks like,

02:15:59.780 --> 02:16:02.080
because whether or not
somebody is in the early stages

02:16:02.080 --> 02:16:04.270
of considering whether
or not they have trauma

02:16:04.270 --> 02:16:05.820
or is in the deep stages of working

02:16:05.820 --> 02:16:08.160
that through or has made
it through the tunnel

02:16:08.160 --> 02:16:11.020
some distance taking care of oneself

02:16:11.020 --> 02:16:12.640
is an ongoing process.

02:16:12.640 --> 02:16:13.473
- [Paul] Yes.

02:16:15.020 --> 02:16:17.020
- I'd love for you to just describe

02:16:17.020 --> 02:16:20.763
what taking care of oneself
means to you as a clinician.

02:16:22.413 --> 02:16:23.720
And of course the practices and things

02:16:23.720 --> 02:16:25.453
that you encourage people to do.

02:16:26.456 --> 02:16:28.320
But how should we think about taking care

02:16:28.320 --> 02:16:30.230
of oneself because on one extreme,

02:16:30.230 --> 02:16:33.300
you could imagine massages
or treats vacations

02:16:35.228 --> 02:16:37.560
and chefs for hire that
take care of everything

02:16:37.560 --> 02:16:39.410
for ourselves.

02:16:39.410 --> 02:16:41.493
And on the other extreme, you could say,

02:16:42.800 --> 02:16:45.650
leaning into life in a way
that you're paying attention

02:16:45.650 --> 02:16:48.213
to small things while
working very, very hard.

02:16:49.310 --> 02:16:51.690
So it's such a big concept,

02:16:51.690 --> 02:16:53.470
how do you think about
taking care of one's self?

02:16:53.470 --> 02:16:55.095
How should I take care of myself?

02:16:55.095 --> 02:16:55.928
- [Paul] Sure.

02:16:55.928 --> 02:16:56.960
- How should people
take care of themselves?

02:16:56.960 --> 02:16:57.880
- Sure.

02:16:57.880 --> 02:16:59.820
- I see here, what I think

02:16:59.820 --> 02:17:01.960
is a very fascinating dichotomy, right?

02:17:01.960 --> 02:17:03.300
That in some ways, like,

02:17:03.300 --> 02:17:05.680
think about how complex
our brains are, right?

02:17:05.680 --> 02:17:08.380
How complex our psyches,
our unconscious minds are.

02:17:08.380 --> 02:17:12.880
There's so much complexity
there, but on the other hand,

02:17:12.880 --> 02:17:15.200
psychological concepts that are consistent

02:17:15.200 --> 02:17:18.900
with health are often very simple, right?

02:17:18.900 --> 02:17:20.903
Which I don't mean light, right?

02:17:22.313 --> 02:17:23.960
But simple, straightforward, right?

02:17:23.960 --> 02:17:26.590
And I think self-care is
absolutely one of them.

02:17:26.590 --> 02:17:27.980
I mean, how much is talked about,

02:17:27.980 --> 02:17:29.500
how to take care of one self

02:17:29.500 --> 02:17:31.360
that just skips over the basics

02:17:31.360 --> 02:17:33.730
that are necessary as a
building block for all else.

02:17:33.730 --> 02:17:36.473
So it doesn't matter how many
chefs or vacations or whatever

02:17:36.473 --> 02:17:41.090
a person has if the basics of
self-care aren't squared away.

02:17:41.090 --> 02:17:43.767
And it's not a light concept to say like,

02:17:43.767 --> 02:17:46.340
are you sleeping enough, right?

02:17:46.340 --> 02:17:48.421
Are you eating well?

02:17:48.421 --> 02:17:49.510
Are you getting natural light?

02:17:49.510 --> 02:17:51.070
Are you interacting with people

02:17:51.070 --> 02:17:53.010
who are good to interact with, right?

02:17:53.010 --> 02:17:56.450
Are you accepting negative
interactions in your life?

02:17:56.450 --> 02:17:57.910
Are you living in circumstances

02:17:57.910 --> 02:18:00.430
that make you feel okay or not?

02:18:00.430 --> 02:18:03.950
They're very basic premises,

02:18:03.950 --> 02:18:07.760
but so often we're not
looking at them at all, right?

02:18:07.760 --> 02:18:11.010
We're not looking at them at
all because we tend to skip

02:18:11.010 --> 02:18:14.220
over them and we tend to
skip over them either,

02:18:14.220 --> 02:18:16.360
because, again, in some automatic way

02:18:16.360 --> 02:18:18.130
that sometimes is trauma driven

02:18:18.130 --> 02:18:20.170
or we're not going to look at that, right?

02:18:20.170 --> 02:18:21.940
And often not taking care of ourselves

02:18:21.940 --> 02:18:24.480
can have the punishment
distraction, right?

02:18:24.480 --> 02:18:27.030
There's so much that can come into that.

02:18:27.030 --> 02:18:29.450
Or our sense of power is,

02:18:29.450 --> 02:18:31.440
is tied to not taking care of ourselves.

02:18:31.440 --> 02:18:34.140
I mean, I'll give you
an example is I tend to,

02:18:34.140 --> 02:18:36.140
for whatever reason do reasonably well

02:18:37.220 --> 02:18:39.280
with very poor self care, right?

02:18:39.280 --> 02:18:41.290
And like, that was very adaptive

02:18:41.290 --> 02:18:43.639
when I was into medical training, right?

02:18:43.639 --> 02:18:45.620
And I'm like, okay, I can eat a lot today.

02:18:45.620 --> 02:18:46.560
I can not eat, right?

02:18:46.560 --> 02:18:47.770
I can sleep two hours.

02:18:47.770 --> 02:18:49.850
I can sleep eight, right?

02:18:49.850 --> 02:18:51.730
I mean, overall, that's not good.

02:18:51.730 --> 02:18:54.340
And it hasn't been good
for me as I've aged.

02:18:54.340 --> 02:18:55.980
But then I realized some look,

02:18:55.980 --> 02:18:58.060
I'm doing all these things
to make myself healthier,

02:18:58.060 --> 02:19:00.250
but like what, I ignored that, right?

02:19:00.250 --> 02:19:01.083
And why am I ignoring it?

02:19:01.083 --> 02:19:01.916
That was a key question.

02:19:01.916 --> 02:19:03.140
Why am I ignoring it?

02:19:03.140 --> 02:19:05.210
Because somewhere inside of me, as it was,

02:19:05.210 --> 02:19:07.260
and still to some extent is,

02:19:07.260 --> 02:19:10.440
this idea that my ability to
be really functional, right?

02:19:10.440 --> 02:19:13.020
To generate success in the world around me

02:19:13.020 --> 02:19:15.640
is tied to my ability to do that, right?

02:19:15.640 --> 02:19:18.100
That, oh, but if I stop
doing that and now I'm like,

02:19:18.100 --> 02:19:19.660
I'm eating and sleeping regularly,

02:19:19.660 --> 02:19:21.470
then I'm going to lose some edge.

02:19:21.470 --> 02:19:23.700
So even I think about this all the time,

02:19:23.700 --> 02:19:25.020
but I realize,

02:19:25.020 --> 02:19:27.470
hey, I'm also, I'm not
doing it inside, you know?

02:19:28.315 --> 02:19:30.240
And I think it's really grounding

02:19:30.240 --> 02:19:33.480
to the basics that really help us of like,

02:19:33.480 --> 02:19:35.220
what are the basics of what I'm doing

02:19:35.220 --> 02:19:37.340
and not doing in my life, diet, exercise,

02:19:37.340 --> 02:19:39.950
sleep, people, circumstances,

02:19:39.950 --> 02:19:40.783
leisure activities.

02:19:40.783 --> 02:19:42.333
I mean, sunlight.

02:19:43.651 --> 02:19:47.160
I think immensely important
and dramatically undervalued.

02:19:47.160 --> 02:19:48.450
- Well, I want to thank you for that.

02:19:48.450 --> 02:19:51.540
And I want to thank you
for today's discussion.

02:19:51.540 --> 02:19:54.280
I found it to be incredibly informative

02:19:54.280 --> 02:19:56.553
and I know our listeners will also.

02:19:56.553 --> 02:19:58.650
I also want to thank
you for the work you do.

02:19:58.650 --> 02:20:01.340
I mean, you obviously run an incredibly

02:20:01.340 --> 02:20:02.950
robust clinical practice

02:20:02.950 --> 02:20:05.680
that I'm aware that you're
constantly trying to improve,

02:20:05.680 --> 02:20:08.510
even though it's operating at
the highest levels already.

02:20:08.510 --> 02:20:09.720
- I appreciate that.

02:20:09.720 --> 02:20:12.690
- I really, the reason
why you're here today

02:20:12.690 --> 02:20:15.510
is because I've done
a wide and deep search

02:20:15.510 --> 02:20:19.315
for people in these areas.

02:20:19.315 --> 02:20:23.230
And there are so few who have
the background in medical

02:20:23.230 --> 02:20:24.940
training and physiology,

02:20:24.940 --> 02:20:28.210
in the psychoanalytic
and psychiatric realm

02:20:28.210 --> 02:20:32.200
and also have a grounding
toward the future,

02:20:32.200 --> 02:20:35.110
of what's coming and who can encapsulate

02:20:35.110 --> 02:20:36.890
so many different orientations

02:20:36.890 --> 02:20:39.570
and bring them together
into a coherent piece.

02:20:39.570 --> 02:20:41.668
So I really thank you.

02:20:41.668 --> 02:20:42.640
- I so appreciate that.

02:20:42.640 --> 02:20:45.600
- Yeah, and for your
book, which is incredible,

02:20:45.600 --> 02:20:48.260
I will go on record saying,

02:20:48.260 --> 02:20:50.990
I think this is the
definitive book on trauma.

02:20:50.990 --> 02:20:51.823
- Wow, thank you.

02:20:51.823 --> 02:20:52.700
- And I really encourage people

02:20:52.700 --> 02:20:55.690
to read it and will continue
to encourage people to read it.

02:20:55.690 --> 02:20:57.700
It has so many valuable takeaways

02:20:57.700 --> 02:20:59.820
and insights and tools there.

02:20:59.820 --> 02:21:03.360
So on behalf of the listeners and myself,

02:21:03.360 --> 02:21:05.970
thank you so much for joining us today.

02:21:05.970 --> 02:21:06.870
- You're very welcome.

02:21:06.870 --> 02:21:09.870
And I take that to heart
and I'm very appreciative

02:21:09.870 --> 02:21:11.350
of being here, so you're very welcome

02:21:11.350 --> 02:21:12.271
and thank you as well.

02:21:12.271 --> 02:21:13.770
- Thank you.

